window.HU_TECH={"sym": "Symmetry and substitution", "tel": "Telescoping", "two": "Counting two ways", "inv": "Invariants", "pig": "Pigeonhole", "mod": "Modular arithmetic", "vie": "Vieta and symmetric functions", "trg": "Trigonometric identities and bounds", "arint": "Integrals as areas or by symmetry", "fint": "Integral reasoning", "root": "Root counting via monotonicity", "ext": "Extremal principle", "col": "Colouring and parity", "game": "Games and losing positions", "log": "Quantifiers, negation and vacuous truth", "prf": "Proof analysis", "ns": "Necessary and sufficient", "ineq": "Inequalities", "circ": "Circle geometry", "ser": "Series and sequences", "poly": "Polynomials", "cnt": "Counting arguments", "exp": "Exponentials and logarithms", "trans": "Graph transformations", "bin": "Binomial expansion", "diff": "Differentiation, tangents and stationary points", "coord": "Coordinate geometry", "quad": "Quadratics, discriminants and parameters"};
window.HU_PAPERS={"J1": "Set J Paper 1", "J2": "Set J Paper 2", "B1": "Set B Paper 1", "B2": "Set B Paper 2", "C1": "Set C Paper 1", "C2": "Set C Paper 2", "D1": "Set D Paper 1", "D2": "Set D Paper 2", "E1": "Set E Paper 1", "E2": "Set E Paper 2", "F1": "Set F Paper 1", "F2": "Set F Paper 2", "G1": "Set G Paper 1", "G2": "Set G Paper 2", "H1": "Set H Paper 1", "H2": "Set H Paper 2", "I1": "Set I Paper 1", "I2": "Set I Paper 2"};
window.HU_QUESTIONS=[{"id": "C2-7", "paper": "C2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>A student is asked to prove: <em>for every integer $n$, if $n^3$ is odd then $n$ is odd.<\/em> The student writes:<\/p><p><br> &ldquo;Let $n$ be odd, so $n=2k+1$ for some integer $k$. Then $n^3=8k^3+12k^2+6k+1=2(4k^3+6k^2+3k)+1$, which is odd. This proves the statement.&rdquo;<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The argument proves the converse. The statement is true, but this is not a proof of it.", "The argument is valid and proves the statement.", "The argument proves the contrapositive of the statement, so it is valid.", "The argument contains an algebraic error.", "The argument proves the converse, and the statement is false."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The student assumes $n$ odd and deduces $n^3$ odd, which is the converse: if $n$ is odd then $n^3$ is odd. The statement asked for is nevertheless true, and the right proof is by contrapositive: if $n$ is even, $n=2k$, then $n^3=8k^3$ is even. The contrapositive of &ldquo;$n^3$ odd $\\Rightarrow n$ odd&rdquo; is &ldquo;$n$ even $\\Rightarrow n^3$ even&rdquo;, not &ldquo;$n$ odd $\\Rightarrow n^3$ odd&rdquo;.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["prf"], "rating": 4.5}, {"id": "D1-1", "paper": "D1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Real numbers $a$, $b$ and $c$ satisfy \\[4^a=9,\\qquad 27^b=25,\\qquad 125^c=64 .\\] Find the value of $abc$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac23$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac43$", "$\\dfrac32$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac83$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Write each as a logarithm and pull the powers out. $a=\\log_4 9=\\log_2 3$, $b=\\log_{27}25=\\tfrac23\\log_3 5$ and $c=\\log_{125}64=\\tfrac{6}{3}\\log_5 2=2\\log_5 2$. So \\[abc=\\tfrac43\\,\\log_2 3\\cdot\\log_3 5\\cdot\\log_5 2=\\tfrac43,\\] because the chain $\\log_2 3\\cdot\\log_3 5\\cdot\\log_5 2$ is $\\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 2}\\cdot\\frac{\\ln5}{\\ln3}\\cdot\\frac{\\ln2}{\\ln5}=1$. The only work is keeping track of the factors $\\tfrac23$ and $2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp"], "rating": 4.5}, {"id": "D2-1", "paper": "D2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Which of the following is the negation of the statement <div class=\"c\">&ldquo;In every class, some student has solved every problem.&rdquo;<\/div><\/p>", "opts": ["In every class, no student has solved every problem.", "In some class, some student has not solved any problem.", "In some class, every student has failed to solve at least one problem.", "In every class, every student has failed to solve at least one problem.", "In some class, no student has solved any problem.", "In some class, some student has failed to solve at least one problem."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The statement has three layers: for every class, there exists a student, such that for every problem, the student solved it. Negating swaps each quantifier and negates the innermost claim: there is a class such that for every student there is a problem the student did not solve. That is option C. Option F negates only the middle layer, and option D keeps &ldquo;every class&rdquo; unchanged.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 4.5}, {"id": "D2-2", "paper": "D2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Consider the statement: <em>if $f'(a)=0$, then the curve $y=f(x)$ has a turning point at $x=a$.<\/em> Which of the following is a counterexample to the statement?<\/p>", "opts": ["$f(x)=x^2$, $a=0$", "$f(x)=x^3$, $a=0$", "$f(x)=x^4$, $a=0$", "$f(x)=x^3-3x$, $a=1$", "$f(x)=x$, $a=0$", "$f(x)=x^4-2x^2$, $a=0$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>A counterexample must make the hypothesis true and the conclusion false. For $x^3$ at $0$ the gradient is zero but the curve keeps increasing through the origin: a point of inflection, not a turning point. Options A, C, D and F have $f'(a)=0$ and a genuine turning point, and option E has $f'(0)=1\\neq0$, so its hypothesis is false and it cannot be a counterexample.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "diff"], "rating": 4.5}, {"id": "E1-1", "paper": "E1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy $x+y=4$ and $x^3+y^3=40$. Find the value of $x^4+y^4$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$120$", "$128$", "$136$", "$144$", "$152$", "$160$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Work with the symmetric quantities rather than solving. $x^3+y^3=(x+y)^3-3xy(x+y)$ gives $40=64-12xy$, so $xy=2$. Then $x^2+y^2=16-4=12$, and $x^4+y^4=(x^2+y^2)^2-2x^2y^2=144-8=136$. (The numbers are $2\\pm\\sqrt2$, which are real, so the data are consistent.)<\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "quad"], "rating": 4.5}, {"id": "E2-1", "paper": "E2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>The statement &ldquo;if a quadrilateral is a rhombus, then its diagonals are perpendicular&rdquo; is true. Which of the following is a counterexample to its <em>converse<\/em>?<\/p>", "opts": ["a square", "a rectangle that is not a square", "a kite that is not a rhombus", "a parallelogram that is not a rhombus", "a rhombus that is not a square", "there is none: the converse is also true"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The converse says: if the diagonals are perpendicular, then the quadrilateral is a rhombus. A counterexample needs perpendicular diagonals but must not be a rhombus. A kite that is not a rhombus is exactly that. Options B and D fail the hypothesis, since their diagonals are not perpendicular, and options A and E are rhombuses, so they satisfy the conclusion.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 4.5}, {"id": "B2-7", "paper": "B2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ for which $n!$ is divisible by $10^{10}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$40$", "$45$", "$50$", "$55$", "$60$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$10^{10} = 2^{10}5^{10}$, and factorials have far more factors of $2$ than of $5$, so only the fives matter. The number of factors of $5$ in $n!$ is $\\lfloor n/5 \\rfloor + \\lfloor n/25 \\rfloor + \\cdots$. For $n = 44$ this is $8 + 1 = 9$; for $n = 45$ it is $9 + 1 = 10$. So $n = 45$. Option C is the answer to &ldquo;ten multiples of $5$&rdquo;, forgetting that $25$ contributes twice.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "cnt"], "rating": 4.6}, {"id": "D1-2", "paper": "D1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>The curve $y=f(x)$ has exactly two stationary points: a local minimum at $(2,-3)$ and a local maximum at $(-1,4)$. Which of the following is a local maximum point of the curve \\[y=3-2f(4-2x)\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\left(\\tfrac52,-5\\right)$", "$(1,9)$", "$(1,-3)$", "$(-1,9)$", "$\\left(\\tfrac52,11\\right)$", "$(3,9)$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Stationary points of $y=3-2f(4-2x)$ occur where $4-2x$ is $2$ or $-1$, that is at $x=1$ and $x=\\tfrac52$. The inner map $x\\mapsto4-2x$ reverses direction, but a reflection in a vertical line never turns a maximum into a minimum; the factor $-2$ outside does. So the minimum of $f$ becomes a maximum: at $x=1$, $y=3-2(-3)=9$. The maximum of $f$ becomes the minimum $\\left(\\tfrac52,-5\\right)$. The answer is $(1,9)$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trans"], "rating": 4.6}, {"id": "D2-3", "paper": "D2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>For a positive integer $n$, the condition &ldquo;$n$ is divisible by 4&rdquo; is <span class=\"blank\">________<\/span> for &ldquo;$n^2+3n$ is divisible by 4&rdquo;.<\/p>", "opts": ["necessary but not sufficient", "sufficient but not necessary", "necessary and sufficient", "neither necessary nor sufficient"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>If $4\\mid n$ then $4\\mid n(n+3)=n^2+3n$, so the condition is sufficient. It is not necessary: $n=1$ gives $n^2+3n=4$, divisible by 4, although $4\\nmid 1$. In general $n(n+3)$ is divisible by 4 when $n\\equiv0$ or $1\\pmod 4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "mod"], "rating": 4.6}, {"id": "E2-2", "paper": "E2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements about real numbers are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>For every $x$ there is a $y$ with $y>x$.<\/li><li>There is a $y$ such that $y>x$ for every $x$.<\/li><li>For every $x$ there is a $y$ with $y^2=x$.<\/li><li>There is an $x$ such that $xy=x$ for every $y$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and IV only", "I, II and IV only", "IV only", "I and III only", "I, III and IV only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>I is true: take $y=x+1$. II swaps the quantifiers and is false: no single $y$ exceeds every $x$, since it would have to exceed itself. III fails for negative $x$. IV is true with $x=0$. So I and IV. The pair I and II is the whole point: &ldquo;for every, there is&rdquo; and &ldquo;there is, for every&rdquo; are different claims.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 4.6}, {"id": "F1-1", "paper": "F1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Given that $a=\\log_2 3$ and $b=\\log_3 5$, express $\\log_{15}20$ in terms of $a$ and $b$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{2+ab}{a+b}$", "$\\dfrac{2+ab}{a(1+b)}$", "$\\dfrac{2+b}{a+ab}$", "$\\dfrac{1+2ab}{a(1+b)}$", "$\\dfrac{2a+b}{a+b}$", "$\\dfrac{2+a}{1+ab}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Convert everything to base $2$. $\\log_2 5=\\log_23\\cdot\\log_35=ab$. Then \\[\\log_{15}20=\\frac{\\log_2 20}{\\log_2 15}=\\frac{2+\\log_25}{\\log_23+\\log_25}=\\frac{2+ab}{a+ab}=\\frac{2+ab}{a(1+b)} .\\] Option A treats $\\log_25$ as $b$, forgetting that $b$ is a base-3 logarithm.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp"], "rating": 4.6}, {"id": "C1-1", "paper": "C1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Real numbers $a>1$ and $b>1$ satisfy $\\log_a b = 2\\log_b a$. Find the value of $\\log_{ab} a$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\sqrt2-1$", "$2-\\sqrt2$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}$", "$\\sqrt2+1$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let $t=\\log_a b$. Then $\\log_b a=\\frac1t$, so $t=\\frac2t$ and $t^2=2$. Since $a,b>1$, $t>0$ and $t=\\sqrt2$. Now \\[ \\log_{ab}a=\\frac{1}{\\log_a(ab)}=\\frac{1}{1+\\log_a b}=\\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt2-1 . \\] Option D, $2-\\sqrt2$, is $\\log_{ab}b=\\frac{t}{1+t}$: the same computation with the roles of $a$ and $b$ swapped.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["sym"], "rating": 4.7}, {"id": "D2-4", "paper": "D2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Each of six cards has a whole number on one side and a shape on the other. The visible faces are \\[4,\\qquad 7,\\qquad \\text{triangle},\\qquad \\text{circle},\\qquad 10,\\qquad \\text{square}.\\] The rule is: <em>if a card has an even number on one side, then it has a triangle on the other side.<\/em> Which cards must be turned over to check whether the rule holds for all six cards?<\/p>", "opts": ["4 and 10 only", "4, 10 and the triangle", "4, 7 and 10", "4, 10, the circle and the square", "the circle and the square only", "all six cards"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The rule fails only on a card with an even number and a non-triangle. So check the even numbers, $4$ and $10$, in case a non-triangle is behind them, and the non-triangles, the circle and the square, in case an even number is behind them. The $7$ cannot break the rule whatever is behind it, and nor can the triangle: the rule does not say that triangles only go with even numbers. Turning the triangle is the classic error of testing the converse.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 4.7}, {"id": "B1-12", "paper": "B1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $x$ for which the infinite series \\[ 1 + (x-1) + (x-1)^2 + (x-1)^3 + \\cdots \\] converges and has sum equal to $\\dfrac{1}{2-x}$.<\/p>", "opts": ["all real $x$ with $x \\ne 2$", "$x &lt; 2$", "$0 &lt; x &lt; 2$", "$0 \\le x &lt; 2$", "$1 &lt; x &lt; 2$", "$x > 0$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The series is geometric with ratio $x - 1$; it converges exactly when $|x-1| &lt; 1$, i.e.\\ $0 &lt; x &lt; 2$, and there its sum is $\\dfrac{1}{1-(x-1)} = \\dfrac{1}{2-x}$. Outside that interval the formula $\\tfrac1{2-x}$ still makes sense as a number but the series diverges, which is the trap in option A. At $x = 0$ the terms are $\\pm1$ and the series does not converge, so option D is also wrong.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "log"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "C1-4", "paper": "C1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Find the minimum value of $x^2+\\dfrac{16}{x}$ for $x>0$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$6\\sqrt[3]{2}$", "$8$", "$8\\sqrt2$", "$12$", "$16$", "$20$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Split the $\\frac{16}{x}$ so that the product of the terms is constant: $x^2+\\frac8x+\\frac8x\\ge 3\\sqrt[3]{x^2\\cdot\\frac8x\\cdot\\frac8x}=3\\sqrt[3]{64}=12$, with equality when $x^2=\\frac8x$, that is $x=2$. Calculus agrees: the derivative $2x-\\frac{16}{x^2}$ vanishes at $x=2$, where the value is $4+8=12$. Applying AM--GM to the two terms $x^2$ and $\\frac{16}{x}$ directly gives the bound $8\\sqrt{x}$, which is not a constant and so says nothing about the minimum.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ineq"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "D1-3", "paper": "D1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>Find the coefficient of $x^4$ in the expansion of \\[(2+x)^6\\left(1-\\tfrac{x}{2}\\right)^6 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$15$", "$30$", "$60$", "$120$", "$240$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Multiply the brackets before expanding: $(2+x)\\left(1-\\tfrac x2\\right)=2-x+x-\\tfrac{x^2}{2}=2-\\tfrac{x^2}{2}$. So the product is $\\left(2-\\tfrac{x^2}{2}\\right)^6$, which contains only even powers. The $x^4$ term comes from choosing $-\\tfrac{x^2}{2}$ twice: \\[\\binom62\\,2^4\\left(-\\tfrac12\\right)^2=15\\cdot16\\cdot\\tfrac14=60 .\\] Expanding the two sixth powers separately and convolving the coefficients also works, but takes five products and invites sign errors.<\/p>", "tags": ["bin"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "E1-2", "paper": "E1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Find the product of all the real solutions of \\[2\\log_x 4-\\log_2 x=1 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$1$", "$2$", "$4$", "$\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>With $t=\\log_2x$, we have $\\log_x4=\\frac2t$, so $\\frac4t-t=1$, that is $t^2+t-4=0$. Both roots are real and non-zero, giving two valid $x$ (positive, and not equal to $1$). The product of the solutions is $2^{t_1}\\cdot2^{t_2}=2^{t_1+t_2}=2^{-1}=\\tfrac12$ by Vieta, without finding either root.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "vie"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "E1-3", "paper": "E1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>The point $(1,2)$ is reflected in the line $y=2x-3$. Find the coordinates of its image.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\left(\\tfrac{17}{5},\\tfrac45\\right)$", "$(3,1)$", "$\\left(\\tfrac{11}{5},\\tfrac25\\right)$", "$(-1,3)$", "$\\left(\\tfrac{13}{5},\\tfrac65\\right)$", "$\\left(\\tfrac{17}{5},-\\tfrac45\\right)$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The perpendicular through $(1,2)$ has gradient $-\\tfrac12$: $y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52$. It meets $y=2x-3$ where $\\tfrac52x=\\tfrac{11}{2}$, at $\\left(\\tfrac{11}{5},\\tfrac75\\right)$, the midpoint of the point and its image. So the image is $\\left(2\\cdot\\tfrac{11}{5}-1,\\ 2\\cdot\\tfrac75-2\\right)=\\left(\\tfrac{17}{5},\\tfrac45\\right)$. Option C is the foot of the perpendicular, a common place to stop.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "E2-3", "paper": "E2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>For a quadratic function $f$, the condition &ldquo;$f(1)$ and $f(3)$ have opposite signs&rdquo; is <span class=\"blank\">________<\/span> for &ldquo;$f(x)=0$ has at least one root strictly between $1$ and $3$&rdquo;.<\/p>", "opts": ["necessary but not sufficient", "sufficient but not necessary", "necessary and sufficient", "neither necessary nor sufficient"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>If $f(1)$ and $f(3)$ have opposite signs, the continuous function $f$ must cross zero between them: sufficient. It is not necessary, because both roots may lie between $1$ and $3$: $f(x)=(x-1.5)(x-2.5)$ has $f(1)$ and $f(3)$ both positive.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "quad"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "F1-2", "paper": "F1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Which of the following single transformations map the curve $y=4^x$ onto the curve $y=2^{2x+3}$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>A translation by $\\tfrac32$ in the negative $x$-direction.<\/li><li>A stretch parallel to the $y$-axis with scale factor $8$.<\/li><li>A stretch parallel to the $x$-axis with scale factor $\\tfrac12$.<\/li><li>A translation by $3$ in the positive $y$-direction.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "II and IV only", "I, II and III only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>$2^{2x+3}=4^{x+3/2}$, which is $y=4^x$ translated $\\tfrac32$ to the left: I works. Also $2^{2x+3}=8\\cdot4^x$: II works. III gives $4^{2x}=16^x$, and IV gives $4^x+3$; neither is right. For exponential curves a horizontal translation and a vertical stretch are the same transformation, which is why two options succeed.<\/p>", "tags": ["trans", "exp"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "F1-3", "paper": "F1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>The geometric series $3+6+12+24+\\cdots$ is summed term by term. What is the smallest number of terms for which the sum exceeds $1\\,000\\,000$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$17$", "$18$", "$19$", "$20$", "$21$", "$22$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The sum of $n$ terms is $3(2^n-1)$. We need $2^n-1>333\\,333.\\overline{3}$, so $2^n\\ge333\\,335$. Since $2^{18}=262\\,144$ is too small and $2^{19}=524\\,288$ is enough, $n=19$. Using $2^{10}\\approx10^3$ carelessly gives $2^{20}\\approx10^6$, off by one in the wrong direction.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "exp"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "F2-1", "paper": "F2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>$A$, $B$ and $C$ are statements. You are told: <div class=\"c\">&ldquo;$A$ only if $B$&rdquo;, \\quad &ldquo;$C$ if $B$&rdquo;, \\quad &ldquo;not $C$ unless $A$&rdquo;.<\/div> Which of the following follows?<\/p>", "opts": ["$A$ and $B$ are equivalent, but $C$ need not be equivalent to either.", "$A$ implies $C$, but not conversely.", "$A$, $B$ and $C$ are all equivalent.", "$C$ implies $A$, and nothing else follows.", "No two of $A$, $B$, $C$ need be equivalent.", "$B$ and $C$ are equivalent, but $A$ need not be equivalent to either."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Translate each clause. &ldquo;$A$ only if $B$&rdquo; is $A\\Rightarrow B$. &ldquo;$C$ if $B$&rdquo; is $B\\Rightarrow C$. &ldquo;Not $C$ unless $A$&rdquo; means &ldquo;if not $A$ then not $C$&rdquo;, whose contrapositive is $C\\Rightarrow A$. The implications form a cycle $A\\Rightarrow B\\Rightarrow C\\Rightarrow A$, so all three are equivalent.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ns"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "F2-2", "paper": "F2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$2^{2024}$ ends in the digit $6$.<\/li><li>$3^{2026}$ ends in the digit $9$.<\/li><li>$7^{2025}$ ends in the digit $7$.<\/li><li>$4^{2025}$ ends in the digit $6$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I, II, III and IV", "I, II and III only", "II and III only", "I and IV only", "III only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Last digits repeat with period $4$ (or $2$ for $4$). Powers of $2$ end in $2,4,8,6$, and $2024\\equiv0\\pmod4$ gives $6$: true. Powers of $3$ end in $3,9,7,1$, and $2026\\equiv2$ gives $9$: true. Powers of $7$ end in $7,9,3,1$, and $2025\\equiv1$ gives $7$: true. Powers of $4$ end in $4,6$ alternately, and $2025$ is odd, giving $4$: false.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "B1-10", "paper": "B1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>The roots of $x^2 - 2x - 1 = 0$ are $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. Find the value of \\[ \\frac{1}{\\alpha^5} + \\frac{1}{\\beta^5}. \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-82$", "$-41$", "$-\\dfrac{1}{82}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{82}$", "$41$", "$82$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Since $\\alpha\\beta = -1$, we have $\\tfrac1\\alpha = -\\beta$ and $\\tfrac1\\beta = -\\alpha$, so the required value is $-(\\alpha^5 + \\beta^5)$. Writing $s_n = \\alpha^n + \\beta^n$ and using $s_{n+1} = 2s_n + s_{n-1}$ (from the equation), $s_1 = 2$, $s_2 = 6$, $s_3 = 14$, $s_4 = 34$, $s_5 = 82$. The answer is $-82$. Option F is the sign slip.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["vie"], "rating": 4.9}, {"id": "C2-10", "paper": "C2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true for every real $x$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\sin x+\\cos x\\le\\sqrt2$<\/li><li>$\\sin x\\cos x\\le\\tfrac12$<\/li><li>$\\sin^4x+\\cos^4x\\ge\\tfrac12$<\/li><li>$\\sin x+\\cos x\\ge -1$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "I, II and IV only", "II and III only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: $\\sin x+\\cos x=\\sqrt2\\sin(x+\\frac\\pi4)\\le\\sqrt2$. II: $\\sin x\\cos x=\\frac12\\sin2x\\le\\frac12$. III: $\\sin^4x+\\cos^4x=(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x)^2-2\\sin^2x\\cos^2x=1-\\frac12\\sin^22x\\ge\\frac12$. IV fails at $x=\\frac{5\\pi}4$, where the sum is $-\\sqrt2$. The lower bound matching I is $\\ge-\\sqrt2$, not $-1$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 4.9}, {"id": "C1-5", "paper": "C1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>The set of values of $x$ with $0&lt;x&lt;\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ for which \\[ \\tan x+\\cot x&lt;4 \\] is an interval. Find its length.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$\\tan x+\\cot x=\\frac{\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x}{\\sin x\\cos x}=\\frac{2}{\\sin 2x}$. For $0&lt;x&lt;\\frac\\pi2$, $\\sin 2x>0$, so the inequality is $\\sin2x>\\frac12$, that is $\\frac\\pi6&lt;2x&lt;\\frac{5\\pi}6$, or $\\frac{\\pi}{12}&lt;x&lt;\\frac{5\\pi}{12}$. The length is $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. The identity is the whole question; clearing denominators directly leads to a quadratic in $\\tan x$ that gets to the same place more slowly.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 5.0}, {"id": "D1-4", "paper": "D1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>An arithmetic progression has first term $a$ and non-zero common difference $d$. Its 1st, 4th and 13th terms, in that order, are the first three terms of a geometric progression, and the sum of the first 20 terms of the arithmetic progression is 440. Find the sum of the first five terms of the geometric progression.<\/p>", "opts": ["$121$", "$186$", "$242$", "$324$", "$363$", "$484$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The geometric condition is $(a+3d)^2=a(a+12d)$, which gives $9d^2=6ad$, so $a=\\tfrac32d$ since $d\\neq0$. Then $S_{20}=10(2a+19d)=220d=440$, so $d=2$ and $a=3$. The terms are $3$, $9$, $27$: ratio $3$. The first five terms sum to $3+9+27+81+243=363$. Notice that the ratio is $3$ whatever $d$ is; the sum of 440 is needed only to fix the scale.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser"], "rating": 5.0}, {"id": "E1-4", "paper": "E1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[\\sum_{k=1}^{48}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}+\\sqrt{k+2}} .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt2$", "$3+\\sqrt2$", "$6$", "$3+2\\sqrt2$", "$4+2\\sqrt2$", "$7-\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Rationalise: $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt k+\\sqrt{k+2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{k+2}-\\sqrt k}{2}$. The sum telescopes with a gap of two, so two terms survive at each end: \\[\\tfrac12\\left(\\sqrt{50}+\\sqrt{49}-\\sqrt1-\\sqrt2\\right)=\\tfrac12\\left(5\\sqrt2+7-1-\\sqrt2\\right)=3+2\\sqrt2 .\\] Keeping only one surviving term at each end, as for a gap of one, gives the wrong answer.<\/p>", "tags": ["sym"], "rating": 5.0}, {"id": "E2-4", "paper": "E2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Call a positive integer <em>neat<\/em> if it can be written as the difference of the squares of two integers. Which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>Every odd positive integer is neat.<\/li><li>The product of two neat numbers is neat.<\/li><li>The sum of two neat numbers is neat.<\/li><li>If $n$ is neat, then $2n$ is neat.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "I and IV only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: $2m+1=(m+1)^2-m^2$. Next, $a^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)$, and the two factors have the same parity, so the difference is odd or a multiple of $4$; it is never $2\\pmod4$. Conversely $4m=(m+1)^2-(m-1)^2$. So the neat numbers are exactly those not congruent to $2\\pmod 4$. II: a product of two such numbers is odd or a multiple of $4$. III fails: $1+1=2$. IV fails: $n=1$ gives $2$. So I and II.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "mod"], "rating": 5.0}, {"id": "E2-5", "paper": "E2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>A student solves \\[\\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}=x^2-2x+4\\] as follows: &ldquo;The left-hand side simplifies to $x+2$, so $x+2=x^2-2x+4$, giving $x^2-3x+2=0$ and $x=1$ or $x=2$.&rdquo; Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The working and the answer are both correct.", "The simplification is invalid, and in fact there are no solutions.", "The quadratic was solved incorrectly.", "$x=2$ must be rejected, so the only solution is $x=1$.", "$x=1$ must be rejected, so the only solution is $x=2$.", "There are three solutions: $1$, $2$ and $-2$."], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The simplification $\\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}=x+2$ is valid only when $x\\neq2$, since the original left-hand side is undefined at $x=2$. So the root $x=2$ of the quadratic must be discarded, and $x=1$ is the only solution. Check: at $x=1$ both sides equal $3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "ineq"], "rating": 5.0}, {"id": "E2-6", "paper": "E2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>What is the largest positive integer $d$ with the property that, for every integer $n$, if $n^2$ is divisible by $12$ then $n$ is divisible by $d$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$6$", "$12$", "$24$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>If $12\\mid n^2$ then $3\\mid n^2$, so $3\\mid n$ (as $3$ is prime), and $4\\mid n^2$, so $n$ is even. Hence $6\\mid n$. Nothing more is forced: $n=6$ has $n^2=36$ divisible by $12$, and $6$ is not divisible by $4$ or $12$. So $d=6$. The tempting answer $12$ assumes that $12\\mid n^2$ implies $12\\mid n$, which fails because $12$ has a square factor.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 5.0}, {"id": "F1-4", "paper": "F1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Find the coefficient of $x^3$ in the expansion of $(1+x+x^2)^5$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$10$", "$15$", "$20$", "$25$", "$30$", "$35$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Write $1+x+x^2=(1+x)+x^2$ and expand by the binomial theorem: $\\sum_{j}\\binom5j(1+x)^{5-j}x^{2j}$. An $x^3$ term needs $2j\\le3$. For $j=0$, take the $x^3$ coefficient of $(1+x)^5$, which is $10$. For $j=1$, take $5$ times the $x$ coefficient of $(1+x)^4$, which is $5\\cdot4=20$. The total is $30$.<\/p>", "tags": ["bin"], "rating": 5.0}, {"id": "F2-3", "paper": "F2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A student proves that $\\left|x+\\dfrac1x\\right|\\ge2$ for every non-zero real $x$.<\/p><p><br> (I) If $x>0$: $(x-1)^2\\ge0$, so $x^2+1\\ge2x$; dividing by $x>0$ gives $x+\\dfrac1x\\ge2$.<br> (II) If $x&lt;0$: $(x+1)^2\\ge0$, so $x^2+1\\ge-2x$; dividing by $x&lt;0$ reverses the inequality, giving $x+\\dfrac1x\\le-2$.<br> (III) In either case, $\\left|x+\\dfrac1x\\right|\\ge2$.<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The proof is valid.", "Line II is wrong: the inequality should not be reversed.", "Line III does not follow from lines I and II.", "Line I is wrong: it needs $x\\ge1$.", "The claim is false for $-1&lt;x&lt;0$.", "Line II uses the wrong square."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>I is correct. II divides $x^2+1\\ge-2x$ by the negative number $x$ and correctly reverses it: $x+\\frac1x\\le-2$. III combines the two cases. The proof is valid. Using $(x-1)^2$ in case II would only give $x+\\frac1x\\le2$, which is true but too weak; choosing $(x+1)^2$ is what makes it work.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "ineq"], "rating": 5.0}, {"id": "B1-17", "paper": "B1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[ \\int_{-2}^{2} (x+2)\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\,dx . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$8$", "$2\\pi$", "$4\\pi$", "$8\\pi$", "$16$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Split the integrand. The function $x\\sqrt{4-x^2}$ is odd, so its integral over $[-2,2]$ is $0$. What remains is \\[ 2\\int_{-2}^{2}\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\,dx, \\] and $y = \\sqrt{4-x^2}$ is the upper half of the circle of radius $2$, so the integral is the area of that semicircle, $2\\pi$. The answer is $4\\pi$. There is no need for an antiderivative of $\\sqrt{4-x^2}$; recognising a definite integral as an area is the method. Option E takes the whole circle, option A forgets the even part.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 5.1}, {"id": "C2-9", "paper": "C2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>For a real number $x$, the condition <div class=\"c\"> $\\sin x>\\tfrac12$ <\/div> is <span class=\"blank\">________<\/span> for $\\cos 2x&lt;\\tfrac12$.<\/p>", "opts": ["necessary but not sufficient", "sufficient but not necessary", "necessary and sufficient", "neither necessary nor sufficient"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$\\cos2x&lt;\\frac12$ is $1-2\\sin^2x&lt;\\frac12$, that is $\\sin^2x>\\frac14$, or $|\\sin x|>\\frac12$. So $\\sin x>\\frac12$ implies it, and is sufficient. It is not necessary: $x=-\\frac\\pi2$ has $\\cos2x=-1&lt;\\frac12$ but $\\sin x=-1$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "trg"], "rating": 5.1}, {"id": "C2-1", "paper": "C2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Consider the statement <div class=\"c\"> &ldquo;The train will be on time unless it snows.&rdquo; <\/div> Here &ldquo;$A$ unless $B$&rdquo; means &ldquo;if not $B$, then $A$&rdquo;. Which one of the following is the negation of the statement?<\/p>", "opts": ["It snows and the train is on time.", "It snows and the train is not on time.", "If it snows, then the train will not be on time.", "If the train is not on time, then it snowed.", "It does not snow and the train is not on time.", "It does not snow or the train is not on time."], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The statement is &ldquo;if it does not snow, the train is on time&rdquo;. An implication is false in exactly one situation: its hypothesis holds and its conclusion fails. So the negation is &ldquo;it does not snow and the train is not on time&rdquo;. Option D is the contrapositive, which is equivalent to the original, not its negation. Option F is &ldquo;not(not snow) or not on time&rdquo; with the first negation dropped, and in any case the negation of an implication is never an &ldquo;or&rdquo;.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "D1-5", "paper": "D1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>For how many real values of $a$ does the equation \\[x^3-(a+1)x^2+(a+1)x-1=0\\] have a repeated root?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "no values"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The coefficients sum to zero, so $x=1$ is always a root, and $x^3-(a+1)x^2+(a+1)x-1=(x-1)(x^2-ax+1)$. A repeated root arises in two ways: the quadratic has a repeated root, which needs $a^2=4$, so $a=\\pm2$; or the quadratic has $1$ as a root, which needs $2-a=0$, so $a=2$ again. The two routes overlap, leaving exactly two values, $a=2$ and $a=-2$. Counting three is the trap.<\/p>", "tags": ["poly"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "D1-6", "paper": "D1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>How many solutions does the equation \\[\\tan x=2\\sin x\\] have in the interval $-360^\\circ\\le x\\le 360^\\circ$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$5$", "$7$", "$8$", "$9$", "$11$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Write $\\tan x=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$ (so $\\cos x\\neq0$) and factorise rather than divide: $\\sin x\\left(\\frac{1}{\\cos x}-2\\right)=0$. Either $\\sin x=0$, giving $x=-360^\\circ,-180^\\circ,0^\\circ,180^\\circ,360^\\circ$, or $\\cos x=\\tfrac12$, giving $x=\\pm60^\\circ,\\pm300^\\circ$. That is $5+4=9$ solutions, none with $\\cos x=0$. Dividing both sides by $\\sin x$ at the start loses five of them.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "D2-5", "paper": "D2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true for every integer $n$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$n^3-n$ is divisible by $6$<\/li><li>$n^5-n$ is divisible by $30$<\/li><li>$n^2+n+1$ is odd<\/li><li>$n^2-1$ is divisible by $8$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I: $n^3-n=(n-1)n(n+1)$ is a product of three consecutive integers, so divisible by $2$ and $3$. II: $n^5-n=n(n-1)(n+1)(n^2+1)$ contains $(n-1)n(n+1)$, so it is divisible by $6$; for $5$, check residues: if $n\\equiv0,\\pm1\\pmod5$ one of the first three factors is a multiple of $5$, and if $n\\equiv\\pm2$ then $n^2+1\\equiv5\\equiv0$. III: $n^2+n=n(n+1)$ is even, so $n^2+n+1$ is odd. IV fails for even $n$: $n=2$ gives $3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "D2-6", "paper": "D2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Real numbers $a$ and $b$ satisfy $a>b$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$a^2>b^2$<\/li><li>$a^3>b^3$<\/li><li>$\\dfrac1a&lt;\\dfrac1b$, provided $a$ and $b$ are non-zero<\/li><li>$2^a>2^b$<\/li><li>$a|a|>b|b|$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["II only", "II and IV only", "II, IV and V only", "I, II and IV only", "II, III, IV and V", "all five"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>II, IV and V hold because $x^3$, $2^x$ and $x|x|$ are all increasing on the whole real line; $x|x|$ is $x^2$ for $x\\ge0$ and $-x^2$ for $x&lt;0$, and both pieces increase. I fails for $a=1$, $b=-2$. III fails for $a=1$, $b=-1$: then $\\tfrac1a=1$ is not less than $\\tfrac1b=-1$. Taking reciprocals reverses an inequality only when both numbers have the same sign.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "D2-7", "paper": "D2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Which of the following are true for every $x$ with $0^\\circ&lt;x&lt;90^\\circ$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\sin x+\\cos x>1$<\/li><li>$\\sin^2x&lt;\\sin x$<\/li><li>$\\tan x>1$<\/li><li>$\\cos x&lt;\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "II and IV only", "I and IV only", "I, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>On this interval $0&lt;\\sin x&lt;1$ and $0&lt;\\cos x&lt;1$. I: $(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2=1+2\\sin x\\cos x>1$ and the sum is positive. II: multiply $\\sin x&lt;1$ by the positive number $\\sin x$. IV: multiply $\\cos x&lt;1$ by the positive number $\\frac{1}{\\cos x}$. III fails for any $x&lt;45^\\circ$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "E1-5", "paper": "E1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>In the expansion of $(2+3x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$, the coefficient of $x^k$ is greatest when $k$ equals<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$", "$7$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Let $c_k=\\binom{10}{k}2^{10-k}3^k$. Then $\\frac{c_{k+1}}{c_k}=\\frac{10-k}{k+1}\\cdot\\frac32$, which is at least $1$ exactly when $3(10-k)\\ge2(k+1)$, that is $k\\le5.6$. So the coefficients increase up to $c_6$ and decrease after it: the greatest is at $k=6$. The middle of the expansion, $k=5$, is where $\\binom{10}{k}$ peaks, but the factor $\\tfrac32$ pushes the peak one place right.<\/p>", "tags": ["bin"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "E1-6", "paper": "E1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>How many real solutions does the equation $2^x=3-x^2$ have?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Let $g(x)=2^x+x^2-3$. Then $g(1)=0$ exactly, $g(-1)=-\\tfrac32&lt;0$ and $g(-2)=\\tfrac54>0$, so there is a second root in $(-2,-1)$. There are no more, because $g$ is the sum of $2^x$ and $x^2$, both of which curve upwards, so the graph of $g$ is a single valley and crosses any horizontal line at most twice. Exactly two solutions. The root $x=1$ is easy to spot; the negative one is the one people miss.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "exp"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "E2-7", "paper": "E2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>A function $f$ satisfies $f'(x)=(x-1)^2(x-3)$ for all $x$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f$ has exactly one local minimum.<\/li><li>$f(0)>f(2)$<\/li><li>$f$ has a local maximum at $x=1$.<\/li><li>$f(4)>f(3)$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and IV only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only", "I and III only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>$(x-1)^2\\ge0$, so $f'$ has the sign of $x-3$, except that it is zero at $x=1$. So $f$ decreases on $(-\\infty,3]$, pausing at $x=1$ without turning, and increases on $[3,\\infty)$. I: the only turning point is the minimum at $x=3$. II: $f$ decreases from $0$ to $2$. III is false: $x=1$ is a stationary point of inflection. IV: $f$ increases after $3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "E2-8", "paper": "E2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>A student proves: <em>for all real $x$ and $y$, if $x>y$ then $x^2>y^2$<\/em>.<\/p><p><br> (I) Multiplying $x>y$ by $x$ gives $x^2>xy$.<br> (II) Multiplying $x>y$ by $y$ gives $xy>y^2$.<br> (III) Combining, $x^2>xy>y^2$.<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The proof is valid.", "Only line II is invalid, and the claim is true.", "Only line III is invalid.", "Lines I and II are invalid, but the claim is true.", "Line I needs $x>0$ and line II needs $y>0$; the claim is false.", "The proof is valid when $x,y\\ge0$, and the claim is true for all real $x,y$."], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Multiplying an inequality by a number keeps its direction only if that number is positive, so line I needs $x>0$ and line II needs $y>0$. The claim is false: $x=1$, $y=-2$ gives $1&lt;4$. The argument does prove the claim for positive $x$ and $y$, but that is not what was asserted.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "ineq"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "F1-5", "paper": "F1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Let $r(x)$ be the remainder when $x^{100}$ is divided by $x^2-3x+2$. Find $r(0)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2^{100}-2$", "$1$", "$2^{100}-1$", "$2-2^{100}$", "$0$", "$2^{99}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>$x^{100}=(x-1)(x-2)q(x)+ax+b$. Putting $x=1$ and $x=2$ gives $a+b=1$ and $2a+b=2^{100}$, so $a=2^{100}-1$ and $b=2-2^{100}$. Then $r(0)=b=2-2^{100}$. The divisor's roots do all the work; long division is hopeless here.<\/p>", "tags": ["poly"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "F1-6", "paper": "F1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>For how many real values of $k$ does the equation $x^3-3x^2=k$ have exactly two distinct real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "infinitely many"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The graph of $y=x^3-3x^2$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,-4)$. A horizontal line $y=k$ meets it in exactly two points only when it is tangent at a turning point, that is $k=0$ (roots $0,0,3$) or $k=-4$ (roots $2,2,-1$). For $-4&lt;k&lt;0$ there are three solutions, and otherwise one. So two values of $k$.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "poly"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "F2-4", "paper": "F2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Let $S$ be the statement &ldquo;if $n$ is divisible by $4$, then $n^2$ is divisible by $8$&rdquo;, where $n$ is a positive integer. How many of the following are true: $S$, its converse, its inverse, and its contrapositive?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>$S$ is true, since $4\\mid n$ gives $16\\mid n^2$, and so is its contrapositive. The converse, &ldquo;if $8\\mid n^2$ then $4\\mid n$&rdquo;, is also true: $8\\mid n^2$ forces $n$ even, and writing $n=2m$, $8\\mid4m^2$ forces $m$ even. The inverse is the contrapositive of the converse, so it is true as well. All four hold. The instinct that &ldquo;the converse is usually false&rdquo; is exactly what this question tests.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "mod"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "F2-5", "paper": "F2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Which of the following conditions on the real number $x$ are sufficient for $x^3&lt;x$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$0&lt;x&lt;1$<\/li><li>$x&lt;1$<\/li><li>$x^2&lt;1$<\/li><li>$x&lt;-1$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and III only", "IV only", "I and IV only", "I, III and IV only", "II only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>$x^3-x=x(x-1)(x+1)&lt;0$ exactly when $x&lt;-1$ or $0&lt;x&lt;1$. So I and IV are sufficient. II and III both allow $-1&lt;x&lt;0$, where for instance $x=-\\tfrac12$ gives $x^3=-\\tfrac18>-\\tfrac12$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "ineq"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "B1-6", "paper": "B1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Let $M$ and $m$ be the maximum and minimum values of \\[ (\\sin x + 2\\cos x)(2\\sin x + \\cos x) \\] over all real $x$. Find $Mm$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-\\dfrac94$", "$-\\dfrac52$", "$-1$", "$0$", "$\\dfrac94$", "$\\dfrac{81}{4}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Expanding, \\[ (\\sin x + 2\\cos x)(2\\sin x + \\cos x) = 2\\sin^2 x + 5\\sin x\\cos x + 2\\cos^2 x = 2 + 5\\sin x\\cos x. \\] Now $\\sin x\\cos x$ ranges over $[-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12]$ (it equals $\\tfrac12\\sin 2x$; or note $(\\sin x \\pm \\cos x)^2 \\ge 0$ gives $|\\sin x\\cos x| \\le \\tfrac12$ with both ends attained). So $M = \\tfrac92$, $m = -\\tfrac12$ and $Mm = -\\tfrac94$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 5.3}, {"id": "B2-3", "paper": "B2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>How many positive integers less than $1000$ have the property that their digits, read from left to right, are strictly increasing? (For example $137$ has this property; $370$, $33$ and $71$ do not.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$84$", "$120$", "$129$", "$130$", "$165$", "$512$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>A number with strictly increasing digits cannot contain $0$ (it would have to be the leading digit), and it is determined completely by the set of its digits, which is any non-empty subset of $\\{1, \\dots, 9\\}$ of size at most $3$. So the count is $\\binom91 + \\binom92 + \\binom93 = 9 + 36 + 84 = 129$. Option A counts only the three-digit numbers; option F is all subsets of the nine digits, size unrestricted.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["cnt"], "rating": 5.3}, {"id": "C1-3", "paper": "C1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>How many integers $x$ satisfy \\[ \\bigl|\\,x^2-5|x|+4\\,\\bigr|&lt;2\\,? \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$5$", "$6$", "$7$", "$8$", "infinitely many"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Put $t=|x|\\ge0$. The inequality is $-2&lt;t^2-5t+4&lt;2$. The left half is $t^2-5t+6>0$, so $t&lt;2$ or $t>3$; the right half is $t^2-5t+2&lt;0$, so $\\frac{5-\\sqrt{17}}{2}&lt;t&lt;\\frac{5+\\sqrt{17}}{2}$, roughly $0.44&lt;t&lt;4.56$. Among non-negative integers this leaves $t=1$ and $t=4$. The boundary values $t=2$ and $t=3$ give $|t^2-5t+4|=2$ exactly and are excluded by the strict inequality, and $t=0$ gives $4$. So $x\\in\\{\\pm1,\\pm4\\}$: four integers. With $\\le$ instead of $&lt;$ the answer would be $8$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["root"], "rating": 5.3}, {"id": "C2-2", "paper": "C2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>$P$ and $Q$ are statements, and the statement &ldquo;if $P$ then $Q$&rdquo; is false. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>if not $P$, then $Q$<\/li><li>if $Q$, then not $P$<\/li><li>if $P$, then not $Q$<\/li><li>if not $Q$, then not $P$<\/li><\/ol> Which of these must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "IV only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>&ldquo;If $P$ then $Q$&rdquo; is false only when $P$ is true and $Q$ is false. Then I has a false hypothesis (not $P$), so it is true. II has a false hypothesis ($Q$), so it is true. III has a true hypothesis and a true conclusion (not $Q$), so it is true. IV is the contrapositive of the original, and has the same truth value: false. The single fact to hold on to is that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is equivalent to (not $P$) or $Q$, so its negation is $P$ and (not $Q$).<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 5.3}, {"id": "B2-2", "paper": "B2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Let $N = 2^{2026}$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$N - 1$ is divisible by $3$.<\/li><li>$N + 1$ is divisible by $5$.<\/li><li>$N - 1$ is divisible by $7$.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements are true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Work with remainders of powers of $2$. Modulo $3$ the powers go $2, 1, 2, 1, \\dots$, so $2^{\\text{even}} \\equiv 1$ and I holds. Modulo $5$ the cycle is $2, 4, 3, 1$ of length $4$; $2026 \\equiv 2 \\pmod 4$, so $N \\equiv 4$ and $N + 1 \\equiv 0$: II holds. Modulo $7$ the cycle is $2, 4, 1$ of length $3$; $2026 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$, so $N \\equiv 2$ and $N - 1 \\equiv 1 \\ne 0$: III fails. The trap in III is assuming that because $2^3 \\equiv 1$ and $2026$ is even, something nice happens; the exponent has to be read modulo the cycle length $3$, not $2$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 5.4}, {"id": "D2-8", "paper": "D2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Five integers have the property that the sum of any three of them is even. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>All five integers are even.<\/li><li>The sum of all five integers is even.<\/li><li>The sum of any two of the integers is even.<\/li><li>Each of the integers is divisible by 4.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "none of them"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Take any two of the numbers, $p$ and $q$, and two others $r$ and $s$. Both $p+r+s$ and $q+r+s$ are even, so their difference $p-q$ is even: all five have the same parity. If all were odd, three of them would have an odd sum. So all five are even, and I, II and III follow. IV fails: $2,2,2,2,2$ has the property.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 5.4}, {"id": "E1-7", "paper": "E1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>A geometric series has sum to infinity $12$, and the series formed by squaring each of its terms has sum to infinity $48$. Find the sum to infinity of the series formed by cubing each term.<\/p>", "opts": ["$144$", "$216$", "$288$", "$432$", "$\\dfrac{1728}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{1728}{7}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$\\frac{a}{1-r}=12$ and $\\frac{a^2}{1-r^2}=48$. Divide the second by the first: $\\frac{a}{1+r}=4$. So $a=12(1-r)=4(1+r)$, giving $r=\\tfrac12$ and $a=6$. The cubes form a geometric series with first term $216$ and ratio $\\tfrac18$, with sum $\\frac{216}{7/8}=\\frac{1728}{7}$. Option E comes from using $1-r^3=\\tfrac58$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser"], "rating": 5.4}, {"id": "E2-10", "paper": "E2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>How many of the following statements about integers $n$ are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$n$ is even only if $n^2$ is even.<\/li><li>$n^2$ is divisible by $9$ unless $n$ is not divisible by $3$.<\/li><li>$n$ is prime only if $n$ is odd.<\/li><li>$n$ is odd if $n^2+1$ is even.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: if $n$ is even then $n^2$ is even; true. II: &ldquo;$P$ unless $Q$&rdquo; means &ldquo;if not $Q$ then $P$&rdquo;, so this reads: if $n$ is divisible by $3$ then $9\\mid n^2$; true. III: if $n$ is prime then $n$ is odd; false for $n=2$. IV: if $n^2+1$ is even then $n^2$ is odd, so $n$ is odd; true. Three are true.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "mod"], "rating": 5.4}, {"id": "E2-9", "paper": "E2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A student proves that $\\log_2 3$ is irrational.<\/p><p><br> (I) Suppose $\\log_2 3=\\dfrac pq$, where $p$ and $q$ are integers and $q\\neq0$.<br> (II) Since $\\log_23>0$, we may take $p$ and $q$ to be positive.<br> (III) Then $2^{p/q}=3$, so $2^p=3^q$.<br> (IV) But $2^p$ is even and $3^q$ is odd, a contradiction.<\/p><p><br> If line II were deleted, which line would no longer be justified?<\/p>", "opts": ["I", "III", "IV", "none: line II is unnecessary", "both III and IV"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Line III is fine for any integers: raise $2^{p/q}=3$ to the power $q$. Line IV uses that $2^p$ is an even integer and $3^q$ an odd integer, which needs $p\\ge1$ and $q\\ge1$. With negative exponents they are fractions, and &ldquo;even&rdquo; and &ldquo;odd&rdquo; mean nothing. Line II is what licenses line IV.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "exp"], "rating": 5.4}, {"id": "F1-7", "paper": "F1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Find the total measure, in degrees, of the set of $x$ in $0^\\circ\\le x\\le360^\\circ$ for which \\[2\\cos^2x>3\\sin x .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$60^\\circ$", "$120^\\circ$", "$180^\\circ$", "$240^\\circ$", "$300^\\circ$", "$330^\\circ$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>With $s=\\sin x$: $2-2s^2>3s$, that is $2s^2+3s-2&lt;0$, or $(2s-1)(s+2)&lt;0$. Since $s+2>0$ always, this is $\\sin x&lt;\\tfrac12$. That fails only on $[30^\\circ,150^\\circ]$, of measure $120^\\circ$, so the solution set has measure $360^\\circ-120^\\circ=240^\\circ$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 5.4}, {"id": "F2-6", "paper": "F2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Call a pair $(a,b)$ of positive integers <em>friendly<\/em> if $a+b$ divides $ab$. Which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>If $(a,b)$ is friendly, then so is $(ka,kb)$ for every positive integer $k$.<\/li><li>$(a,a)$ is friendly if and only if $a$ is even.<\/li><li>$(1,b)$ is never friendly.<\/li><li>If $(a,b)$ is friendly, then one of $a$ and $b$ divides the other.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II, III and IV", "I, II and III only", "II and III only", "I and IV only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: $\\frac{k^2ab}{k(a+b)}=k\\cdot\\frac{ab}{a+b}$ is an integer. II: $2a\\mid a^2$ exactly when $2\\mid a$. III: $b+1\\mid b$ is impossible for positive $b$, since $b+1>b$. IV fails: $(10,15)$ has $25\\mid150$, but neither number divides the other.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 5.4}, {"id": "F2-7", "paper": "F2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>A student proves that there are infinitely many primes: &ldquo;Suppose there are finitely many, $p_1,p_2,\\dots,p_k$. Let $N=p_1p_2\\cdots p_k+1$. None of the $p_i$ divides $N$. Hence $N$ is prime; but $N$ is larger than every $p_i$, a contradiction.&rdquo; Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The proof is correct as written.", "$N$ need not be prime, but it has a prime factor not in the list, so the proof is repaired by changing that step.", "$N$ need not be prime, and the argument cannot be repaired.", "The error is that some $p_i$ may divide $N$.", "The argument shows only that there are more than $k$ primes for one particular $k$.", "The assumption that the primes can be listed is itself invalid."], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>&ldquo;Hence $N$ is prime&rdquo; does not follow: $2\\cdot3\\cdot5\\cdot7\\cdot11\\cdot13+1=30031=59\\cdot509$. What is true is that $N>1$ has some prime factor, and no $p_i$ divides $N$, so that factor is a prime missing from the list. That still contradicts the assumption, so the proof survives once that one step is corrected.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "mod"], "rating": 5.4}, {"id": "B2-16", "paper": "B2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>A student is asked to prove the following claim: <em>if $a$ and $b$ are positive integers and $a^2 + b^2$ is divisible by $3$, then both $a$ and $b$ are divisible by $3$.<\/em> The student writes:<\/p><p><br> &ldquo;Every square leaves remainder $0$ or $1$ on division by $3$. If neither $a$ nor $b$ were divisible by $3$, then $a^2 + b^2$ would leave remainder $1 + 1 = 2$, so it would not be divisible by $3$. Hence at least one of $a$, $b$ is divisible by $3$, and the claim is proved.&rdquo;<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The claim is false: $a = 1$, $b = 2$ is a counterexample.", "The claim is true and the argument proves it.", "The claim is true, but the argument proves only that at least one of $a$, $b$ is divisible by $3$; the case in which exactly one of them is divisible by $3$ still has to be ruled out.", "The claim is true, but the argument is invalid because a square can leave remainder $2$ on division by $3$.", "The claim is false: $a = b = 3$ is a counterexample."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The claim is true, but the argument does not reach it. What has been shown is that $a$ and $b$ cannot both be non-multiples of $3$; that leaves open the case where exactly one of them is, and the argument never mentions it. That case is easy to close (the remainders are then $0 + 1 = 1$, again not divisible by $3$), but it has to be closed, and a proof that omits a case proves only the cases it treats. Options A and E are not counterexamples: $1 + 4 = 5$ is not divisible by $3$, and $a = b = 3$ satisfies the conclusion. Option D is false since squares are $0$ or $1$ modulo $3$. The point of the question is the difference between &ldquo;at least one&rdquo; and &ldquo;both&rdquo;, which the student's final sentence quietly glosses over.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["prf"], "rating": 5.5}, {"id": "D1-7", "paper": "D1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $x$ for which \\[\\log_2 x+\\log_x 16&lt;5 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$0&lt;x&lt;1$ or $2&lt;x&lt;16$", "$2&lt;x&lt;16$", "$x&lt;1$ or $2&lt;x&lt;16$", "$1&lt;x&lt;16$", "$0&lt;x&lt;2$ or $x>16$", "$0&lt;x&lt;\\tfrac12$ or $2&lt;x&lt;16$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>We need $x>0$ and $x\\neq1$. Put $t=\\log_2x$, so $\\log_x16=\\frac{4}{t}$ and the inequality is $t+\\frac4t&lt;5$. Do not multiply through by $t$ without its sign. If $t>0$, it becomes $t^2-5t+4&lt;0$, so $1&lt;t&lt;4$, that is $2&lt;x&lt;16$. If $t&lt;0$, multiplying reverses it: $t^2-5t+4>0$, which holds for every negative $t$, so every $0&lt;x&lt;1$ works. The solution set is $0&lt;x&lt;1$ or $2&lt;x&lt;16$.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "ineq"], "rating": 5.5}, {"id": "D1-8", "paper": "D1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $x$ for which \\[\\frac{2x}{x-1}\\ge x .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$0\\le x\\le 3$", "$1&lt;x\\le3$", "$x\\le0$ or $x\\ge3$", "$x\\le0$ or $1\\le x\\le3$", "$x&lt;1$ or $x\\ge3$", "$x\\le0$ or $1&lt;x\\le3$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Move everything to one side instead of multiplying by $x-1$, whose sign is unknown: \\[\\frac{2x}{x-1}-x=\\frac{3x-x^2}{x-1}=\\frac{x(3-x)}{x-1}\\ge0 .\\] The critical values are $0$, $1$ and $3$. The expression is positive for $x&lt;0$, negative on $(0,1)$, positive on $(1,3)$ and negative for $x>3$; it is zero at $x=0$ and $x=3$ and undefined at $x=1$. So the solution set is $x\\le0$ or $1&lt;x\\le3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq"], "rating": 5.5}, {"id": "D2-9", "paper": "D2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>Consider the statement: <em>if $f$ and $g$ are both decreasing on the real line, then the product $fg$ is increasing on the real line.<\/em> How many of the following pairs are counterexamples to this statement? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(x)=e^{-x}$, $g(x)=e^{-x}$<\/li><li>$f(x)=-e^{x}$, $g(x)=-e^{x}$<\/li><li>$f(x)=-x$, $g(x)=-x$<\/li><li>$f(x)=-x^3$, $g(x)=-e^{x}$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Every pair consists of decreasing functions, so each is a counterexample exactly when $fg$ is not increasing. I: $e^{-2x}$ decreases, so it is a counterexample. II: $e^{2x}$ increases, so it is not. III: $x^2$ decreases for $x&lt;0$, so it is a counterexample. IV: $x^3e^x$ has derivative $x^2(x+3)e^x$, negative for $x&lt;-3$, so it is a counterexample. That makes three.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 5.5}, {"id": "E2-11", "paper": "E2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Which of the following is the largest? (All angles are in radians.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\sin1$", "$\\sin2$", "$\\sin3$", "$\\cos1$", "$\\cos2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$\\cos2&lt;0$, since $2>\\frac\\pi2$. $\\sin3=\\sin(\\pi-3)$, which is the sine of a small angle, about $0.14$. $\\cos1=\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi2-1\\right)$, about $\\sin0.57$. $\\sin2=\\sin(\\pi-2)$, about $\\sin1.14$. Sine is increasing on $\\left[0,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$ and $1.14>1>0.57>0.14$, so $\\sin2$ is the largest. Reflecting every angle into $\\left[0,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$ turns the comparison into one of angles.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 5.5}, {"id": "B1-13", "paper": "B1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>How many chords of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 25$ have length $6$ and pass through the point $(3,1)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "infinitely many"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>A chord of length $6$ in a circle of radius $5$ sits at distance $\\sqrt{25 - 9} = 4$ from the centre. Any line through $(3,1)$ has distance at most $|OP| = \\sqrt{10} &lt; 4$ from the origin, since the perpendicular distance to a line through $P$ cannot exceed the distance to $P$ itself. So no such chord exists. The instinct to answer $2$ works for chords of length $8$, which need distance $3 &lt; \\sqrt{10}$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "C2-12", "paper": "C2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true for every $x$ with $0&lt;x&lt;\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\sin x+\\cos x>1$<\/li><li>$\\sin 2x&lt;2\\sin x$<\/li><li>$\\cos 2x>\\cos x$<\/li><li>$\\tan x>\\sin x$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: $(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2=1+\\sin2x>1$ on this interval, and the sum is positive. II: $\\sin2x=2\\sin x\\cos x&lt;2\\sin x$ since $0&lt;\\cos x&lt;1$ and $\\sin x>0$. III: $\\cos2x-\\cos x=2\\cos^2x-\\cos x-1=(2\\cos x+1)(\\cos x-1)&lt;0$ here, so III is false for every such $x$. IV: $\\tan x=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}>\\sin x$ since $0&lt;\\cos x&lt;1$. So I, II and IV.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "D1-9", "paper": "D1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A triangle has sides of length $x$, $x+1$ and $x+2$, and one of its angles is $120^\\circ$. Find its area.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{15\\sqrt3}{16}$", "$\\dfrac{15\\sqrt3}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{15}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{35\\sqrt3}{16}$", "$\\dfrac{5\\sqrt3}{4}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The $120^\\circ$ angle is the largest angle, so it faces the longest side $x+2$. The cosine rule gives \\[(x+2)^2=x^2+(x+1)^2-2x(x+1)\\cos120^\\circ=3x^2+3x+1,\\] so $2x^2-x-3=0$ and $x=\\tfrac32$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac32\\cdot\\tfrac52\\cdot\\sin120^\\circ=\\frac{15\\sqrt3}{16}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "D2-10", "paper": "D2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A triangle has sides $a\\le b\\le c$, with the angle $C$ opposite $c$. Which of the following conditions is sufficient for the triangle to be acute-angled? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$C&lt;90^\\circ$<\/li><li>$a^2+b^2>c^2$<\/li><li>$a=b=c$<\/li><li>$b^2+c^2>a^2$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I, II and III only", "I and II only", "III only", "II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "II only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Since $c$ is the longest side, $C$ is the largest angle, so the triangle is acute exactly when $C&lt;90^\\circ$, which by the cosine rule is exactly $a^2+b^2>c^2$. So I and II are sufficient, and so is III, since an equilateral triangle is acute. IV holds for every triangle with $a$ as the shortest side, including obtuse ones such as sides $3,4,6$, so it is not sufficient.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "trg"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "E1-8", "paper": "E1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>An angle $x$ satisfies $0^\\circ&lt;x&lt;90^\\circ$ and $\\sin x+\\cos x=\\tfrac75$. Find the sum of all possible values of $\\tan x$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac34$", "$\\dfrac43$", "$\\dfrac{25}{12}$", "$\\dfrac75$", "$\\dfrac{12}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{24}{7}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Squaring gives $1+2\\sin x\\cos x=\\tfrac{49}{25}$, so $\\sin x\\cos x=\\tfrac{12}{25}$. Then $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$ are the roots of $t^2-\\tfrac75t+\\tfrac{12}{25}=0$, namely $\\tfrac35$ and $\\tfrac45$, in either order. Both orders give angles in the first quadrant, so $\\tan x=\\tfrac34$ or $\\tfrac43$, and the sum is $\\tfrac{25}{12}$. The trap is to find one value and stop.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "vie"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "E1-9", "paper": "E1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>Find the total area of the regions enclosed between the curves $y=x^3$ and $y=4x$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$4$", "$6$", "$8$", "$16$", "$32$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The curves meet where $x^3=4x$, at $x=-2,0,2$. By the odd symmetry of both curves the two regions have equal area, and the one for $0\\le x\\le2$ is $\\int_0^2(4x-x^3)\\,dx=8-4=4$. The total is $8$. Integrating $4x-x^3$ from $-2$ to $2$ gives $0$, the signed cancellation that the word &ldquo;total&rdquo; warns against.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "E2-12", "paper": "E2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>In triangle $ABC$, which of the following conditions guarantee that the triangle is isosceles? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\sin A=\\sin B$<\/li><li>$\\cos A=\\cos B$<\/li><li>$\\tan A=\\tan B$<\/li><li>$\\sin 2A=\\sin 2B$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: $\\sin A=\\sin B$ means $A=B$ or $A+B=180^\\circ$, and the second is impossible in a triangle, so $A=B$. II: cosine is one-to-one on $(0^\\circ,180^\\circ)$. III: $\\tan A=\\tan B$ means $A=B$ or they differ by $180^\\circ$, which is impossible. IV allows $2A+2B=180^\\circ$, a right angle at $C$ with $A\\neq B$: the $3,4,5$ triangle satisfies it and is not isosceles.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "F1-8", "paper": "F1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>The equation $x^2+(k-3)x+k=0$ has real roots $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ with $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2=7$. Find all possible values of $k$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$k=4-\\sqrt{14}$ only", "$k=4+\\sqrt{14}$ only", "$k=4\\pm\\sqrt{14}$", "$k=1$ or $k=9$", "there are no such $k$", "$k=4-\\sqrt7$ only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$\\alpha^2+\\beta^2=(\\alpha+\\beta)^2-2\\alpha\\beta=(3-k)^2-2k=7$ gives $k^2-8k+2=0$, so $k=4\\pm\\sqrt{14}$. But the roots must be real: $(k-3)^2-4k\\ge0$, that is $k\\le1$ or $k\\ge9$. Now $4-\\sqrt{14}\\approx0.26$ qualifies and $4+\\sqrt{14}\\approx7.74$ does not; there the formula still gives $7$, but for a pair of complex roots. So $k=4-\\sqrt{14}$ only.<\/p>", "tags": ["quad", "vie"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "F1-9", "paper": "F1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $x$ for which \\[\\log_{1/2}\\left(x^2-3x\\right)>-2 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-1&lt;x&lt;4$", "$x&lt;-1$ or $x>4$", "$-1&lt;x&lt;0$ or $3&lt;x&lt;4$", "$0&lt;x&lt;3$", "$x&lt;0$ or $x>3$", "$3&lt;x&lt;4$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The logarithm needs $x^2-3x>0$, that is $x&lt;0$ or $x>3$. Since the base $\\tfrac12$ is less than $1$, $\\log_{1/2}$ is decreasing, so the inequality reverses: $x^2-3x&lt;\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)^{-2}=4$, giving $-1&lt;x&lt;4$. Intersecting with the domain gives $-1&lt;x&lt;0$ or $3&lt;x&lt;4$. Forgetting the domain gives A, and forgetting the reversal gives B.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "ineq"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "F2-8", "paper": "F2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Positive integers $a$ and $b$ have highest common factor $1$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\gcd(a+b,\\,ab)=1$<\/li><li>$\\gcd(a+b,\\,a-b)$ is $1$ or $2$ (when $a\\neq b$)<\/li><li>$\\gcd(a^2,\\,b^2)=1$<\/li><li>$\\gcd(a+b,\\,a^2+b^2)=1$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I, II and III only", "I and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only", "I only", "III only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>I: a prime dividing $ab$ divides $a$ or $b$, and if it also divides $a+b$ it divides both, which is impossible. II: a common divisor of $a+b$ and $a-b$ divides their sum $2a$ and difference $2b$, hence divides $2\\gcd(a,b)=2$. III: $a^2$ and $b^2$ have the same prime factors as $a$ and $b$. IV fails for $a=b=1$, since $\\gcd(2,2)=2$; in general $a^2+b^2=(a+b)^2-2ab$, so the common divisor divides $2ab$ and can be $2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "F2-9", "paper": "F2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>If $a$ is rational and $b$ is irrational, then $a+b$ is irrational.<\/li><li>If $a$ is rational and $b$ is irrational, then $ab$ is irrational.<\/li><li>If $x$ and $y$ are irrational, then $x+y$ is irrational.<\/li><li>If $x^2$ is irrational, then $x$ is irrational.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, II and IV only", "IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and II only", "I and IV only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: if $a+b$ were rational, then $b=(a+b)-a$ would be rational. II fails for $a=0$. III fails for $x=\\sqrt2$, $y=-\\sqrt2$. IV is the contrapositive of &ldquo;if $x$ is rational then $x^2$ is rational&rdquo;, which is true. So I and IV.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "B2-12", "paper": "B2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>A sequence is defined by $a_1 = 4$ and $a_{n+1} = a_n^2 - 2$ for $n \\ge 1$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>No term of the sequence is divisible by $3$.<\/li><li>For every $n \\ge 2$, $a_n$ leaves remainder $2$ on division by $12$.<\/li><li>Every term of the sequence ends in the digit $4$.<\/li><li>Some term of the sequence is divisible by $5$.<\/li><\/ol> How many of these statements are true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Each statement is a claim about remainders, and the recursion $a \\mapsto a^2 - 2$ acts on remainders. Modulo $3$: $4 \\equiv 1$, then $1^2 - 2 \\equiv 2$, then $2^2 - 2 = 2$, and the remainder stays $2$ forever; no term is divisible by $3$, so I holds. Modulo $12$: $a_2 = 14 \\equiv 2$, and $2^2 - 2 = 2$, so every later term is $2 \\pmod{12}$; II holds. Modulo $10$: $4 \\mapsto 16 - 2 = 14 \\equiv 4$, so every term ends in $4$; III holds. Modulo $5$: the remainder is $4$ forever, so no term is divisible by $5$; IV fails. Three of the four are true. The first four terms $4, 14, 194, 37634$ confirm all of this, but the remainder argument is what makes it a proof rather than a pattern.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "ser"], "rating": 5.7}, {"id": "D1-10", "paper": "D1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $k$ for which the equation \\[9^x-(k+1)\\,3^x+k=0\\] has exactly one real solution.<\/p>", "opts": ["$k=1$", "$k\\le0$", "$k\\le 0$ or $k=1$", "$k&lt;0$ or $k=1$", "$k\\ge1$", "$0&lt;k\\le1$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>With $u=3^x>0$ the equation is $u^2-(k+1)u+k=(u-1)(u-k)=0$. The root $u=1$ always gives $x=0$. The root $u=k$ gives a second value of $x$ exactly when $k>0$ and $k\\neq1$. So there is exactly one solution when $k\\le0$ or $k=1$. Excluding $k=0$ is the usual slip: then $u=0$ is simply not available, and $x=0$ is still the only solution.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "quad"], "rating": 5.7}, {"id": "D2-11", "paper": "D2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>An integer $N$ with $1\\le N\\le30$ is the subject of five statements: \\[\\text{A: $N$ is prime.}\\quad\\text{B: $N$ is even.}\\quad\\text{C: $N$ is a perfect square.}\\quad\\text{D: $N>10$.}\\quad\\text{E: $N$ is a multiple of 3.}\\] Exactly three of the five statements are true, and statement C is one of them. Find $N$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$9$", "$12$", "$16$", "$25$", "there is no such $N$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>C is true, so $N$ is one of $1,4,9,16,25$, and a perfect square above $1$ is never prime, so A is false. We need exactly two of B, D, E. $1$: none. $4$: B only. $9$: E only. $16$: B and D. $25$: D only. So $N=16$. Option C, $12$, makes exactly three statements true (B, D, E), but not with C among them.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 5.7}, {"id": "E1-10", "paper": "E1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>The tangents from $P(7,1)$ to the circle $x^2+y^2-2x-4y-4=0$ touch the circle at $A$ and $B$. Find the area of the quadrilateral $PACB$, where $C$ is the centre of the circle.<\/p>", "opts": ["$3\\sqrt7$", "$6\\sqrt7$", "$9$", "$12$", "$3\\sqrt{37}$", "$\\sqrt{37}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Completing the square, $C=(1,2)$ and the radius is $3$. $CP^2=36+1=37$, so each tangent has length $\\sqrt{37-9}=2\\sqrt7$. The quadrilateral consists of two right-angled triangles $CAP$ and $CBP$, each of area $\\tfrac12\\cdot3\\cdot2\\sqrt7$, so the total is $6\\sqrt7$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "circ"], "rating": 5.7}, {"id": "E2-13", "paper": "E2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>An odd integer $N$ with $1\\le N\\le50$ is the subject of four statements: \\[\\text{(1) $N$ is divisible by 4.}\\quad\\text{(2) $N$ is divisible by 6.}\\quad\\text{(3) $N$ is a perfect square.}\\quad\\text{(4) $N>40$.}\\] Exactly two of the four statements are true. Find $N$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$9$", "$25$", "$36$", "$45$", "$49$", "there is no such $N$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>$N$ is odd, so statements (1) and (2) are false, and both (3) and (4) must be true: $N$ is an odd perfect square greater than $40$ and at most $50$. That is $49$. Option C, $36$, satisfies (1), (2) and (3) but is even.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "mod"], "rating": 5.7}, {"id": "F1-10", "paper": "F1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>How many solutions does the equation $\\tan 3x=\\tan x$ have in the interval $0^\\circ\\le x\\le360^\\circ$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$7$", "$9$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$\\tan$ has period $180^\\circ$, so $\\tan3x=\\tan x$ requires $3x=x+180^\\circ n$, that is $x=90^\\circ n$: the candidates are $0^\\circ,90^\\circ,180^\\circ,270^\\circ,360^\\circ$. But at $90^\\circ$ and $270^\\circ$ both $\\tan x$ and $\\tan3x$ are undefined, so these are not solutions. That leaves $0^\\circ$, $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$: three solutions.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 5.7}, {"id": "B1-8", "paper": "B1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Find the sum of all solutions of \\[ \\sin x \\cos x = \\sin x + \\cos x - 1 \\] in the range $0^\\circ \\le x \\le 360^\\circ$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$90^\\circ$", "$180^\\circ$", "$450^\\circ$", "$540^\\circ$", "$720^\\circ$", "$900^\\circ$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let $s = \\sin x + \\cos x$, so $\\sin x\\cos x = \\tfrac12(s^2-1)$. The equation becomes $s^2 - 1 = 2s - 2$, i.e.\\ $(s-1)^2 = 0$, so $\\sin x + \\cos x = 1$. Squaring, $\\sin x\\cos x = 0$, which gives the candidates $0^\\circ, 90^\\circ, 180^\\circ, 270^\\circ, 360^\\circ$; but squaring is not reversible, and checking in $\\sin x + \\cos x = 1$ kills $180^\\circ$ and $270^\\circ$. The solutions are $0^\\circ, 90^\\circ, 360^\\circ$ with sum $450^\\circ$. Option F is the unchecked sum $900^\\circ$; option A forgets the endpoints.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "prf"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "B2-1", "paper": "B2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>The twenty numbers $1, \\tfrac12, \\tfrac13, \\dots, \\tfrac{1}{20}$ are written on a board. A move consists of choosing two numbers $a$ and $b$ on the board, erasing them, and writing the single number \\[ \\frac{ab}{a+b} \\] in their place. After $19$ moves exactly one number remains. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["The final number is always $\\dfrac{1}{20}$.", "The final number is always $\\dfrac{1}{21}$.", "The final number is always $\\dfrac{1}{210}$.", "The final number is always $20$.", "The final number is always $210$.", "The final number depends on the order in which the moves are made."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Look for a quantity a move does not change. Since $\\dfrac{1}{ab/(a+b)} = \\dfrac1a + \\dfrac1b$, the sum of the reciprocals of the numbers on the board is invariant. Initially it is $1 + 2 + \\cdots + 20 = 210$, so the final number has reciprocal $210$, whatever order the moves are made in. The answer is $\\tfrac{1}{210}$. Option F is the answer for someone who tries a couple of orders, gets messy fractions, and gives up.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["inv"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "C2-5", "paper": "C2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>A student wants to prove the following statement by contradiction: <div class=\"c\"> for every real $x$, if $x^2-3x+2&lt;0$ then $1&lt;x&lt;2$. <\/div> Which of the following is a correct first line for such a proof?<\/p>", "opts": ["Suppose that for every real $x$, $x^2-3x+2\\ge 0$ and ($x\\le1$ or $x\\ge2$).", "Suppose there is a real $x$ with $x^2-3x+2&lt;0$ and ($x\\le1$ or $x\\ge2$).", "Suppose there is a real $x$ with $x^2-3x+2&lt;0$ and $x\\le1$ and $x\\ge2$.", "Suppose there is a real $x$ with $x^2-3x+2\\ge0$ and $1&lt;x&lt;2$.", "Suppose there is a real $x$ with $x^2-3x+2\\ge0$ or $1&lt;x&lt;2$.", "Suppose there is a real $x$ such that, if $x^2-3x+2&lt;0$ then ($x\\le1$ or $x\\ge2$)."], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>A proof by contradiction of &ldquo;for all $x$, if $A(x)$ then $B(x)$&rdquo; starts from its negation: there is an $x$ with $A(x)$ and not $B(x)$. Here $B(x)$ is $1&lt;x&lt;2$, meaning $x>1$ and $x&lt;2$, and its negation is $x\\le1$ or $x\\ge2$. Option C negates the conclusion incorrectly: no $x$ satisfies both $x\\le1$ and $x\\ge2$, so that assumption leads to a contradiction for a trivial reason and proves nothing about the statement.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "prf"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "D1-11", "paper": "D1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $m$ for which the line $y=mx$ meets the curve $y=x^3-3x^2+2x$ at exactly three distinct points.<\/p>", "opts": ["$m>-\\tfrac14$", "$m\\ge-\\tfrac14$", "$m>-\\tfrac14$ and $m\\neq0$", "$m>-\\tfrac14$ and $m\\neq2$", "$m>2$", "$m&lt;-\\tfrac14$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The intersections satisfy $x\\left(x^2-3x+2-m\\right)=0$. The origin is always one; we need the quadratic to have two distinct roots, neither of them $0$. Distinct roots need $9-4(2-m)>0$, that is $m>-\\tfrac14$. A root at $0$ happens when $2-m=0$: for $m=2$ the quadratic is $x^2-3x$, whose root $x=0$ duplicates the origin, leaving only two points. So the answer is $m>-\\tfrac14$ with $m\\neq2$. At $m=0$ the roots are $1$ and $2$, which is fine.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "poly"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "D2-12", "paper": "D2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>A student proves: <em>if $a>0$, $b>0$ and $a+b=2$, then $ab\\le1$<\/em>.<\/p><p><br> (I) Suppose, for contradiction, that $ab>1$.<br> (II) Then $b>\\dfrac1a$.<br> (III) So $a+b>a+\\dfrac1a$.<br> (IV) But $a+\\dfrac1a\\ge2$ for every $a>0$.<br> (V) Hence $a+b>2$, which contradicts $a+b=2$.<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The proof is valid.", "Line II is wrong: dividing by $a$ may reverse the inequality.", "Line IV is false for some $a>0$.", "Line V is wrong: from lines III and IV we may only conclude $a+b\\ge2$.", "The argument proves the converse of the claim.", "The claim itself is false."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Every step holds. Line II divides by $a$, which is positive. Line IV is true, since $a+\\frac1a-2=\\frac{(a-1)^2}{a}\\ge0$. Line V combines a strict inequality with a non-strict one, $a+b>a+\\frac1a\\ge2$, and the result is strict. The contradiction is genuine, so the proof is valid. Questions like this test whether you can confirm a correct argument rather than hunt for a mistake that is not there.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "ineq"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "E1-11", "paper": "E1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $k$ for which \\[(k-1)x^2+2kx+k+3\\ge0\\qquad\\text{for every real }x .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$k\\ge\\tfrac32$", "$k>\\tfrac32$", "$k>1$", "$k\\ge1$", "$1&lt;k\\le\\tfrac32$", "$k\\le\\tfrac32$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>If $k=1$ the expression is $2x+4$, which is negative for $x&lt;-2$, so $k=1$ fails. Otherwise we need a positive leading coefficient, $k>1$, and a non-positive discriminant: $4k^2-4(k-1)(k+3)=12-8k\\le0$, that is $k\\ge\\tfrac32$. So $k\\ge\\tfrac32$, and the boundary is included, since a zero discriminant still satisfies $\\ge0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["quad", "ineq"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "E1-12", "paper": "E1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Positive real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy $x^2y=32$. Find the minimum value of $x+2y$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$4\\sqrt[3]{4}$", "$6\\sqrt[3]{2}$", "$8$", "$8\\sqrt2$", "$12$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Eliminate $y=\\frac{32}{x^2}$ and minimise $g(x)=x+\\frac{64}{x^2}$ for $x>0$. $g'(x)=1-\\frac{128}{x^3}=0$ at $x^3=128$, so $x=4\\sqrt[3]2$, and this is a minimum since $g\\to\\infty$ at both ends. Then $\\frac{64}{x^2}=\\frac{64}{16\\sqrt[3]4}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]4}=2\\sqrt[3]2$, so the minimum is $4\\sqrt[3]2+2\\sqrt[3]2=6\\sqrt[3]2$. At the minimum $x=4y$, the same balance a three-term AM-GM with $\\frac x2+\\frac x2+2y$ would predict.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "diff"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "E1-13", "paper": "E1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $k$ for which the equation $\\cos^2x-\\sin x=k$ has at least one real solution $x$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-1\\le k\\le1$", "$0\\le k\\le\\tfrac54$", "$-\\tfrac54\\le k\\le1$", "$-1\\le k\\le2$", "$-1\\le k\\le\\tfrac54$", "$k\\le\\tfrac54$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>With $s=\\sin x\\in[-1,1]$, the equation is $k=1-s^2-s=\\tfrac54-\\left(s+\\tfrac12\\right)^2$. On $[-1,1]$ this takes its maximum $\\tfrac54$ at $s=-\\tfrac12$ and its minimum $-1$ at $s=1$, and every value in between. So $-1\\le k\\le\\tfrac54$. Allowing all real $s$ gives F; forgetting the vertex gives A.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "quad"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "E1-14", "paper": "E1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>The triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(4,1)$ and $(k,5)$ has area $10$. Find the sum of all possible values of $k$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$10$", "$20$", "$30$", "$35$", "$40$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The area is $\\tfrac12|4\\cdot5-1\\cdot k|=\\tfrac12|20-k|$. Setting it equal to $10$ gives $20-k=\\pm20$, so $k=0$ or $k=40$, and the sum is $40$. For $k=0$ the third vertex is $(0,5)$, with base $5$ on the $y$-axis and height $4$: area $10$, as required. Missing the modulus finds only one value.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "E2-14", "paper": "E2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>The sequence $x_1,x_2,\\dots$ is defined by $x_1=a$ and $x_{n+1}=x_n^{\\,2}-2$. For how many of the values $a=2,\\ 0,\\ 3,\\ -2.5,\\ 1,\\ -2$ is the sequence bounded?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Compute: $a=2$ gives $2,2,\\dots$; $a=0$ gives $0,-2,2,2,\\dots$; $a=1$ gives $1,-1,-1,\\dots$; $a=-2$ gives $-2,2,2,\\dots$. All four are bounded. $a=3$ gives $3,7,47,\\dots$ and $a=-2.5$ gives $4.25,\\dots$, both unbounded, since once $|x_n|>2$ we have $x_{n+1}-2=x_n^2-4>0$ and the excess over $2$ grows. So four. The rule is that the sequence is bounded exactly when $|a|\\le2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "F1-11", "paper": "F1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A circle lies in the first quadrant, touches both coordinate axes, and also touches the line $3x+4y=12$. Find the sum of all possible radii.<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$5$", "$6$", "$7$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Touching both axes in the first quadrant means centre $(r,r)$ and radius $r$. Tangency to the line requires $\\frac{|3r+4r-12|}{5}=r$, that is $|7r-12|=5r$, giving $r=6$ or $r=1$. Both work: the circle of radius $1$ is the incircle of the triangle cut off by the line, and the circle of radius $6$ lies beyond the line. The sum is $7$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "circ"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "F1-12", "paper": "F1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>In triangle $ABC$, $AB=5$ and $AC=7$, and the median from $A$ to the midpoint $M$ of $BC$ has length $4$. Find $BC$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\sqrt{21}$", "$2\\sqrt{21}$", "$2\\sqrt{10}$", "$6$", "$\\sqrt{74}$", "$8$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Let $BM=MC=m$ and $\\theta=\\angle AMB$, so $\\angle AMC=180^\\circ-\\theta$ and $\\cos\\angle AMC=-\\cos\\theta$. The cosine rule in the two halves gives $25=16+m^2-8m\\cos\\theta$ and $49=16+m^2+8m\\cos\\theta$. Adding eliminates $\\theta$: $74=32+2m^2$, so $m^2=21$ and $BC=2m=2\\sqrt{21}$. Option A is $m$ itself.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "F1-13", "paper": "F1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>How many real values of $k$ are there for which the region enclosed by the curve $y=x^2$ and the line $y=kx$ has area $36$?<\/p>", "opts": ["none", "exactly one", "infinitely many", "exactly two", "exactly three", "exactly four"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The curve and line meet at $x=0$ and $x=k$, and the enclosed area is $\\left|\\int_0^k(kx-x^2)\\,dx\\right|=\\frac{|k|^3}{6}$. Setting this equal to $36$ gives $|k|=6$, so $k=6$ or $k=-6$. The two regions are mirror images in the $y$-axis. Writing $\\int_0^k$ without the modulus finds only $k=6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "F2-10", "paper": "F2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>On an island, knights always tell the truth and knaves always lie. Three islanders make these statements:<\/p><p><br> A: &ldquo;B is a knave.&rdquo;<br> B: &ldquo;Exactly one of us three is a knight.&rdquo;<br> C: &ldquo;B and I are different types.&rdquo;<\/p><p><br> What are A, B and C?<\/p>", "opts": ["All three are knights.", "A and C are knights; B is a knave.", "Only A is a knight.", "Only C is a knight.", "A and B are knights; C is a knave.", "No assignment is consistent."], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>A and B are of opposite types, since A's statement is true exactly when B is a knave. Suppose B is a knight. Then A is a knave, and exactly one knight (B) means C is a knave; but C's claim that B and C differ would then be true, which a knave cannot say. So B is a knave and A is a knight. B's statement is false, so the number of knights is not one; with A a knight, C must be a knight too. Check C: B and C do differ, so C tells the truth.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "F2-11", "paper": "F2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Call a function $f$, defined for $x\\ge0$, <em>subadditive<\/em> if $f(x+y)\\le f(x)+f(y)$ for all $x,y\\ge0$. Which of the following functions are subadditive? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(x)=\\sqrt x$<\/li><li>$f(x)=x^2$<\/li><li>$f(x)=x+1$<\/li><li>$f(x)=2^x$<\/li><li>$f(x)=3x$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and III only", "I, III and V only", "I only", "III and V only", "I, II and V only", "I, II, III, IV and V"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>I: squaring shows $\\sqrt{x+y}\\le\\sqrt x+\\sqrt y\\iff x+y\\le x+y+2\\sqrt{xy}$, which is true. III: $x+y+1\\le x+y+2$. V gives equality, which is allowed. II fails at $x=y=1$ ($4>2$), and IV fails at $x=y=2$ ($16>8$). So I, III and V.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ineq"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "B1-2", "paper": "B1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[ \\int_{-1}^{5} \\bigl|\\,x^3 - 6x^2 + 12x - 9\\,\\bigr| \\,dx. \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-6$", "$6$", "$24$", "$36$", "$42$", "$48$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The cubic is $(x-2)^3 - 1$, so put $u = x - 2$; the limits become $-3$ and $3$ and the integrand is $|u^3 - 1|$, which changes sign at $u = 1$. Then \\[ \\int_{-3}^{1} (1 - u^3)\\,du + \\int_{1}^{3} (u^3 - 1)\\,du = 24 + 18 = 42. \\] Ignoring the modulus gives $\\int_{-3}^{3}(u^3-1)\\,du = -6$, option A, which is negative and therefore impossible for the integral of a non-negative function. A negative answer to a modulus integral is always a signal to look again.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["vie"], "rating": 5.9}, {"id": "B2-4", "paper": "B2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Let $a$, $b$, $c$ be real numbers. Exactly two of the following three statements are true: \\[ a &lt; b, \\qquad b &lt; c, \\qquad a &lt; c . \\] Which of the following must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a &lt; b$", "$b &lt; c$", "$a &lt; c$", "$b$ is the largest of the three numbers", "$a$ is the smallest of the three numbers"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Which statement is the false one? It cannot be $a &lt; c$: if $a &lt; c$ were false then $c \\le a$, but the other two statements would then be true, giving $a &lt; b &lt; c \\le a$, impossible. So $a &lt; c$ is always among the two true statements, and must be true. Either of the other two can be the false one: $(a,b,c) = (1,3,2)$ makes $b &lt; c$ false, and $(2,1,3)$ makes $a &lt; b$ false. Those two examples also dispose of D and E.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 5.9}, {"id": "C1-12", "paper": "C1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is continuous and satisfies $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all real $x$, and $\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 f(x)\\,dx=2$. Find \\[ \\int_0^{675} f(3x+1)\\,dx . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$150$", "$225$", "$450$", "$900$", "$1350$", "it cannot be determined from the information given"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Substitute $u=3x+1$: $dx=\\frac13du$, and the limits become $1$ and $2026$, so the integral is $\\frac13\\int_1^{2026}f(u)\\,du$. The interval $[1,2026]$ has length $2025=3\\times675$, exactly $675$ periods, and the integral of a periodic function over any interval of one period is the same, here $2$. So the integral is $\\frac13\\cdot675\\cdot2=450$. Option E forgets the $\\frac13$ from $dx$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "arint"], "rating": 5.9}, {"id": "C2-19", "paper": "C2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is continuous on the real numbers and satisfies $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for every $x$. Let $a>0$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_{-a}^{a} f(x)\\,dx=0$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{a} f(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^{a} f(a-x)\\,dx$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_{-a}^{a} x f(x)\\,dx=0$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2a} f(x-a)\\,dx=0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I and IV only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: $f$ is odd. II: the substitution $x\\mapsto a-x$ works for any $f$. IV: with $u=x-a$ the integral is $\\int_{-a}^{a}f(u)\\,du=0$. III fails: $xf(x)$ is even, not odd, and $f(x)=x$ gives $\\int_{-a}^a x^2\\,dx>0$. So I, II and IV.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "sym"], "rating": 5.9}, {"id": "C1-2", "paper": "C1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>When the polynomial $p(x)$ is divided by $(x-1)^2$ the remainder is $2x+1$, and when $p(x)$ is divided by $x+1$ the remainder is $-3$. Let $r(x)$ be the remainder when $p(x)$ is divided by $(x-1)^2(x+1)$. Find $r(3)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$\\dfrac{15}{2}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The remainder has degree at most $2$. Since dividing $r(x)$ by $(x-1)^2$ must leave the same remainder as dividing $p(x)$ by $(x-1)^2$, we can write $r(x)=c(x-1)^2+2x+1$ for some constant $c$. The second condition says $r(-1)=p(-1)=-3$, so $4c-1=-3$ and $c=-\\frac12$. Then \\[ r(3)=-\\tfrac12\\cdot 4+7=5 . \\] The whole question is the choice of form for $r$: writing $r(x)=\\alpha x^2+\\beta x+\\gamma$ and using the derivative condition at $x=1$ also works, but takes three equations instead of one. Option F comes from expanding $-\\frac12(x-1)^2$ with a sign slip.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["poly"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "C2-14", "paper": "C2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>For a positive integer $n$, consider \\[ A:\\ 4\\mid n,\\qquad B:\\ 6\\mid n,\\qquad C:\\ 8\\mid n^2,\\qquad D:\\ 12\\mid n^2 . \\] Which one of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$A$ is equivalent to $C$, and $B$ is equivalent to $D$.", "$A$ is equivalent to $C$, but $B$ is not equivalent to $D$.", "$B$ is equivalent to $D$, but $A$ is not equivalent to $C$.", "Neither $A$ is equivalent to $C$, nor $B$ to $D$.", "$A$ is equivalent to $D$, and $B$ is equivalent to $C$."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$C$: $8\\mid n^2$ forces $n$ even; writing $n=2m$, $8\\mid4m^2$ means $m$ is even, so $4\\mid n$. Conversely $4\\mid n$ gives $16\\mid n^2$. So $A\\Leftrightarrow C$. $D$: $12\\mid n^2$ means $4\\mid n^2$ and $3\\mid n^2$, that is $2\\mid n$ and $3\\mid n$, so $6\\mid n$; conversely $6\\mid n$ gives $36\\mid n^2$. So $B\\Leftrightarrow D$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "mod"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "D1-12", "paper": "D1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>The circle $C$ has centre $(3,4)$ and passes through the origin $O$. How many lines through $O$ meet $C$ again at a point $P$ with $OP=8$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "infinitely many"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The radius is $5$. A chord of length $8$ lies at distance $3$ from the centre, so we need lines through $O$ at distance $3$ from $(3,4)$. The line $x=0$ is at distance $3$: it meets the circle at $(0,0)$ and $(0,8)$. For $y=kx$ the distance is $\\frac{|3k-4|}{\\sqrt{1+k^2}}=3$, giving $24k=7$, so $k=\\tfrac{7}{24}$. That is two lines. Writing every line through $O$ as $y=kx$ silently discards the vertical one and gives the tempting answer $1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "circ"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "D1-13", "paper": "D1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Find the total area of the regions enclosed between the curve $y=x^3-4x$ and the $x$-axis for $-2\\le x\\le 3$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{25}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{33}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{41}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{49}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{57}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{65}{4}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The curve crosses the axis at $0$ and $2$ inside the interval, so split there. With $F(x)=\\tfrac{x^4}{4}-2x^2$: $\\int_{-2}^0=F(0)-F(-2)=4$, $\\int_0^2=F(2)-F(0)=-4$, and $\\int_2^3=F(3)-F(2)=\\tfrac94+4=\\tfrac{25}{4}$. The total area is $4+4+\\tfrac{25}{4}=\\tfrac{57}{4}$. Integrating straight across from $-2$ to $3$ gives $\\tfrac{25}{4}$, because the first two pieces cancel.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "D2-13", "paper": "D2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>A student adapts the standard proof that $\\sqrt2$ is irrational to &ldquo;prove&rdquo; that $\\sqrt4$ is irrational.<\/p><p><br> (I) Suppose $\\sqrt4=\\dfrac pq$, where $p$ and $q$ are positive integers with no common factor.<br> (II) Then $p^2=4q^2$, so $p^2$ is even and hence $p$ is even.<br> (III) Write $p=2k$. Then $4k^2=4q^2$, so $k^2=q^2$.<br> (IV) Hence $q^2$ is even, so $q$ is even.<br> (V) So $p$ and $q$ are both even, a contradiction. Hence $\\sqrt4$ is irrational.<\/p><p><br> In which line does the first error occur?<\/p>", "opts": ["I", "II", "III", "IV", "V", "there is no error"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Lines I to III are correct. In the $\\sqrt2$ proof the corresponding step reads $2k^2=q^2$, which forces $q^2$ to be even. Here the equation is $k^2=q^2$, which says nothing about parity: indeed $p=2$, $q=1$, $k=1$. Line IV is the first false step.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "E1-15", "paper": "E1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Two circles of radius $1$ have their centres a distance $1$ apart. Find the area of the region inside both circles.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt3$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt3$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The two centres and either intersection point form an equilateral triangle, so each centre sees the common chord at an angle of $\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}$. The lens is two circular segments, each a sector of area $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac{2\\pi}3=\\tfrac\\pi3$ minus a triangle of area $\\tfrac12\\sin\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}$. The total is $2\\left(\\tfrac\\pi3-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}4\\right)=\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$. Option A is one segment only.<\/p>", "tags": ["circ", "trg"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "E1-16", "paper": "E1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The normal to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $P(1,1)$ meets the curve again at $Q$. Find the length $PQ$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac52$", "$\\dfrac94$", "$\\dfrac{5\\sqrt2}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt5}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{5\\sqrt5}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{5\\sqrt3}{2}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The tangent gradient at $P$ is $2$, so the normal is $y-1=-\\tfrac12(x-1)$. Substituting into $y=x^2$ gives $2x^2+x-3=0$, that is $(x-1)(2x+3)=0$, so $Q=\\left(-\\tfrac32,\\tfrac94\\right)$. Then $PQ=\\sqrt{\\left(\\tfrac52\\right)^2+\\left(\\tfrac54\\right)^2}=\\tfrac54\\sqrt{4+1}=\\tfrac{5\\sqrt5}{4}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "coord"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "E2-15", "paper": "E2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The polynomial $p(x)$ has integer coefficients, and $p(0)$ and $p(1)$ are both odd. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$p$ has no integer roots.<\/li><li>$p$ has no rational roots.<\/li><li>$p(2)$ is odd.<\/li><li>$p(x)\\neq0$ for every real $x$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and III only", "I only", "I, II and III only", "III only", "I, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>For an integer $n$, $p(n)-p(0)$ is a sum of multiples of $n$, and $p(n)-p(1)$ is a sum of terms $a_k(n^k-1)$, each a multiple of $n-1$. For even $n$, $p(n)$ has the parity of $p(0)$; for odd $n$, that of $p(1)$. Either way $p(n)$ is odd, never $0$: I and III hold. II and IV fail for $p(x)=2x-1$, with $p(0)=-1$ and $p(1)=1$, whose root is $\\tfrac12$.<\/p>", "tags": ["poly", "mod"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "F1-14", "paper": "F1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>The curve $y=x^3+px^2+qx$ has a stationary point at $(1,-4)$. Find the $y$-coordinate of its other stationary point.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-4$", "$\\dfrac{44}{27}$", "$13$", "$\\dfrac{49}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{392}{27}$", "$-\\dfrac{392}{27}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>From $y(1)=-4$: $1+p+q=-4$. From $y'(1)=0$: $3+2p+q=0$. Subtracting gives $p=2$ and $q=-7$. Then $y'=3x^2+4x-7=(3x+7)(x-1)$, so the other stationary point is at $x=-\\tfrac73$, where \\[y=-\\tfrac{343}{27}+2\\cdot\\tfrac{49}{9}+\\tfrac{49}{3}=\\tfrac{-343+294+441}{27}=\\tfrac{392}{27} .\\]<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "F1-15", "paper": "F1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Find the area of the region of points $(x,y)$ satisfying all three of \\[y\\ge x^2,\\qquad y\\le 2x+3,\\qquad x\\ge0 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac53$", "$\\dfrac{16}{3}$", "$7$", "$8$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{32}{3}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The parabola and line meet where $x^2=2x+3$, at $x=-1$ and $x=3$. With $x\\ge0$ the region runs from $0$ to $3$, and its area is $\\int_0^3(2x+3-x^2)\\,dx=9+9-9=9$. Option F is the whole segment from $-1$ to $3$, before the condition $x\\ge0$ is applied.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "F2-12", "paper": "F2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Which of the following conditions guarantee that a triangle is equilateral? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>Two of its angles are $60^\\circ$.<\/li><li>Its angles form an arithmetic progression.<\/li><li>Its sides form an arithmetic progression and one of its angles is $60^\\circ$.<\/li><li>Its angles form an arithmetic progression and so do its sides.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and III only", "I, III and IV only", "I and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "III and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: the third angle is also $60^\\circ$. II only forces the middle angle to be $60^\\circ$ (for example $30^\\circ,60^\\circ,90^\\circ$). III: take sides $a-d,a,a+d$. If the $60^\\circ$ angle faces $a$, the cosine rule gives $a^2=a^2+3d^2$; if it faces $a+d$ or $a-d$, it gives $3ad=0$. Either way $d=0$. IV reduces to III, since angles in AP include a $60^\\circ$ angle.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ns"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "F2-13", "paper": "F2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>$f$ is a polynomial with $f(x)\\ge0$ for $0\\le x\\le2$ and $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2f(x)\\,dx=0$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(1)=0$<\/li><li>$f(x)=0$ for every real $x$.<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 f(x)\\,dx=0$<\/li><li>The statements above would still all be true if $f$ were only known to be continuous.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>A continuous non-negative function with zero integral over $[0,2]$ must vanish throughout $[0,2]$: if it were positive somewhere, it would be positive on a small interval and the integral would be positive. So I holds. A polynomial with infinitely many roots is the zero polynomial, so II holds, and then III. IV fails for a continuous function that is zero on $[0,2]$ and positive beyond it, such as $\\max(0,x-2)$, which breaks II and III.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "poly"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "F2-14", "paper": "F2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A positive integer $n\\le100$ is the subject of five statements: \\[\\text{(1) $n$ is a perfect square.}\\quad\\text{(2) $n$ is a perfect cube.}\\quad\\text{(3) $n$ is even.}\\quad\\text{(4) $n&lt;50$.}\\quad\\text{(5) $n$ is divisible by $9$.}\\] Exactly one of the statements is false. Find $n$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$16$", "$18$", "$27$", "$36$", "$64$", "$81$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Squares that are also cubes are sixth powers: $1$ and $64$. $64$ fails (4) and (5), and $1$ fails (3) and (5), so neither works with only one false statement. So exactly one of (1) and (2) is false and the other four are true. If (1) is false, $n$ is an even cube below $50$ divisible by $9$: none, since $8\\nmid$ and $27$ is odd. If (2) is false, $n$ is an even square below $50$ divisible by $9$: that is $36$.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "F2-15", "paper": "F2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The cubic function $f$ satisfies $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)>0$, and it has a local maximum at $x=2$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f$ has a local minimum at some $x>2$.<\/li><li>$f(2)>0$<\/li><li>$f'(1)>0$<\/li><li>$f(x)=0$ has three distinct real roots.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and II only", "III and IV only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$f'$ is a quadratic that changes from positive to negative at $x=2$, and $f'(0)>0$. If $f'$ opens upwards, $2$ is its smaller root and the minimum is to the right; if it opens downwards, $2$ is its larger root and the other root is below $0$. Either way $f'>0$ on $[0,2)$, so III holds and $f(2)>f(0)=0$ gives II. I fails in the second case. IV can fail too: $f(x)=x^3-9x^2+24x$ has $f'(x)=3(x-2)(x-4)$, so $f'(0)=24>0$ and a maximum at $2$, but $f(x)=x(x^2-9x+24)$ has only the real root $0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "root"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "B1-16", "paper": "B1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The curve $y = x^4 - 4x^3 + ax^2$, where $a$ is a real constant, has exactly one stationary point. Find the complete set of possible values of $a$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$a > \\dfrac92$", "$a \\ge \\dfrac92$", "$a &lt; \\dfrac92$", "$0 &lt; a &lt; \\dfrac92$", "$a > 0$", "$a \\ge 0$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$y' = 4x^3 - 12x^2 + 2ax = 2x(2x^2 - 6x + a)$. There is always the stationary point $x = 0$, so we need $2x^2 - 6x + a = 0$ to have no real roots: $36 - 8a &lt; 0$, i.e.\\ $a > \\tfrac92$. The boundary case $a = \\tfrac92$ gives the double root $x = \\tfrac32$, which is still a second stationary point (a stationary point of inflection), so option B is wrong. The factor $2x$ is the other trap: $x = 0$ is a stationary point for every $a$, so \"no real roots\" applies to the quadratic factor only.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["root"], "rating": 6.1}, {"id": "C2-8", "paper": "C2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>For a real number $x$, how many of the following statements are true for every real $x$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$x>2$ if $x^2>4$.<\/li><li>$x>2$ only if $x^2>4$.<\/li><li>$x^2>4$ if and only if $|x|>2$.<\/li><li>$x^3>8$ only if $x>2$.<\/li><li>$x^2>4$ if $x^3>8$.<\/li><li>$x^2>4$ only if $x^3>8$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>&ldquo;$A$ if $B$&rdquo; means $B\\Rightarrow A$; &ldquo;$A$ only if $B$&rdquo; means $A\\Rightarrow B$. I is $x^2>4\\Rightarrow x>2$: false at $x=-3$. II is $x>2\\Rightarrow x^2>4$: true. III is true. IV is $x^3>8\\Rightarrow x>2$: true, since cubing is increasing. V is $x^3>8\\Rightarrow x^2>4$: true, via $x>2$. VI is $x^2>4\\Rightarrow x^3>8$: false at $x=-3$. Four are true.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ns"], "rating": 6.1}, {"id": "B1-14", "paper": "B1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A sequence is defined by $a_1 = 3$ and \\[ a_{n+1} = \\frac{a_n - 1}{a_n + 1} \\qquad (n \\ge 1). \\] Find the value of the product $a_1 a_2 a_3 \\cdots a_{2026}$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-2$", "$-\\dfrac12$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac32$", "$3$", "$\\dfrac94$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Compute a few terms: $a_1 = 3$, $a_2 = \\tfrac12$, $a_3 = -\\tfrac13$, $a_4 = -2$, $a_5 = 3$. The sequence has period $4$. (Structurally, if $g(x) = \\tfrac{x-1}{x+1}$ then $g(g(x)) = -\\tfrac1x$, so $g$ has order $4$.) The product over one period is $3\\cdot\\tfrac12\\cdot(-\\tfrac13)\\cdot(-2) = 1$. Since $2026 = 4\\cdot 506 + 2$, the product is $a_1 a_2 = \\tfrac32$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "inv"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "C1-16", "paper": "C1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The continuous function $f$ satisfies \\[ f(x)=x^2+x\\int_0^1 t\\,f(t)\\,dt\\qquad\\text{for all } x . \\] Find $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{11}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{48}$", "$\\dfrac{13}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{7}{12}$", "$\\dfrac58$", "$\\dfrac23$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The integral $\\int_0^1 tf(t)\\,dt$ is just a number; call it $B$. Then $f(x)=x^2+Bx$, and substituting back, \\[ B=\\int_0^1 t(t^2+Bt)\\,dt=\\tfrac14+\\tfrac B3, \\] so $B=\\frac38$. Hence $\\int_0^1 f=\\frac13+\\frac B2=\\frac13+\\frac3{16}=\\frac{25}{48}$. Option A comes from stopping at $B=\\frac14$, having dropped the self-referential term.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "C2-4", "paper": "C2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A function $f$ from the real numbers to the real numbers is called <em>increasing<\/em> if, for all real $x$ and $y$, if $x&lt;y$ then $f(x)&lt;f(y)$. Which one of the following is equivalent to &ldquo;$f$ is not increasing&rdquo;?<\/p>", "opts": ["For all real $x$ and $y$, if $x&lt;y$ then $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "There exist real $x$ and $y$ such that, if $x&lt;y$ then $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "There exist real $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ge y$ and $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "For all real $x$ and $y$, $x\\ge y$ or $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "There exist real $x$ and $y$ with $x&lt;y$ or $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "There exist real $x$ and $y$ with $x&lt;y$ and $f(x)\\ge f(y)$."], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>&ldquo;Increasing&rdquo; is: for all $x,y$, (if $x&lt;y$ then $f(x)&lt;f(y)$). Negating swaps the quantifier and negates the implication, and the negation of &ldquo;if $A$ then $B$&rdquo; is &ldquo;$A$ and not $B$&rdquo;: there exist $x,y$ with $x&lt;y$ and $f(x)\\ge f(y)$. Option B is the classic mistake of negating only the conclusion. It is true for every function whatsoever, since choosing $x=y$ makes the hypothesis $x&lt;y$ false and the implication vacuously true.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "D1-14", "paper": "D1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>In triangle $ABC$, $AB=10$, $BC=6$ and angle $BAC=30^\\circ$. There are two possible triangles. Find the positive difference between their areas.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt{11}$", "$5\\sqrt3$", "$10\\sqrt3$", "$5\\sqrt{11}$", "$10\\sqrt{11}$", "$0$: the triangle is determined"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Let $AC=b$. The cosine rule at $A$ gives $36=100+b^2-20b\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, that is $b^2-10\\sqrt3\\,b+64=0$. Both roots are positive, and they differ by $\\sqrt{300-256}=2\\sqrt{11}$. The area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot10\\cdot b\\cdot\\sin30^\\circ=\\tfrac52b$, so the areas differ by $\\tfrac52\\cdot2\\sqrt{11}=5\\sqrt{11}$. Treating the cosine rule as a quadratic in the unknown side handles the ambiguous case with no need to find either angle.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "vie"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "D1-15", "paper": "D1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>How many tangents to the curve $y=x^3-3x$ pass through the point $(2,2)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The tangent at $x=t$ is $y=(3t^2-3)(x-t)+t^3-3t$. It passes through $(2,2)$ when $2=(3t^2-3)(2-t)+t^3-3t$, which simplifies to $t^3-3t^2+4=0$, that is $(t+1)(t-2)^2=0$. The double root $t=2$ is a single tangent (the point lies on the curve and this is its own tangent), and $t=-1$ gives a second. So there are two tangents. Counting roots with multiplicity gives three.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "D2-14", "paper": "D2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>The cubic $f(x)=x^3+bx^2+cx+d$ satisfies $f(0)&lt;0$, $f(1)>0$ and $f(2)&lt;0$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f$ has three distinct real roots.<\/li><li>$b&lt;-3$<\/li><li>$f$ has a local minimum between $x=1$ and $x=2$.<\/li><li>$d&lt;0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and IV only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and IV only", "I and III only", "IV only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>There are sign changes on $(0,1)$ and $(1,2)$, and since $f(x)\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$ with $f(2)&lt;0$, a third root lies beyond $2$: I is true. The roots are then $r_1\\in(0,1)$, $r_2\\in(1,2)$ and $r_3>2$, so $-b=r_1+r_2+r_3>3$ and II is true. IV is $d=f(0)&lt;0$. III can fail: with roots $0.5$, $1.5$ and $10$ the local minimum is near $x=7$.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "poly"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "D2-15", "paper": "D2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Which of the following are equal to $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,dx$ for <em>every<\/em> quadratic function $f$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\tfrac13\\left(f(-1)+4f(0)+f(1)\\right)$<\/li><li>$f\\!\\left(-\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)+f\\!\\left(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$<\/li><li>$2f(0)$<\/li><li>$f(-1)+f(1)$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Take $f(x)=px^2+qx+r$, so the integral is $\\tfrac{2p}{3}+2r$. I: $\\tfrac13\\left((p-q+r)+4r+(p+q+r)\\right)=\\tfrac{2p}{3}+2r$. II: $2\\left(\\tfrac p3+r\\right)=\\tfrac{2p}{3}+2r$. III gives $2r$, correct only when $p=0$, and IV gives $2p+2r$, also correct only when $p=0$. So I and II. It is striking that two well-chosen points do as well as three.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "poly"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "D2-16", "paper": "D2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Each of the following statements is either true or false.<\/p><p><br> 1. Exactly one of these three statements is true.<br> 2. Statement 1 is false.<br> 3. Statements 1 and 2 are both false.<\/p><p><br> Which statements are true?<\/p>", "opts": ["statement 1 only", "statement 2 only", "statement 3 only", "statements 2 and 3 only", "no assignment of truth values is consistent", "more than one assignment is consistent"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Statements 1 and 2 contradict each other, so exactly one of them is true, and statement 3 is false. If 1 were false, 2 would be true and 3 false, giving exactly one true statement, which would make 1 true: a contradiction. So 1 is true and 2 is false, and then exactly one statement is true, which is consistent. Only statement 1 is true.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "E1-17", "paper": "E1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>For which value of $a$ is \\[\\int_a^{a+1}\\left(x^2-4x\\right)dx\\] as small as possible?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$\\dfrac32$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac52$", "$3$", "$0$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The integral is the signed area under $y=x^2-4x$ over a window of width $1$, and it is smallest when the window sits where the curve is lowest. The curve is symmetric about its minimum at $x=2$, so the best window is $\\left[\\tfrac32,\\tfrac52\\right]$, giving $a=\\tfrac32$. To confirm, $I(a)=\\tfrac{(a+1)^3-a^3}{3}-2\\left((a+1)^2-a^2\\right)=a^2-3a-\\tfrac53$, which is minimised at $a=\\tfrac32$. Choosing $a=2$ puts the minimum at the edge of the window rather than its centre.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "fint"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "E2-16", "paper": "E2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is continuous and satisfies $f(x)+f(1-x)=1$ for every real $x$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx=\\tfrac12$<\/li><li>$f\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)=\\tfrac12$<\/li><li>$f$ is increasing.<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 xf(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^1(1-x)\\bigl(1-f(x)\\bigr)\\,dx$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "II only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and IV only", "II and IV only", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>II: put $x=\\tfrac12$. Reflecting $[0,1]$ in $x=\\tfrac12$ shows that $\\int_0^1 f(1-x)\\,dx=\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$, so adding gives $2\\int_0^1 f=\\int_0^1 1=1$, and I holds. For IV, the same reflection turns $\\int_0^1 xf(x)\\,dx$ into $\\int_0^1(1-x)f(1-x)\\,dx=\\int_0^1(1-x)\\bigl(1-f(x)\\bigr)dx$. III fails: $f(x)=\\tfrac12+\\sin\\left(2\\pi\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)\\right)$ satisfies the condition but is not increasing.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "sym"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "F1-16", "paper": "F1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The curves $y=x^2$ and $y=-(x-3)^2$ have two common tangent lines. Find the point where these two lines meet.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\left(\\tfrac32,0\\right)$", "$(3,9)$", "$(0,-9)$", "$\\left(\\tfrac32,-\\tfrac92\\right)$", "$(0,0)$", "$(3,0)$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The tangent to $y=x^2$ at $x=a$ is $y=2ax-a^2$. It touches $y=-(x-3)^2$ when $x^2+(2a-6)x+9-a^2=0$ has a repeated root: $(2a-6)^2-4(9-a^2)=8a^2-24a=0$, so $a=0$ or $a=3$. The common tangents are $y=0$ and $y=6x-9$, meeting at $\\left(\\tfrac32,0\\right)$. By symmetry, the two parabolas are images of each other under a half-turn about $\\left(\\tfrac32,0\\right)$, so the meeting point had to be there.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "coord"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "F1-17", "paper": "F1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A cylinder is inscribed in a sphere of radius $3$. Find the greatest possible volume of the cylinder.<\/p>", "opts": ["$12\\sqrt3\\,\\pi$", "$36\\pi$", "$24\\pi$", "$8\\sqrt3\\,\\pi$", "$16\\pi$", "$18\\sqrt2\\,\\pi$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>If the cylinder has height $h$ and radius $\\rho$, then $\\rho^2+\\left(\\tfrac h2\\right)^2=9$, so $V=\\pi\\left(9-\\tfrac{h^2}{4}\\right)h$. Then $V'(h)=\\pi\\left(9-\\tfrac{3h^2}{4}\\right)=0$ at $h=2\\sqrt3$, a maximum. This gives $V=\\pi(9-3)\\cdot2\\sqrt3=12\\sqrt3\\,\\pi$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "F1-18", "paper": "F1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Find the sum of all the integers from $1$ to $500$ inclusive that are divisible by $3$ or by $7$, but not by both.<\/p>", "opts": ["$5796$", "$36\\,015$", "$41\\,583$", "$47\\,883$", "$53\\,679$", "$59\\,475$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Multiples of $3$: $3,6,\\dots,498$, which is $166$ terms with sum $3\\cdot\\frac{166\\cdot167}{2}=41\\,583$. Multiples of $7$: $7,\\dots,497$, which is $71$ terms with sum $7\\cdot\\frac{71\\cdot72}{2}=17\\,892$. Multiples of $21$: $21,\\dots,483$, which is $23$ terms with sum $21\\cdot\\frac{23\\cdot24}{2}=5796$. &ldquo;Not both&rdquo; removes the multiples of $21$ from both lists: $41\\,583+17\\,892-2\\cdot5796=47\\,883$. Subtracting $5796$ only once gives the inclusive &ldquo;or&rdquo;, option E.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "cnt"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "F2-16", "paper": "F2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Consider the equation $\\tan x=x$, with $x$ in radians. Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>It has exactly one solution in the interval $\\frac\\pi2&lt;x&lt;\\frac{3\\pi}2$.<\/li><li>It has no solution in the interval $0&lt;x&lt;\\frac\\pi2$.<\/li><li>Its solution in $\\frac\\pi2&lt;x&lt;\\frac{3\\pi}2$ is greater than $\\pi$.<\/li><li>Its solution in $\\frac\\pi2&lt;x&lt;\\frac{3\\pi}2$ is less than $\\frac{5\\pi}4$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I, II, III and IV", "I only", "I and III only", "II and IV only", "I, II and III only", "I and II only"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>II: on $\\left(0,\\frac\\pi2\\right)$, $\\tan x>x$. On $\\left(\\frac\\pi2,\\pi\\right]$, $\\tan x\\le0&lt;x$, so any solution lies in $\\left(\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}2\\right)$, where $\\tan x-x$ increases from $-\\pi$ to $+\\infty$: exactly one solution, and it exceeds $\\pi$. So I and III hold. IV fails: at $x=\\frac{5\\pi}4$, $\\tan x=1&lt;\\frac{5\\pi}{4}$, so the crossing comes later (in fact near $4.49$).<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "root"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "F2-17", "paper": "F2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Let $p=\\log_2 3$, $q=\\log_3 5$ and $r=\\log_5 8$. Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["$r&lt;q<p$", "$p&lt;q&lt;r$", "$q&lt;r<p$", "$r<p&lt;q$", "$q<p&lt;r$", "$p&lt;r&lt;q$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Compare each with $1.5$ and then refine. $2^{1.5}=2\\sqrt2\\approx2.83&lt;3$, so $p>1.5$. $3^{1.5}=3\\sqrt3\\approx5.20>5$, so $q&lt;1.5$. To separate $q$ and $r$: $5^{1.3}>8$ (since $5^{13}\\approx1.22\\times10^9>8^{10}\\approx1.07\\times10^9$), so $r&lt;1.3$, while $3^{1.4}&lt;5$ (since $3^{14}=4\\,782\\,969&lt;5^{10}=9\\,765\\,625$), so $q>1.4$. Hence $r&lt;q<p$.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "ineq"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "B2-5", "paper": "B2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>For which positive integers $n$ is it possible to fill an $n \\times n$ grid with integers so that the sum of the entries in every row is odd and the sum of the entries in every column is even?<\/p>", "opts": ["Every $n \\ge 1$", "Even $n$ only", "Odd $n$ only", "No $n$", "Every $n \\ge 2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Add up every entry in the grid in two ways. Summing the rows gives a sum of $n$ odd numbers, which is odd exactly when $n$ is odd; summing the columns gives a sum of $n$ even numbers, which is always even. So $n$ odd is impossible. For $n$ even, put $1$ in every cell of the first column and $0$ everywhere else: every row sums to $1$ and the columns sum to $n$ (even) and $0$. The answer is even $n$ only. Counting one quantity two ways is the whole method.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["col", "two"], "rating": 6.3}, {"id": "C1-19", "paper": "C1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>The tangent to the curve $y=x^3$ at the point $P(1,1)$ meets the curve again at $Q$. Find the area of the region enclosed between the curve and the line segment $PQ$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac94$", "$\\dfrac{27}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{4}$", "$8$", "$\\dfrac{27}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{81}{4}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The tangent at $P$ is $y=3x-2$, and $x^3-(3x-2)=x^3-3x+2=(x-1)^2(x+2)$, where the double factor is forced by tangency. So $Q$ has $x=-2$, and the curve lies above the tangent on $(-2,1)$. With $u=x-1$, \\[ \\int_{-2}^{1}(x-1)^2(x+2)\\,dx=\\int_{-3}^{0}(u^3+3u^2)\\,du=0-\\left(\\tfrac{81}4-27\\right)=\\tfrac{27}{4} . \\] Knowing that the factorisation must contain $(x-1)^2$ saves the polynomial division, and the shift $u=x-1$ saves expanding.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "poly"], "rating": 6.3}, {"id": "B1-19", "paper": "B1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>A function $f$ satisfies \\[ \\int_0^2 f(2x)\\,dx = 3, \\qquad \\int_1^3 f(x+1)\\,dx = 5, \\qquad \\int_0^1 f(3-3x)\\,dx = 2. \\] Find the value of $\\displaystyle\\int_3^4 f(x)\\,dx$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-1$", "$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$5$", "$6$", "cannot be determined from the information given"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Substitute in each integral. In the first, $u = 2x$ gives $\\tfrac12\\int_0^4 f = 3$, so $\\int_0^4 f = 6$. In the second, $u = x+1$ gives $\\int_2^4 f = 5$. In the third, $u = 3 - 3x$ reverses the limits and contributes a factor $\\tfrac13$, giving $\\tfrac13\\int_0^3 f = 2$, so $\\int_0^3 f = 6$. Then \\[ \\int_3^4 f = \\int_0^4 f - \\int_0^3 f = 6 - 6 = 0. \\] The information is enough, so option G is wrong; the value $5$ in option E is $\\int_2^4 f$, read off carelessly.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "C1-10", "paper": "C1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>The two tangents from the origin to the circle $(x-4)^2+(y-3)^2=4$ meet at an acute angle $\\theta$. Find $\\tan\\theta$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac25$", "$\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}$", "$\\dfrac{12}{17}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{21}}{21}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{21}}{17}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{21}}{2}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The centre $C=(4,3)$ is at distance $5$ from the origin $O$ and the radius is $2$. Each tangent makes an angle $\\alpha$ with $OC$ where $\\sin\\alpha=\\frac25$, so $\\tan\\alpha=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}$, and the angle between the tangents is $\\theta=2\\alpha$, which is acute since $\\alpha&lt;\\frac\\pi4$. Then \\[ \\tan\\theta=\\frac{2\\tan\\alpha}{1-\\tan^2\\alpha}=\\frac{4/\\sqrt{21}}{17/21}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{21}}{17} . \\] The coordinate route, requiring $y=mx$ to be at distance $2$ from $C$, gives $12m^2-24m+5=0$ and reaches the same value through $\\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}$, with more work. Option B is $\\tan\\alpha$, the half-angle.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ", "trg"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "C2-11", "paper": "C2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A student solves the inequality $\\sin x\\cos x>\\cos^2x$ for $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$ as follows.<\/p><p><br> &ldquo;When $\\cos x=0$, both sides are $0$, so the inequality fails. Otherwise $\\cos^2x>0$, so I can divide by it without changing the direction of the inequality, giving $\\tan x>1$. Hence the solution set is $\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}&lt;x&lt;\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$ together with $\\tfrac{5\\pi}{4}&lt;x&lt;\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2}$.&rdquo;<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The final answer is correct and the argument is valid.", "The final answer is wrong, because dividing by $\\cos^2x$ may reverse the inequality.", "The final answer is wrong, because solutions with $\\cos x&lt;0$ have been lost.", "The final answer is wrong, because it includes values where $\\cos x=0$.", "The final answer is correct, but the argument is invalid.", "The final answer is wrong; the solution set is $\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}&lt;x&lt;\\tfrac{5\\pi}{4}$."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The student handles $\\cos x=0$ separately, where the inequality reads $0>0$ and fails, and elsewhere divides by $\\cos^2x>0$, which is legitimate and preserves the direction. So the original inequality is equivalent to $\\tan x>1$ with $\\cos x\\ne0$, whose solution set in $[0,2\\pi)$ is exactly the one given. Compare this with dividing $\\sin x>\\cos x$ by $\\cos x$, where the sign of $\\cos x$ matters: the difference is that $\\cos^2x$ is never negative.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "trg"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "D1-16", "paper": "D1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $k$ for which the curve \\[y=x^4-4x^3+kx^2\\] has exactly one stationary point.<\/p>", "opts": ["$k>\\tfrac92$", "$k\\ge\\tfrac92$", "$k>9$", "$k&lt;0$ or $k>\\tfrac92$", "$0&lt;k&lt;\\tfrac92$", "$k\\neq0$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$\\frac{dy}{dx}=2x\\left(2x^2-6x+k\\right)$, so $x=0$ is always stationary and we need the quadratic to add nothing new. If $k>\\tfrac92$ it has no real roots: exactly one stationary point. If $k=\\tfrac92$ it has the double root $x=\\tfrac32$; the gradient is zero there, so it is a second stationary point, a point of inflection. If $k=0$ its roots are $0$ and $3$, and $3$ is new. So the answer is $k>\\tfrac92$. Including $\\tfrac92$ confuses &ldquo;stationary point&rdquo; with &ldquo;turning point&rdquo;.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "quad"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "D1-17", "paper": "D1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>$OABC$ is the parallelogram with vertices $O(0,0)$, $A(6,0)$, $B(8,4)$ and $C(2,4)$. The point $M$ lies on $AB$ with $AM:MB=1:2$, and the line $OM$ meets the diagonal $AC$ at $P$. What fraction of the area of the parallelogram is the area of triangle $OPC$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac27$", "$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac38$", "$\\dfrac25$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>$M=A+\\tfrac13(B-A)=\\left(\\tfrac{20}{3},\\tfrac43\\right)$, so $OM$ is the line $y=\\tfrac15x$, while $AC$ is $x+y=6$. They meet at $P=(5,1)$, three quarters of the way from $O$ to $M$. Triangle $OPC$ has vertices $(0,0)$, $(5,1)$ and $(2,4)$. Enclose it in the rectangle $[0,5]\\times[0,4]$ of area $20$ and remove the three right triangles around it, of areas $\\tfrac52$, $\\tfrac92$ and $4$: the triangle has area $9$. The parallelogram has area $6\\times4=24$, so the fraction is $\\tfrac{9}{24}=\\tfrac38$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "E2-17", "paper": "E2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>A parallelogram whose vertices lie on a circle is a rectangle.<\/li><li>A rhombus whose vertices lie on a circle is a square.<\/li><li>A trapezium whose vertices lie on a circle is isosceles.<\/li><li>A kite whose vertices lie on a circle has two right angles.<\/li><li>A quadrilateral whose vertices lie on a circle and whose diagonals are perpendicular is a kite.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I, II, III and IV only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III, IV and V", "I, II and IV only", "III and V only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to $180^\\circ$. I: a parallelogram's opposite angles are equal, so each is $90^\\circ$. II follows from I, since a rectangle that is a rhombus is a square. III: the angles on each parallel side are supplementary to their neighbours and so equal in pairs, which makes the trapezium isosceles. IV: a kite has one pair of equal opposite angles, so each is $90^\\circ$. V fails: many cyclic quadrilaterals have perpendicular diagonals without two pairs of equal adjacent sides.<\/p>", "tags": ["circ", "prf"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "E2-18", "paper": "E2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Three arguments are offered for the claim <em>no positive integer $n$ makes $n^2+n+1$ divisible by $5$<\/em>.<\/p><p><br> <strong>I.<\/strong> For $n=1,2,3,4,5$ the values are $3,7,13,21,31$, none of which is divisible by $5$.<br> <strong>II.<\/strong> Every $n$ is congruent to $0,1,2,3$ or $4$ modulo $5$, and for these $n^2+n+1\\equiv1,3,2,3,1\\pmod5$ respectively.<br> <strong>III.<\/strong> $n^2+n+1=n(n+1)+1$, and the product of two consecutive integers ends in $0$, $2$ or $6$, so $n^2+n+1$ ends in $1$, $3$ or $7$.<\/p><p><br> Which of these are valid proofs of the claim?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I, II and III", "II and III only", "none of them"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I checks five cases and says nothing about $n=6$ or beyond, so it is not a proof. II covers every integer through its remainder, since $n^2+n+1$ modulo $5$ depends only on $n$ modulo $5$: a complete proof. III is also complete: the last digit of $n(n+1)$ depends only on the last digit of $n$, and the ten cases give only $0$, $2$ and $6$. A last digit of $1$, $3$ or $7$ rules out divisibility by $5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "mod"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "F1-19", "paper": "F1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Find the minimum value of \\[\\frac{x^2+3}{\\sqrt{x^2+2}}\\] over all real $x$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac32$", "$\\sqrt3$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt2}{2}$", "$\\dfrac52$", "$2\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Put $u=\\sqrt{x^2+2}\\ge\\sqrt2$. The expression is $\\frac{u^2+1}{u}=u+\\frac1u$. The inequality $u+\\frac1u\\ge2$ is useless here: equality needs $u=1$, which is not allowed. Since $u+\\frac1u$ is increasing for $u\\ge1$, the minimum is at $u=\\sqrt2$, that is $x=0$, giving $\\sqrt2+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt2}{2}$. Option C is the unattainable bound.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "diff"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "F2-18", "paper": "F2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>A function $f$ satisfies $f(4-x)=f(x)$ and $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for every real $x$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(4)=0$<\/li><li>$f(x+8)=f(x)$ for every $x$<\/li><li>$f(2)=0$<\/li><li>$f(6)=-f(2)$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "II only", "I, II, III and IV", "I, II and IV only", "II and IV only", "I and IV only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Oddness gives $f(0)=0$, and then $f(4)=f(4-0)=f(0)=0$: I. Next $f(x+4)=f(4-(x+4))=f(-x)=-f(x)$, and applying this twice gives $f(x+8)=f(x)$: II. IV: $f(6)=f(4-6)=f(-2)=-f(2)$. III fails: $f(x)=\\sin\\frac{\\pi x}{4}$ satisfies both conditions and has $f(2)=1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["sym"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "B1-20", "paper": "B1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>Let $f(x) = x^3 + x$, and let $f^{-1}$ denote the inverse function of $f$. Find the value of \\[ \\int_0^{10} f^{-1}(x)\\,dx. \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$10$", "$14$", "$16$", "$20$", "$34$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Do not try to invert $f$. Since $f$ is increasing and $f(2) = 10$, the graph of $f^{-1}$ on $[0,10]$ is the reflection of the graph of $f$ on $[0,2]$ in the line $y = x$, and together the two regions fill the $2\\times 10$ rectangle: \\[ \\int_0^{10} f^{-1}(x)\\,dx = 2\\cdot 10 - \\int_0^2 (x^3 + x)\\,dx = 20 - (4 + 2) = 14. \\] Option A is $\\int_0^2 f$, the wrong piece of the rectangle.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "B2-14", "paper": "B2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>Let $A$, $B$, $C$ be the angles of a triangle. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\sin A + \\sin B > \\sin C$<\/li><li>$\\cos A + \\cos B > 0$<\/li><li>$\\sin A \\sin B > \\cos C$<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true for every triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: by the sine rule the sides are proportional to the sines of the opposite angles, so $\\sin A + \\sin B > \\sin C$ is the triangle inequality $a + b > c$ in disguise. True. II: since $A + B &lt; \\pi$, we have $B &lt; \\pi - A$, and cosine is decreasing on $(0,\\pi)$, so $\\cos B > \\cos(\\pi - A) = -\\cos A$. True. III: $\\cos C = -\\cos(A + B) = \\sin A \\sin B - \\cos A \\cos B$, so $\\sin A \\sin B > \\cos C$ is equivalent to $\\cos A \\cos B > 0$, which fails whenever one of $A$, $B$ is obtuse. For instance $A = 120^\\circ$, $B = C = 30^\\circ$ gives $\\sin A \\sin B = \\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4} &lt; \\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2} = \\cos C$. False. The answer is I and II. Each statement is a triangle fact wearing trigonometric clothing: the triangle inequality, the angle sum, and the sign of $\\cos A \\cos B$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "B2-20", "paper": "B2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>A pile contains $2026$ stones. Two players move alternately; a move consists of removing either $2$ or $3$ stones from the pile. A player who cannot move loses. With best play by both sides, which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["The second player wins.", "The first player wins, and removing $2$ stones is their only winning first move.", "The first player wins, and removing $3$ stones is their only winning first move.", "The first player wins, and removing either $2$ or $3$ stones is a winning first move.", "The result depends on choices made later in the game."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Classify pile sizes as losing (L, the player to move loses) or winning (W). $0$ and $1$ are L, since no move is possible. Then $2 \\to 0$ and $3 \\to 1$ are W; $4 \\to 1$ is W; $5$ can only reach $3$ and $2$, both W, so $5$ is L; $6$ reaches $4$ and $3$, both W, so L; $7 \\to 5$ is W; $8 \\to 6$ is W; $9 \\to 6$ is W; $10$ reaches $8$ and $7$, both W, so L; $11$ reaches $9$ and $8$, so L. The pattern is periodic with period $5$: the losing sizes are those leaving remainder $0$ or $1$ on division by $5$. Since $2026 = 5 \\cdot 405 + 1$, the starting position is losing for the player to move: the second player wins. Options B to D assume the first player has a good move; option E misunderstands what best play means.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["game"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "C1-14", "paper": "C1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^3+3x$, and let $f^{-1}$ be the inverse function of $f$. Find \\[ \\int_4^{14} f^{-1}(x)\\,dx . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{33}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{63}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{79}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{95}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{111}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{129}{4}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$f$ is increasing with $f(1)=4$ and $f(2)=14$. On $[4,14]$, the graph of $f^{-1}$ is the reflection of the graph of $f$ on $[1,2]$, and the two areas fill the $L$-shaped region between the rectangles $[0,2]\\times[0,14]$ and $[0,1]\\times[0,4]$: \\[ \\int_4^{14}f^{-1}(x)\\,dx=2\\cdot14-1\\cdot4-\\int_1^2(x^3+3x)\\,dx=24-\\left(\\tfrac{15}{4}+\\tfrac92\\right)=24-\\tfrac{33}{4}=\\tfrac{63}{4} . \\] Option C forgets to subtract the small rectangle $1\\cdot4$: the formula is $bf(b)-af(a)-\\int_a^bf$, and the $af(a)$ term is only zero when $a=0$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "C2-6", "paper": "C2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Consider the statement <div class=\"c\"> for all real $x$, if $\\sin x>0$ or $\\cos x>0$, then $\\sin x+\\cos x>0$. <\/div> How many of the following values of $x$ are counterexamples to this statement? \\[ \\frac{\\pi}{6},\\qquad \\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\qquad \\frac{5\\pi}{6},\\qquad \\frac{7\\pi}{6},\\qquad \\frac{5\\pi}{3},\\qquad \\frac{7\\pi}{4} \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>A counterexample needs the hypothesis true and the conclusion false. At $\\frac\\pi6$ and $\\frac{2\\pi}3$ the hypothesis holds and $\\sin x+\\cos x>0$. At $\\frac{5\\pi}6$ the hypothesis holds ($\\sin x=\\frac12$) and $\\sin x+\\cos x=\\frac{1-\\sqrt3}{2}&lt;0$: a counterexample. At $\\frac{7\\pi}6$ both are negative, so the hypothesis fails and this is not a counterexample, however negative the sum. At $\\frac{5\\pi}3$, $\\cos x=\\frac12>0$ and the sum is $\\frac{1-\\sqrt3}{2}&lt;0$: a counterexample. At $\\frac{7\\pi}4$, $\\cos x>0$ and the sum is exactly $0$, which is not $>0$: a counterexample. Three in total.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "trg"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "D2-17", "paper": "D2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A student solves $x^4-3x^3+4x^2-3x+1=0$ over the real numbers.<\/p><p><br> (I) $x=0$ is not a solution, so divide by $x^2$: $x^2+\\dfrac1{x^2}-3\\left(x+\\dfrac1x\\right)+4=0$.<br> (II) With $t=x+\\dfrac1x$, we have $x^2+\\dfrac1{x^2}=t^2-2$, so $t^2-3t+2=0$ and $t=1$ or $t=2$.<br> (III) For every non-zero real $x$, $x+\\dfrac1x\\ge2$, so $t=1$ is impossible.<br> (IV) $t=2$ gives $x^2-2x+1=0$, so $x=1$.<br> (V) Hence the only real solution is $x=1$.<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The argument is correct.", "Line III is false, but the conclusion is correct.", "Line III is false, and the conclusion is wrong.", "Line II contains an algebraic error.", "Line IV is wrong: $x+\\frac1x=2$ has two real solutions.", "Line I is wrong, since $x=0$ must be checked separately."], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Line III is false as stated: for $x&lt;0$, $x+\\frac1x\\le-2$, for instance $-2$ at $x=-1$. The correct fact is $\\left|x+\\frac1x\\right|\\ge2$. That still rules out $t=1$, and directly, $x^2-x+1=0$ has no real roots. So the conclusion stands: the polynomial is $(x-1)^2(x^2-x+1)$, whose only real root is $1$. The answer is B.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "poly"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "E1-18", "paper": "E1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Let $a>0$. For which values of $a$ is the greatest value of $f(x)=x^3-3a^2x$ on the interval $0\\le x\\le2$ equal to $0$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a\\ge2$", "$a\\ge1$", "$a\\ge\\sqrt2$", "$0&lt;a\\le\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt3}$", "$a\\ge\\dfrac43$", "$a\\ge\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt3}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$f'(x)=3(x^2-a^2)$, so $f$ decreases on $[0,a]$ and increases after $a$. On $[0,2]$ the maximum is therefore at an endpoint: it is $\\max\\left(f(0),f(2)\\right)=\\max\\left(0,\\,8-6a^2\\right)$. This equals $0$ exactly when $8-6a^2\\le0$, that is $a\\ge\\tfrac{2}{\\sqrt3}$. It holds even when $a>2$, since then $f$ decreases on the whole interval.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "B2-9", "paper": "B2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>How many ordered triples $(x, y, z)$ of integers satisfy $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 2023$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$8$", "$24$", "$48$", "$96$", "Infinitely many"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>A square leaves remainder $0$, $1$ or $4$ on division by $8$. Adding three such remainders can give $0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6$ but never $7$ (the largest available combinations are $4+4+4 \\equiv 4$, $4+4+1 \\equiv 1$, $4+1+1 = 6$, $1+1+1 = 3$). Since $2023 = 8 \\cdot 252 + 7$, there are no solutions at all. No searching is needed, which is the point: the options B to E are the counts one gets after finding one solution and multiplying by sign and permutation symmetries, and there is no solution to start from.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "C1-6", "paper": "C1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>For a real number $k$, say that the equation \\[ \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(2\\sin x)^n=k \\] is <em>solvable<\/em> if there is a real $x$ for which the series converges and its sum is $k$. For how many of the values $k=\\tfrac13,\\ \\tfrac12,\\ \\tfrac23,\\ 2,\\ 4$ is the equation solvable?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The series is geometric with ratio $2\\sin x$, so it converges only when $|2\\sin x|&lt;1$, and then its sum is $\\frac{1}{1-2\\sin x}$. Setting this equal to $k$ gives $2\\sin x=1-\\frac1k$, and convergence requires $\\left|1-\\frac1k\\right|&lt;1$, that is $0&lt;\\frac1k&lt;2$, or $k>\\frac12$. Any such $k$ gives $|\\sin x|&lt;\\frac12$, so a real $x$ exists. Hence $k=\\frac23,2,4$ work and $k=\\frac13,\\frac12$ do not: three values. The trap is $k=\\frac13$, where solving the formula gives $\\sin x=-1$, a perfectly good value of sine at which the series diverges.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "trg"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "C2-17", "paper": "C2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>For real numbers $a$ and $b$, consider the four conditions \\[ (1)\\ a+b>0,\\qquad (2)\\ ab>0,\\qquad (3)\\ a>0,\\qquad (4)\\ b>0 . \\] How many of the six pairs of these conditions are such that the two conditions together are equivalent to all four conditions holding?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>All four together are equivalent to $a>0$ and $b>0$. Test the six pairs: (1)(2): $ab>0$ means same sign, and a positive sum makes both positive; equivalent. (3)(4): trivially equivalent. (2)(3): $a>0$ and $ab>0$ give $b>0$; equivalent. (2)(4): likewise. (1)(3): $a=5$, $b=-1$ satisfies both but not (4). (1)(4): symmetric. So four pairs. Every single condition is also individually removable here, which shows that &ldquo;removable one at a time&rdquo; and &ldquo;which small subsets suffice&rdquo; are different questions.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "ineq"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "D2-18", "paper": "D2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>The function $f(x)=x^3+ax^2+bx$ is called <em>increasing<\/em> if $f(x_1)&lt;f(x_2)$ whenever $x_1&lt;x_2$. Which condition on $a$ and $b$ is necessary and sufficient for $f$ to be increasing?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a^2&lt;3b$", "$b>0$", "$b\\ge0$", "$a^2\\le b$", "$a^2\\le3b$", "$a=0$ and $b\\ge0$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>$f'(x)=3x^2+2ax+b$. If $a^2&lt;3b$, $f'>0$ everywhere. If $a^2=3b$, $f'$ vanishes at one point and is positive elsewhere, and $f$ is still increasing, just as $x^3$ is. If $a^2>3b$, $f'$ is negative between its roots, so $f$ decreases there. The condition is $a^2\\le3b$. Option A demands $f'>0$ everywhere, which is sufficient but not necessary.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "diff"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "E1-19", "paper": "E1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Find the total area of the regions enclosed between the curve $y=\\dfrac{x-2}{\\sqrt x}$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=1$ and $x=4$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{16\\sqrt2-18}{3}$", "$\\dfrac23$", "$\\dfrac{8\\sqrt2-10}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{8\\sqrt2-8}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{16\\sqrt2-10}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{10}{3}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The curve is below the axis on $[1,2)$ and above it on $(2,4]$. With $F(x)=\\tfrac23x^{3/2}-4x^{1/2}$: $F(1)=-\\tfrac{10}{3}$, $F(2)=-\\tfrac{8\\sqrt2}{3}$ and $F(4)=-\\tfrac83$. The two areas are $F(1)-F(2)=\\tfrac{8\\sqrt2-10}{3}$ and $F(4)-F(2)=\\tfrac{8\\sqrt2-8}{3}$, totalling $\\tfrac{16\\sqrt2-18}{3}$. The signed integral from $1$ to $4$ is $\\tfrac23$, option B.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "E2-19", "paper": "E2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>An arithmetic progression $u_1,u_2,\\dots$ has non-zero common difference, and its partial sums satisfy $S_{10}=S_{20}$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$S_{30}=0$<\/li><li>$u_{15}+u_{16}=0$<\/li><li>$u_{15}=0$<\/li><li>$S_{15}$ is either the largest or the smallest of all the $S_n$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and III only", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$S_n=An^2+Bn$ with $A\\neq0$. $S_{10}=S_{20}$ gives $300A+10B=0$, so $S_n=A(n^2-30n)$. I: $S_{30}=0$. IV: $n^2-30n$ is least at $n=15$, so $S_{15}$ is the extreme value. II: $u_{11}+\\dots+u_{20}=S_{20}-S_{10}=0$, and these ten terms pair up as five pairs each equal to $u_{15}+u_{16}$. III fails: in $u_n=31-2n$ we have $S_{10}=S_{20}=200$ but $u_{15}=1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "F2-19", "paper": "F2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>For an integer $k$ with $-10\\le k\\le10$, let $P$ be the statement &ldquo;$x^2-kx+1=0$ has real roots&rdquo; and $Q$ the statement &ldquo;$x^2-x+k=0$ has real roots&rdquo;. For how many such $k$ is exactly one of $P$ and $Q$ true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$7$", "$9$", "$10$", "$11$", "$13$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>$P$ holds when $k^2\\ge4$, that is $k\\le-2$ or $k\\ge2$. $Q$ holds when $1-4k\\ge0$, that is $k\\le\\tfrac14$, so $k\\le0$ for integers. Exactly one holds when $P$ is true and $Q$ false, which is $k=2,\\dots,10$ (nine values), or $Q$ is true and $P$ false, which is $k=-1,0$ (two values). That is $11$. For $k\\le-2$ both hold, and for $k=1$ neither does.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "quad"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "B2-11", "paper": "B2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>For a convex quadrilateral, the condition <div class=\"c\"> &ldquo;the midpoints of the four sides are the vertices of a square&rdquo; <\/div> is <span class=\"blank\">________<\/span> for the quadrilateral to be a square.<\/p>", "opts": ["necessary but not sufficient", "sufficient but not necessary", "necessary and sufficient", "neither necessary nor sufficient"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The midpoints of the sides of any quadrilateral form a parallelogram whose sides are parallel to the diagonals and half their length. That parallelogram is a square exactly when the diagonals are equal in length and perpendicular. A square has such diagonals, so the condition is necessary. It is not sufficient: the quadrilateral with vertices $(-2,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$, $(0,-3)$ is convex, has perpendicular diagonals both of length $4$, and is a kite, not a square. The condition is necessary but not sufficient.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "circ"], "rating": 6.7}, {"id": "C2-3", "paper": "C2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are logically equivalent to &ldquo;if $P$, then $Q$ or $R$&rdquo;? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>If $P$ and not $Q$, then $R$.<\/li><li>If not $Q$ and not $R$, then not $P$.<\/li><li>Either &ldquo;if $P$ then $Q$&rdquo; is true, or &ldquo;if $P$ then $R$&rdquo; is true (or both).<\/li><li>&ldquo;If $P$ then $Q$&rdquo; and &ldquo;if $P$ then $R$&rdquo; are both true.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "II and III only", "I and III only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Write each as a disjunction. The original is (not $P$) or $Q$ or $R$. I is not$(P$ and not $Q)$ or $R$, which is (not $P$) or $Q$ or $R$: equivalent. II is the contrapositive of the original: equivalent. III is ((not $P$) or $Q$) or ((not $P$) or $R$), which simplifies to (not $P$) or $Q$ or $R$: equivalent. IV is ((not $P$) or $Q$) and ((not $P$) or $R$), which is (not $P$) or ($Q$ and $R$): stronger, and false when $P$ and $Q$ are true and $R$ is false.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 6.7}, {"id": "B1-11", "paper": "B1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>There are exactly two straight lines that are tangent to both of the curves \\[ y = x^2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y = -x^2 + 6x - 10. \\] Find the product of the gradients of these two lines.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-4$", "$-2$", "$-1$", "$1$", "$2$", "$4$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The tangent to $y = x^2$ at $x = a$ is $y = 2ax - a^2$; the tangent to the second curve at $x = b$ is $y = (6-2b)x + b^2 - 10$. Matching gradients and intercepts, $a = 3 - b$ and $-(3-b)^2 = b^2 - 10$, which reduces to $2b^2 - 6b - 1 = 0$. The two common tangents correspond to the two roots $b_1, b_2$, with gradients $6 - 2b_i$, so \\[ (6-2b_1)(6-2b_2) = 36 - 12(b_1+b_2) + 4b_1b_2 = 36 - 36 - 2 = -2 \\] by Vieta, without solving for $b$ at all. (The gradients are $3 \\pm \\sqrt{11}$.)<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["vie", "poly"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "C1-9", "paper": "C1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $a$ for which the equation \\[ x^3-3ax^2+4=0 \\] has exactly one real solution.<\/p>", "opts": ["$a&lt;1$", "$a&lt;1$ and $a\\ne0$", "$0&lt;a&lt;1$", "$a>1$", "$a\\ne1$", "$a&lt;-1$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $g(x)=x^3-3ax^2+4$, so $g'(x)=3x(x-2a)$. If $a=0$, $g$ is increasing and has exactly one root. Otherwise the stationary values are $g(0)=4$ and $g(2a)=4-4a^3$, and there is exactly one real root precisely when these have the same sign, that is $4-4a^3>0$, or $a&lt;1$. At $a=1$, $g(x)=(x-2)^2(x+1)$ has two distinct roots, so $a=1$ is excluded. Hence $a&lt;1$, including $a=0$. Option B is the answer to a different question, the one that also demands two stationary points.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["root"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "C2-13", "paper": "C2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>For a positive integer $n$, consider the four statements \\[ (1)\\ 4\\mid n,\\qquad (2)\\ 6\\mid n,\\qquad (3)\\ 12\\mid n,\\qquad (4)\\ 8\\mid n . \\] Let $T$ be the set of positive integers satisfying all four. A statement is called <em>removable<\/em> if deleting it from the list does not change the set of integers satisfying all the remaining statements. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["Each of the four statements is removable.", "Only statement (4) is not removable.", "Only statement (1) is not removable.", "Only statements (3) and (4) are not removable.", "No statement is removable.", "Only statements (2) and (4) are not removable."], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>All four together mean $8\\mid n$ and $3\\mid n$, that is $24\\mid n$. Remove (1): $6,12,8\\mid n$ still gives $24\\mid n$. Remove (2): $4,12,8$ gives $24$. Remove (3): $\\operatorname{lcm}(4,6,8)=24$. Remove (4): $\\operatorname{lcm}(4,6,12)=12$, so $n=12$ now slips in. Only (4) is not removable. Note that removability is about one statement at a time: (1), (2) and (3) are each removable, but not all three together.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "mod"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "D1-18", "paper": "D1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>The line $y=kx$, where $0&lt;k&lt;4$, divides the finite region enclosed by the curve $y=x(4-x)$ and the $x$-axis into two parts of equal area. Find $k$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$4-2\\sqrt2$", "$4-2\\sqrt[3]{4}$", "$2$", "$4-\\sqrt[3]{16}$", "$\\dfrac83$", "$4-\\sqrt[3]{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The whole region has area $\\int_0^4(4x-x^2)\\,dx=\\tfrac{32}{3}$. The line meets the curve again where $4-x=k$. The part between the curve and the line has area \\[\\int_0^{4-k}\\left((4-k)x-x^2\\right)dx=\\frac{(4-k)^3}{6},\\] the familiar $\\tfrac{w^3}{6}$ for the segment of $y=x(w-x)$. Setting this equal to $\\tfrac{16}{3}$ gives $(4-k)^3=32$, so $4-k=\\sqrt[3]{32}=2\\sqrt[3]4$ and $k=4-2\\sqrt[3]{4}$. Using a square root instead of a cube root gives option A.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "H2-1", "paper": "H2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>For an arithmetic progression $u_1,u_2,u_3,\\dots$, the condition &ldquo;$u_1$ and $u_3$ are both integers&rdquo; is <span class=\"blank\">________<\/span> for &ldquo;every term of the progression is an integer&rdquo;.<\/p>", "opts": ["necessary but not sufficient", "sufficient but not necessary", "necessary and sufficient", "neither necessary nor sufficient"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>If every term is an integer, then certainly $u_1$ and $u_3$ are: necessary. It is not sufficient, because the common difference is $\\frac{u_3-u_1}{2}$, which may be a half-integer: $1,\\tfrac32,2,\\tfrac52,\\dots$ has $u_1$ and $u_3$ integers but $u_2$ not. The condition &ldquo;$u_1$ and $u_2$ are integers&rdquo; would have been necessary and sufficient.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "ser"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "H2-2", "paper": "H2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Consider the statement: <em>for every real number $x$, if $x^2>x$ then $x>1$.<\/em> How many of the following values of $x$ are counterexamples to it? \\[-1,\\qquad -\\tfrac12,\\qquad 0,\\qquad \\tfrac12,\\qquad 1,\\qquad 2\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>A counterexample needs $x^2>x$ and $x\\le1$. $x^2>x$ holds exactly when $x&lt;0$ or $x>1$, so the counterexamples are the negative numbers: $-1$ and $-\\tfrac12$. For $0$, $\\tfrac12$ and $1$ the hypothesis fails, so they are not counterexamples, and $2$ satisfies the conclusion. Two in all.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ineq"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "B2-8", "paper": "B2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ with the following property: among any $n$ integers there are two whose sum or whose difference is divisible by $12$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$7$", "$8$", "$12$", "$13$", "$24$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Two integers have sum or difference divisible by $12$ exactly when their remainders on division by $12$ are equal or add to $12$. Group the remainders into the classes $\\{0\\}$, $\\{6\\}$, $\\{1,11\\}$, $\\{2,10\\}$, $\\{3,9\\}$, $\\{4,8\\}$, $\\{5,7\\}$: seven classes, and two numbers from the same class always work. So $8$ integers force a pair. Seven can avoid it: the remainders $0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6$ are all distinct and no two of them sum to $12$. The answer is $8$. Option B is the count of classes, one short of the pigeonhole answer.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["pig", "mod"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "C1-15", "paper": "C1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=\\tfrac12\\left(x^3+x\\right)$. Find the area of the finite region in the first quadrant bounded by the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=f^{-1}(x)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{16}$", "$\\dfrac18$", "$\\dfrac16$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>$f$ is increasing and odd, and $f(x)=x$ exactly when $x^3=x$, so the graphs of $f$ and $f^{-1}$ meet the line $y=x$, and each other, at $0$ and $1$ in the first quadrant. On $(0,1)$, $f(x)&lt;x$. The region between $f$ and $f^{-1}$ is symmetric about $y=x$, so its area is twice the area between $y=x$ and $y=f(x)$: \\[ 2\\int_0^1\\left(x-\\tfrac12(x^3+x)\\right)dx=\\int_0^1(x-x^3)\\,dx=\\tfrac14 . \\] No inverse function needs to be found. Option B is the area between $f$ and the line $y=x$ alone.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "B1-18", "paper": "B1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>The points $A(1,2)$ and $B(5,2)$ are fixed, and $C$ is a point such that $\\angle ACB = 45^\\circ$. Find the maximum possible area of triangle $ABC$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2 + 2\\sqrt2$", "$4\\sqrt2$", "$4 + 2\\sqrt2$", "$4 + 4\\sqrt2$", "$8$", "$8\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The angle at $C$ is fixed, so $C$ lies on an arc of a circle through $A$ and $B$, and the sine rule in the circumcircle gives $2R = \\dfrac{AB}{\\sin 45^\\circ} = \\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt2/2}$, so $R = 2\\sqrt2$. The centre is at distance $\\sqrt{R^2 - 2^2} = 2$ from $AB$. Since $45^\\circ$ is acute, $C$ is on the major arc, on the far side of the centre from $AB$, and the largest height is $2 + 2\\sqrt2$ at the midpoint of that arc. The maximum area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot 4\\cdot(2+2\\sqrt2) = 4 + 4\\sqrt2$. Option A is the height, not the area.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "B2-15", "paper": "B2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Each cell of a $6 \\times 6$ grid is coloured black or white. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>Every row contains more black cells than white cells, and every column contains more white cells than black cells.<\/li><li>Every row contains an odd number of black cells, and every column contains an odd number of black cells.<\/li><li>Every $2 \\times 2$ block of four adjacent cells contains exactly one black cell.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements can be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I is impossible by counting black cells two ways: each row has at least $4$ black cells, giving at least $24$ in total, while each column has at most $2$, giving at most $12$. II is possible: colour the six cells on a diagonal black, so every row and column has exactly one. III is possible: colour black exactly the cells whose row number and column number are both even; any two consecutive rows contain exactly one even row and any two consecutive columns exactly one even column, so every $2 \\times 2$ block contains exactly one black cell. The answer is II and III. Note that for II the parity check passes (six odd rows and six odd columns both give an even total) but passing a parity check is not a construction; the diagonal is.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["col", "two"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "C1-11", "paper": "C1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>The function $F$ is defined for all real $x$ by \\[ F(x)=\\int_0^x (x-t)\\sin t\\,dt . \\] Find $F(\\pi)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$\\pi-2$", "$2$", "$3$", "$\\pi$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The variable $x$ appears inside the integrand, so take it outside first: \\[ F(x)=x\\int_0^x\\sin t\\,dt-\\int_0^x t\\sin t\\,dt=x(1-\\cos x)-(\\sin x-x\\cos x)=x-\\sin x . \\] So $F(\\pi)=\\pi$. The integration by parts is routine; the step that matters is noticing that $x$ is a constant as far as the $t$-integration is concerned and pulling it out.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "sym"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "D2-19", "paper": "D2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Which of the following conditions on the real numbers $b$ and $c$ is necessary and sufficient for the equation $x^2+bx+c=0$ to have two distinct positive roots?<\/p>", "opts": ["$b&lt;0$ and $c>0$", "$b^2>4c$ and $c>0$", "$b&lt;0$ and $b^2>4c$", "$c>0$ and $b&lt;-2\\sqrt c$", "$b&lt;-2\\sqrt c$", "$b&lt;0$, $c>0$ and $b^2\\ge4c$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Two distinct positive roots means distinct real roots ($b^2>4c$), a positive product ($c>0$) and a positive sum ($-b>0$). With $c>0$, the pair $b&lt;0$ and $b^2>4c$ is the single condition $b&lt;-2\\sqrt c$, so D is exactly right. E alone allows $c=0$, which gives the roots $0$ and $-b$. A allows complex roots, B allows two negative roots, C allows $c&lt;0$ with roots of opposite signs, and F allows a repeated root.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "quad"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "G1-1", "paper": "G1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>An equilateral triangle $T_1$ has side length $1$. A circle $C_1$ is inscribed in $T_1$. An equilateral triangle $T_2$ is then inscribed in $C_1$, with its three vertices on $C_1$; a circle $C_2$ is inscribed in $T_2$; an equilateral triangle $T_3$ is inscribed in $C_2$; and the process continues without end, producing circles $C_1,C_2,C_3,\\dots$. Find the total area of all the circles $C_1,C_2,C_3,\\dots$<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>An equilateral triangle of side $s$ has inradius $\\tfrac{s}{2\\sqrt3}$ and circumradius $\\tfrac{s}{\\sqrt3}$, twice the inradius. $T_2$ is inscribed in $C_1$, so the circumradius of $T_2$ is the inradius of $T_1$; hence the inradius of $T_2$ is half the inradius of $T_1$. Each circle therefore has half the radius, and a quarter of the area, of the one before. $C_1$ has radius $\\tfrac{1}{2\\sqrt3}$ and area $\\tfrac{\\pi}{12}$, so the total is $\\frac{\\pi/12}{1-1/4}=\\frac{\\pi}{9}$. Option A is $C_1$ alone.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "trg"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "G1-2", "paper": "G1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>In the expansion of $(1+2x)^n$ in ascending powers of $x$, where $n$ is a positive integer, there are three consecutive terms whose coefficients are in the ratio $1:4:10$. If these are the terms in $x^{r-1}$, $x^r$ and $x^{r+1}$, find $n+r$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$7$", "$9$", "$10$", "$11$", "$13$", "$14$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The coefficient of $x^k$ is $\\binom nk2^k$, so consecutive ratios are $\\frac{c_r}{c_{r-1}}=\\frac{2(n-r+1)}{r}$ and $\\frac{c_{r+1}}{c_r}=\\frac{2(n-r)}{r+1}$. Setting these equal to $4$ and to $\\tfrac{10}{4}$ gives $n-r+1=2r$, so $n=3r-1$, and $4(n-r)=5(r+1)$. Substituting, $4(2r-1)=5r+5$, so $r=3$ and $n=8$. Check: $\\binom82 2^2=112$, $\\binom83 2^3=448$ and $\\binom84 2^4=1120$ are in the ratio $1:4:10$. So $n+r=11$.<\/p>", "tags": ["bin"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "G2-1", "paper": "G2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>A student solves the equation \\[\\sqrt{2x+3}-\\sqrt{x-2}=2\\] by rearranging to $\\sqrt{2x+3}=2+\\sqrt{x-2}$, squaring to obtain $x+1=4\\sqrt{x-2}$, and squaring again to reach $x^2-14x+33=0$, so that $x=3$ or $x=11$. The student then writes: &ldquo;Squaring can introduce solutions that do not satisfy the original equation, so the larger root must be rejected, and the only solution is $x=3$.&rdquo; Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The student is right: $x=3$ is the only solution.", "Only $x=11$ satisfies the original equation.", "Neither value satisfies the original equation.", "Both $x=3$ and $x=11$ satisfy the original equation, so the student\\textquoteright s rejection is wrong.", "The first squaring is invalid, so the quadratic is wrong.", "There is a third solution, lost in the second squaring."], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Check both values directly. $x=3$: $\\sqrt9-\\sqrt1=3-1=2$. $x=11$: $\\sqrt{25}-\\sqrt9=5-3=2$. Both work. Squaring can introduce false solutions, but that is a reason to <em>check<\/em> candidates, not to reject one by size. Here both sides were non-negative at each squaring for both values, so nothing extraneous appeared.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "ineq"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "G2-2", "paper": "G2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>A positive integer $n\\le100$ is the subject of four statements:<\/p><p><br> A: $n$ is divisible by exactly one of the numbers $2$ and $3$.<br> B: $n+1$ is a perfect square.<br> C: $n$ has exactly four positive divisors.<br> D: the digits of $n$ sum to $8$.<\/p><p><br> Exactly three of the four statements are true, and statement C is false. Find $n$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$80$", "$35$", "$62$", "$99$", "there is no such $n$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>With C false, A, B and D are all true. B makes $n+1$ a square: $n\\in\\{3,8,15,24,35,48,63,80,99\\}$. D keeps only those with digit sum $8$: $8$, $35$ and $80$. A: $35$ is divisible by neither $2$ nor $3$, so it fails. $8$ satisfies A, but it has exactly four divisors ($1,2,4,8$), so C would be true. $80$ satisfies A, and it has ten divisors, so C is false as required. So $n=80$.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "mod"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "G2-3", "paper": "G2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>For positive integers $a$, $b$ and $c$, which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>If $a$ divides $b^2$ and $a$ divides $c^2$, then $a$ divides $bc$.<\/li><li>If $a$ divides $bc$, then $a$ divides $b$ or $a$ divides $c$.<\/li><li>If $a$ divides $b$ and $a$ divides $c$, then $a^2$ divides $bc$.<\/li><li>If $a^2$ divides $b^2$, then $a$ divides $b$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and III only", "III and IV only", "I, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "III only", "II, III and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: $a^2$ divides $b^2c^2=(bc)^2$, and comparing the power of each prime shows $a\\mid bc$. II fails: $6\\mid2\\cdot3$ but $6\\nmid2$ and $6\\nmid3$. III: $b=a\\beta$ and $c=a\\gamma$ give $bc=a^2\\beta\\gamma$. IV: each prime occurs in $a^2$ to twice the power it has in $a$, so $a^2\\mid b^2$ forces the powers in $a$ to be at most those in $b$. So I, III and IV. Statement I is the one that looks false and is not.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "prf"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "H2-3", "paper": "H2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A student claims that <em>if $n^2$ is divisible by $8$, then $n$ is divisible by $8$<\/em>, and argues: &ldquo;Suppose $8\\nmid n$. Write $n=8q+r$ with $1\\le r\\le7$. Then $n^2=64q^2+16qr+r^2$, and $r^2$ is one of $1,4,9,16,25,36,49$, none of which is divisible by $8$. So $8\\nmid n^2$.&rdquo; Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The argument is valid and the claim is true.", "The claim is true, but the argument should use remainders on division by $4$.", "The argument is invalid because $64q^2+16qr$ need not be divisible by $8$.", "The claim is false, and the argument fails because $16$ is in the list and is divisible by $8$.", "The claim is false only when $n$ is odd.", "The argument proves the converse of the claim."], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The setup is sound: $64q^2+16qr$ is divisible by $8$, so $8\\mid n^2$ exactly when $8\\mid r^2$. But the student misreads the list: $r=4$ gives $r^2=16$, which is divisible by $8$. So the argument fails, and the claim is false: $n=4$ has $n^2=16$ divisible by $8$ while $8\\nmid4$. What is true is that $8\\mid n^2$ forces $4\\mid n$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "mod"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "H2-4", "paper": "H2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $x$ for which \\[x&lt;\\frac1x&lt;x^2 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$0&lt;x&lt;1$", "$x>1$", "$-1&lt;x&lt;0$", "$x&lt;-1$ or $x>1$", "$-1&lt;x&lt;0$ or $x>1$", "$x&lt;-1$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Split by sign, since multiplying by $x$ reverses inequalities when $x&lt;0$. For $x>0$: $x&lt;\\frac1x$ gives $x&lt;1$, while $\\frac1x&lt;x^2$ gives $x^3>1$, that is $x>1$; these are incompatible. For $x&lt;0$: $\\frac1x&lt;0&lt;x^2$ holds automatically, and $x&lt;\\frac1x$, multiplied by the negative $x$, gives $x^2>1$, so $x&lt;-1$. The solution set is $x&lt;-1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "B1-5", "paper": "B1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Positive real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy $x + y = 1$. Find the minimum value of \\[ \\Bigl(x + \\frac1x\\Bigr)^2 + \\Bigl(y + \\frac1y\\Bigr)^2 . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$\\dfrac{25}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{2}$", "$13$", "$15$", "$25$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Put $u = x + \\frac1x$ and $v = y + \\frac1y$. Then $u + v = 1 + \\frac1x + \\frac1y \\ge 1 + \\frac{4}{x+y} = 5$, the inequality being $\\frac1x + \\frac1y \\ge \\frac{4}{x+y}$ (AM--HM, or clear denominators to $(x-y)^2 \\ge 0$). Then \\[ u^2 + v^2 \\ge \\frac{(u+v)^2}{2} \\ge \\frac{25}{2}, \\] with equality throughout at $x = y = \\tfrac12$, where each bracket is $\\tfrac52$. Substituting $y = 1 - x$ and differentiating is possible but ugly, and the two-step inequality is what the question is testing.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ineq"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "C1-13", "paper": "C1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[ \\int_0^4 \\frac{1}{1+2^{\\,x-2}}\\,dx . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{2}{\\ln 2}$", "$4-\\dfrac{2}{\\ln 2}$", "$\\ln 5$", "$\\dfrac52$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $g(x)=\\frac{1}{1+2^{x-2}}$ and pair $x$ with $4-x$. Writing $t=2^{x-2}$, we have $2^{(4-x)-2}=\\frac1t$, so \\[ g(x)+g(4-x)=\\frac{1}{1+t}+\\frac{1}{1+\\frac1t}=\\frac{1}{1+t}+\\frac{t}{1+t}=1 . \\] Substituting $x\\mapsto4-x$ shows $\\int_0^4g(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^4g(4-x)\\,dx$, so twice the integral is $\\int_0^4 1\\,dx=4$, and the answer is $2$. Integrating directly is possible, via $\\frac{1}{1+t}=1-\\frac{t}{1+t}$, but slower and error-prone.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "arint"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "C1-7", "paper": "C1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>For how many integers $k$ does the equation \\[ 2\\sin^2x+3\\sin x+k=0 \\] have exactly two solutions with $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Put $s=\\sin x\\in[-1,1]$. A value of $s$ strictly between $-1$ and $1$ gives two values of $x$ in $[0,2\\pi)$; $s=\\pm1$ gives one each. Write the equation as $k=g(s)=-2s^2-3s$. On $[-1,1]$, $g$ rises from $g(-1)=1$ to its maximum $g(-\\frac34)=\\frac98$, then falls to $g(1)=-5$. For an integer $k$ with $-5&lt;k&lt;1$, the horizontal line meets the falling branch exactly once, at some $s$ strictly inside $(-1,1)$, and misses the rising branch: exactly two solutions. At $k=1$ the roots are $s=-1$ and $s=-\\frac12$, giving $1+2=3$ solutions; at $k=-5$ the only admissible root is $s=1$, giving $1$. So $k\\in\\{-4,-3,-2,-1,0\\}$: five integers.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "root"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "D1-19", "paper": "D1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $k$ for which both roots of \\[x^2-2kx+k+6=0\\] are real and lie strictly between $-3$ and $3$. (A repeated root counts as both roots.)<\/p>", "opts": ["no such $k$", "$-3&lt;k\\le-2$", "$-\\tfrac{15}{7}&lt;k&lt;-2$", "$k\\le-2$", "$-\\tfrac{15}{7}&lt;k\\le-2$", "$-\\tfrac{15}{7}&lt;k&lt;3$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^2-2kx+k+6$. Both roots lie in $(-3,3)$ exactly when the roots are real, the vertex $x=k$ lies in $(-3,3)$, and $f(-3)>0$ and $f(3)>0$. Real roots: $k^2-k-6\\ge0$, so $k\\le-2$ or $k\\ge3$. Vertex: $-3&lt;k&lt;3$. $f(3)=15-5k>0$ gives $k&lt;3$, and $f(-3)=7k+15>0$ gives $k>-\\tfrac{15}{7}$. Intersecting, $-\\tfrac{15}{7}&lt;k\\le-2$. At $k=-2$ the repeated root $x=-2$ is inside the interval, so the endpoint is included. The condition $f(-3)>0$ is the one most often forgotten, and it is the only one that cuts the interval from below.<\/p>", "tags": ["quad"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "E2-20", "paper": "E2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>Real numbers $a$ and $b$ satisfy $0&lt;a&lt;b&lt;1$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$a^b&lt;b^a$<\/li><li>$a^a&lt;b^b$<\/li><li>$\\log_b a>1$<\/li><li>$a^b&lt;a^a$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and III only", "III and IV only", "I, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and IV only", "II, III and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>IV: for a base between $0$ and $1$, a larger exponent gives a smaller power, and $b>a$. I needs no calculus: $a^b&lt;b^b$, because raising to the positive power $b$ preserves $a&lt;b$, and $b^b&lt;b^a$ by the same fact as IV with base $b$. So $a^b&lt;b^b&lt;b^a$. III: $\\log_ba=\\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln b}$ is a ratio of two negative numbers, and $|\\ln a|>|\\ln b|$, so it exceeds $1$. II fails: $a=0.1$, $b=0.3$ gives $a^a\\approx0.794$ and $b^b\\approx0.697$. The function $x^x$ decreases before it increases, so no ordering is forced.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "ineq"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "F1-20", "paper": "F1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>For how many integers $c$ does the equation \\[x^3-6x^2+9x+c=0\\] have three distinct positive real roots?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>$f'(x)=3(x-1)(x-3)$, with a local maximum $f(1)=4+c$ and a local minimum $f(3)=c$. Three distinct real roots need $f(1)>0>f(3)$, that is $-4&lt;c&lt;0$. They are all positive exactly when the smallest root is: since $f(0)=c&lt;0$ and $f(1)>0$, the smallest root lies in $(0,1)$. So $c\\in\\{-3,-2,-1\\}$, three integers. At $c=0$ one root is $0$, not positive, and at $c=-4$ a repeated root appears.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "diff"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "G1-3", "paper": "G1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>In triangle $ABC$, $AB=3$ and $AC=5$. The median from $A$, that is the line segment from $A$ to the midpoint $M$ of $BC$, is perpendicular to the side $AB$. Find the length of $BC$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\sqrt{34}$", "$2\\sqrt{13}$", "$\\sqrt{58}$", "$8$", "$2\\sqrt{17}$", "$\\sqrt{73}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Place $A$ at the origin and $B$ at $(3,0)$. The median is perpendicular to $AB$, so $M=(0,m)$ for some $m$, and since $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$, $C=2M-B=(-3,2m)$. Then $AC^2=9+4m^2=25$, so $m=2$ and $C=(-3,4)$. So $BC=\\sqrt{6^2+4^2}=2\\sqrt{13}$. The sine and cosine rules can be forced through, but the coordinate placement makes the median condition do all the work.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "trg"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "G1-4", "paper": "G1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Find the sum of all the real solutions of the equation \\[\\log_2(x+3)+\\log_4\\!\\left((x-1)^2\\right)=\\log_2 3 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\sqrt7-1$", "$-2$", "$0$", "$\\sqrt7+1$", "$\\sqrt7-3$", "$-1-\\sqrt7$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The domain is $x>-3$, $x\\neq1$. The key step is $\\log_4\\!\\left((x-1)^2\\right)=\\log_2|x-1|$, not $\\log_2(x-1)$. The equation becomes $(x+3)|x-1|=3$. For $x>1$: $x^2+2x-6=0$, giving $x=-1+\\sqrt7$. For $-3&lt;x&lt;1$: $(x+3)(1-x)=3$, that is $x^2+2x=0$, giving $x=0$ and $x=-2$, both valid. The sum is $\\sqrt7-3$. Writing $2\\log_4(x-1)$ as $\\log_2(x-1)$ silently restricts to $x>1$ and loses two solutions, giving option A.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "ineq"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "G2-4", "paper": "G2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Real numbers $a$ and $b$ satisfy $a^2+b^2=1$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$|a+b|\\le\\sqrt2$<\/li><li>$a^3+b^3\\le1$<\/li><li>$ab\\ge-\\tfrac12$<\/li><li>$a+b\\ge ab$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I, II and III only", "I and II only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and III only", "II and IV only", "I, III and IV only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>I: $(a+b)^2=1+2ab\\le1+(a^2+b^2)=2$. III: $(a+b)^2\\ge0$ gives $2ab\\ge-1$. II: $|a|,|b|\\le1$, so $a^3\\le a^2$ and $b^3\\le b^2$, giving $a^3+b^3\\le a^2+b^2=1$. IV fails at $a=b=-\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}$, where $a+b=-\\sqrt2$ but $ab=\\tfrac12$. So I, II and III.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "G2-5", "paper": "G2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true for every real number $x$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$|x-1|+|x+1|\\ge2$<\/li><li>$|x-1|-|x+1|\\le2$<\/li><li>$\\left|x^2-1\\right|\\ge|x|-1$<\/li><li>$|x-2|+|x-5|+|x-9|\\ge7$<\/li><li>$\\bigl||x|-3\\bigr|\\le|x|-3$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, IV and V only", "I, II, III, IV and V", "I, II, III and IV only", "III and IV only"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I and II follow from the triangle inequality: the distances from $x$ to $1$ and to $-1$ sum to at least $2$ and differ by at most $2$. III: if $|x|&lt;1$ the left side is non-negative and the right side negative; if $|x|\\ge1$ it reads $x^2\\ge|x|$, which holds. IV: a sum of distances to $2,5,9$ is least at the median $5$, where it is $3+0+4=7$. V fails whenever $|x|&lt;3$, since the left side is positive and the right side negative. So I to IV.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "H1-1", "paper": "H1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Find the minimum value, over all real $x$, of \\[|x-1|+|2x-3|+|3x-6| .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac12$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac32$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac52$", "$3$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The function is piecewise linear, so track its gradient. The kinks are at $1$, $\\tfrac32$ and $2$, where the gradient jumps by $2$, $4$ and $6$ respectively (twice the coefficient of $x$). Far to the left the gradient is $-(1+2+3)=-6$; it becomes $-4$ after $x=1$, $0$ after $x=\\tfrac32$, and $6$ after $x=2$. So the function is constant on $\\left[\\tfrac32,2\\right]$, and that constant is the minimum: at $x=2$ it is $1+1+0=2$. The minimum sits where the &ldquo;weight&rdquo; to the left first reaches half the total, the weighted median.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "H1-2", "paper": "H1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>A sequence is defined by $a_1=1$ and \\[a_{n+1}=\\frac{a_n}{1+n\\,a_n}\\qquad(n\\ge1).\\] Find $\\dfrac{1}{a_{100}}$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$4950$", "$4951$", "$5050$", "$5051$", "$9901$", "$100$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Take reciprocals: $\\frac{1}{a_{n+1}}=\\frac{1+na_n}{a_n}=\\frac1{a_n}+n$. So the reciprocals increase by $1,2,3,\\dots$ in turn, and \\[\\frac{1}{a_{100}}=\\frac1{a_1}+(1+2+\\cdots+99)=1+4950=4951 .\\] Computing the first few terms directly gives $1,\\tfrac12,\\tfrac14,\\tfrac17,\\dots$, which is how the reciprocal pattern suggests itself.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "sym"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "H1-3", "paper": "H1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>How many ordered pairs of integers $(x,y)$ satisfy \\[x^2+xy+y^2=7\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$6$", "$8$", "$10$", "$12$", "$16$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Complete the square: multiplying by $4$ gives $(2x+y)^2+3y^2=28$, so $3y^2\\le28$ and $|y|\\le3$. $y=\\pm3$: $(2x\\pm3)^2=1$, giving $x=-1,-2$ for $y=3$ and $x=1,2$ for $y=-3$. $y=\\pm2$: $(2x\\pm2)^2=16$, giving $x=1,-3$ for $y=2$ and $x=-1,3$ for $y=-2$. $y=\\pm1$: $(2x\\pm1)^2=25$, giving $x=2,-3$ for $y=1$ and $x=-2,3$ for $y=-1$. $y=0$: $4x^2=28$, no integer solutions. That is $12$ pairs, matching the six-fold symmetry of the equation.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "quad"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "H1-4", "paper": "H1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>The real number $x$ satisfies $9^x-6^x=4^x$. Find the value of \\[\\left(\\tfrac32\\right)^x-\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^x .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$\\sqrt5$", "$\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac32$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Divide by $4^x$: $\\left(\\tfrac32\\right)^{2x}-\\left(\\tfrac32\\right)^x-1=0$. With $t=\\left(\\tfrac32\\right)^x>0$, $t^2-t-1=0$, so $t=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$. Then $\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^x=\\frac1t$, and $t-\\frac1t=1$ comes straight from $t^2-t=1$, no need to find $x$ or even $t$. The quantity is $1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "quad"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "H2-5", "paper": "H2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x|x|+ax$, where $a$ is a real constant. The condition &ldquo;$a>0$&rdquo; is <span class=\"blank\">________<\/span> for &ldquo;$f$ is one-to-one&rdquo;.<\/p>", "opts": ["necessary but not sufficient", "sufficient but not necessary", "necessary and sufficient", "neither necessary nor sufficient"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$x|x|$ is increasing on the whole line, so for $a\\ge0$, $f$ is a sum of increasing functions and is one-to-one. For $a&lt;0$, $f(0)=0$ and $f(-a)=a^2+a(-a)=0$ with $-a\\neq0$, so $f$ is not one-to-one. So $f$ is one-to-one exactly when $a\\ge0$, and $a>0$ is sufficient but not necessary: $a=0$ gives the one-to-one $f(x)=x|x|$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "diff"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "H2-6", "paper": "H2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Four friends each make a statement about a real number $x$:<\/p><p><br> Alice: &ldquo;$x>2$.&rdquo; \\quad Bob: &ldquo;$x>3$.&rdquo; \\quad Carol: &ldquo;$x&lt;5$.&rdquo; \\quad Dan: &ldquo;$x^2>9$.&rdquo;<\/p><p><br> Exactly two of them are telling the truth. Find the complete set of possible values of $x$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2&lt;x\\le3$", "$x&lt;-3$", "$x&lt;-3$ or $2&lt;x\\le3$", "$x&lt;-3$ or $2&lt;x&lt;5$", "$-3\\le x\\le3$", "$x\\ge5$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Go through the regions cut by $-3$, $2$, $3$ and $5$. For $x&lt;-3$ only Carol and Dan are right: two. For $-3\\le x\\le2$ only Carol: one. For $2&lt;x\\le3$ Alice and Carol: two. For $3&lt;x&lt;5$ Alice, Bob, Carol and Dan: four. For $x\\ge5$ Alice, Bob and Dan: three. So the set is $x&lt;-3$ or $2&lt;x\\le3$. The endpoint $x=3$ is included because $9>9$ is false, and $x=-3$ is excluded for the same reason.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ineq"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "H2-7", "paper": "H2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>On an island every inhabitant either always tells the truth or always lies. A group of $n$ inhabitants, where $n\\ge3$, sit around a round table, and each of them says: &ldquo;The person on my left is a liar.&rdquo; Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>When $n=6$ this is possible.<\/li><li>When $n=7$ this is impossible.<\/li><li>Whenever this is possible, exactly half of the people are liars.<\/li><li>Whenever this is possible, there is only one way to decide who is a liar.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I, II and III only", "I and II only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only", "I only", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Each statement forces the person on the left to be the opposite type from the speaker, so types alternate around the table. Alternation round a cycle is possible exactly when $n$ is even: I and II. Then exactly half are liars: III. IV fails: for even $n$ there are two alternating patterns, depending on whether a chosen person is a truth-teller or a liar.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "mod"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "H2-8", "paper": "H2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>For real numbers $a$, $b$, $c$, consider the statement $G$: &ldquo;$a$, $b$, $c$ (in that order) are consecutive terms of a geometric progression with non-zero common ratio&rdquo;. Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$b^2=ac$ is necessary for $G$.<\/li><li>$b^2=ac$ is sufficient for $G$.<\/li><li>$b^2=ac$ together with $a\\neq0$ is sufficient for $G$.<\/li><li>$b^2=ac$ together with $b\\neq0$ is sufficient for $G$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "I, III and IV only", "I and IV only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I: if $b=ar$ and $c=ar^2$ then $b^2=a^2r^2=ac$. II fails: $a=b=0$, $c=5$ has $b^2=ac$ but is not a geometric progression. III fails: $a=1$, $b=0$, $c=0$ has $b^2=ac$ and $a\\neq0$, but the ratio would have to be $0$, which is excluded. IV holds: $b\\neq0$ forces $a$ and $c$ non-zero, and then $\\frac ba=\\frac cb$ is a common non-zero ratio. So I and IV.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "ser"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "I1-1", "paper": "I1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>The curve $C$ has equation $x^2+xy+y^2=3$. It is a closed curve, tilted relative to the axes. There are exactly two points on $C$ at which the tangent to $C$ is parallel to the line $y=x$. Find the distance between these two points.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt3$", "$2\\sqrt6$", "$4$", "$\\sqrt6$", "$3\\sqrt2$", "$2\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Differentiate implicitly: $2x+y+(x+2y)\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2x+y}{x+2y}$. Setting this equal to $1$ gives $2x+y=-x-2y$, that is $y=-x$. On the curve, $x^2-x^2+x^2=3$, so the points are $\\left(\\sqrt3,-\\sqrt3\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\sqrt3,\\sqrt3\\right)$, at distance $\\sqrt{12+12}=2\\sqrt6$. As a check on the geometry: the curve is symmetric in the line $y=x$, so the tangents parallel to that line occur where the curve crosses the perpendicular line $y=-x$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "coord"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "I1-2", "paper": "I1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>An arithmetic progression has first term $a$ and common difference $d$, where $a$ and $d$ are both non-zero. Its 2nd, 5th and 11th terms, in that order, are three consecutive terms of a geometric progression. It turns out that the sum of the first ten terms of the arithmetic progression is itself one of the terms of the progression. Which term is it?<\/p>", "opts": ["the 30th", "the 32nd", "the 55th", "the 64th", "the 65th", "the 66th"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The geometric condition is $(a+4d)^2=(a+d)(a+10d)$, which simplifies to $6d^2=3ad$, so $a=2d$ (the common ratio is then $\\frac{6d}{3d}=2$). The $n$th term is $a+(n-1)d=(n+1)d$, and the sum of ten terms is $5(2a+9d)=65d$. Setting $(n+1)d=65d$ gives $n=64$. The trap is to see $65d$ and answer &ldquo;the 65th&rdquo;: the off-by-one comes from $a=2d$ rather than $a=d$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "quad"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "I2-1", "paper": "I2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. Consider the events $A$: the first die shows an even number; $B$: the total is $7$; $C$: the total is $6$; $D$: both dice show even numbers. Which of the following pairs of events are independent? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$A$ and $B$<\/li><li>$A$ and $C$<\/li><li>$A$ and $D$<\/li><li>$B$ and $D$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "I and IV only", "III only", "none of them"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Independence means $P(X\\cap Y)=P(X)P(Y)$. I: $P(A\\cap B)=\\tfrac{3}{36}=\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac16$, independent. II: $P(A\\cap C)=\\tfrac{2}{36}$, from $(2,4)$ and $(4,2)$, but $P(A)P(C)=\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac5{36}$. III: $D$ is contained in $A$, so $P(A\\cap D)=\\tfrac14\\neq\\tfrac18$. IV: two even numbers never total $7$, so $P(B\\cap D)=0$, while $P(B)P(D)>0$. Only I. It is striking that a total of $7$ carries no information about the first die, but a total of $6$ does.<\/p>", "tags": ["cnt", "log"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "I2-2", "paper": "I2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>A function $f$, defined for all $x>0$, is said to <em>tend to infinity<\/em> if for every number $M$ there is a number $N$ such that $f(x)>M$ for every $x>N$. How many of the following functions tend to infinity? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(x)=x-\\sin x$<\/li><li>$f(x)=x\\sin x$<\/li><li>$f(x)=(\\ln x)^2$<\/li><li>$f(x)=xe^{-x}$<\/li><li>$f(x)=x+10\\sin x$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The definition asks that $f$ eventually stays above any level, not merely that it gets large sometimes. I: $x-\\sin x\\ge x-1$, so $N=M+1$ works. V: $x+10\\sin x\\ge x-10$, so $N=M+10$ works, despite the large oscillation. III: $(\\ln x)^2>M$ once $x>e^{\\sqrt{|M|}}$, slowly but surely. II fails: $x\\sin x$ reaches arbitrarily large values but returns to $0$ at every multiple of $\\pi$, so it never stays above $M=1$. IV fails: $xe^{-x}\\to0$. Three functions.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "exp"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "B1-7", "paper": "B1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Find the value of \\[ \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{n+2}{n(n+1)\\,2^{n}} . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac34$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac54$", "$\\dfrac32$", "$2$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Partial fractions in $n$ alone go nowhere, because of the $2^n$. The trick is to split the numerator against the factor $2$ rather than against $n$ and $n+1$: write $n + 2 = 2(n+1) - n$, so \\[ \\frac{n+2}{n(n+1)2^n} = \\frac{2}{n\\,2^n} - \\frac{1}{(n+1)2^n} = \\frac{1}{n\\,2^{n-1}} - \\frac{1}{(n+1)\\,2^{n}} . \\] With $b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n\\,2^{n-1}}$ this is $b_n - b_{n+1}$, so the partial sum is $b_1 - b_{N+1} = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{(N+1)2^N}$, and the infinite sum is $1$. The first three partial sums are $\\tfrac34, \\tfrac{11}{12}, \\tfrac{31}{32}$, which is the pattern a candidate without the decomposition would have to guess from; option B is the first term on its own.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["tel", "ser"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "C2-15", "paper": "C2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>For a real number $x$, consider the five statements \\[ x>1,\\qquad x^2>1,\\qquad x^3>1,\\qquad x>-1,\\qquad x^2>x . \\] Find the complete set of real $x$ for which exactly two of these statements are true.<\/p>", "opts": ["$x&lt;-1$", "$-1&lt;x&lt;0$", "$x&lt;0$", "$x\\le 0$ and $x\\ne-1$", "$x&lt;0$ and $x\\ne-1$", "there is no such $x$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Count true statements region by region. For $x>1$ all five hold. For $-1&lt;x\\le1$, $x>-1$ holds, and $x^2>x$ holds only when $x&lt;0$; so two are true on $(-1,0)$ and one on $[0,1]$. At $x=-1$ only $x^2>x$ holds. For $x&lt;-1$, exactly $x^2>1$ and $x^2>x$ hold. So exactly two are true for $x&lt;-1$ and for $-1&lt;x&lt;0$, but not at $x=-1$ itself.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "root"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "B2-13", "paper": "B2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Let $f$ be a continuous function on $[0,1]$ with $0 \\le f(x) \\le 1$ for every $x$ in $[0,1]$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)^2\\,dx \\le \\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)^2\\,dx \\le \\left(\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx\\right)^{\\!2}$<\/li><li>If $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)^2\\,dx = \\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$, then $f$ is constant on $[0,1]$.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I holds because $0 \\le f \\le 1$ gives $f^2 \\le f$ pointwise, and integrating preserves the inequality. II is false: $f(x) = x$ gives $\\int f^2 = \\tfrac13$ and $(\\int f)^2 = \\tfrac14$. The true inequality runs the other way, and the neatest proof is worth knowing: with $m = \\int_0^1 f$, expanding $0 \\le \\int_0^1 (f - m)^2 = \\int f^2 - 2m^2 + m^2$ gives $\\int f^2 \\ge m^2$. For III, the hypothesis says $\\int_0^1 (f - f^2) = 0$ with $f - f^2 = f(1-f) \\ge 0$ continuous, so $f(1 - f)$ vanishes everywhere: every value of $f$ is $0$ or $1$. A continuous function taking only the values $0$ and $1$ cannot take both (it would have to pass through $\\tfrac12$), so $f$ is constant. The answer is I and III. Option D is the reader who assumes an inequality between $\\int f^2$ and $(\\int f)^2$ without checking which way it goes.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "ineq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "D1-20", "paper": "D1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>For which values of the constant $c$ is the point of the curve $y=x^2$ that is closest to $(0,c)$ the origin?<\/p>", "opts": ["$c\\le0$", "$c&lt;\\tfrac12$", "$c\\le1$", "$c\\le2$", "$c&lt;1$", "$c\\le\\tfrac12$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The squared distance from $(x,x^2)$ to $(0,c)$ is $x^2+(x^2-c)^2=u^2+(1-2c)u+c^2$ with $u=x^2\\ge0$. This quadratic in $u$ has its vertex at $u=c-\\tfrac12$. If $c\\le\\tfrac12$ the vertex is at $u\\le0$, so the minimum over $u\\ge0$ is at $u=0$, the origin. If $c>\\tfrac12$ the minimum is at $u=c-\\tfrac12>0$, two points either side of the axis, strictly closer than the origin. So the answer is $c\\le\\tfrac12$. Intuition says the origin wins until $c$ is about the radius of curvature, and the radius of curvature of $y=x^2$ at the origin is exactly $\\tfrac12$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "quad"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "F2-20", "paper": "F2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>Real numbers $a$, $b$ and $c$ satisfy $a+b+c=0$ and $a^2+b^2+c^2=6$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$ab+bc+ca=-3$<\/li><li>Each of $a$, $b$ and $c$ lies in the interval $[-2,2]$.<\/li><li>$abc\\le1$<\/li><li>$a^4+b^4+c^4=18$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and IV only", "II and IV only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II and III only"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I: $0=(a+b+c)^2=6+2(ab+bc+ca)$. II: substituting $c=-a-b$ gives $b^2+ab+a^2-3=0$, and for real $b$ the discriminant $a^2-4(a^2-3)=12-3a^2\\ge0$ forces $|a|\\le2$, and likewise for $b$ and $c$. IV: $\\sum a^2b^2=(ab+bc+ca)^2-2abc(a+b+c)=9$, so $\\sum a^4=36-2\\cdot9=18$. III fails: $a=2$, $b=c=-1$ satisfies both conditions and has $abc=2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "quad"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "G1-5", "paper": "G1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>A circle passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$ and touches the line $y=x+4$. There are two such circles. Find the sum of their radii.<\/p>", "opts": ["$12\\sqrt2$", "$8\\sqrt2$", "$10\\sqrt2$", "$16$", "$6\\sqrt2$", "$4+8\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The centre lies on the perpendicular bisector $x=2$, say at $(2,k)$, with radius $\\sqrt{4+k^2}$. Tangency to $x-y+4=0$ requires $\\frac{|2-k+4|}{\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt{4+k^2}$, that is $(6-k)^2=2(4+k^2)$, which simplifies to $k^2+12k-28=0$. So $k=2$ or $k=-14$, with radii $\\sqrt8=2\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt{200}=10\\sqrt2$. The sum is $12\\sqrt2$. The large circle, centred at $(2,-14)$, is easy to overlook, because nobody draws it.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "circ"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "G1-6", "paper": "G1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy $x+y=4$. Find the minimum value of \\[\\left(x^2+1\\right)\\left(y^2+1\\right).\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$9$", "$13$", "$16$", "$17$", "$20$", "$25$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Expand in terms of $p=xy$: $(x^2+1)(y^2+1)=p^2+x^2+y^2+1=p^2+(16-2p)+1=(p-1)^2+16$. Real $x$ and $y$ with sum $4$ allow any $p\\le4$, and $p=1$ is allowed, so the minimum is $16$, at $x,y=2\\pm\\sqrt3$. The symmetric guess $x=y=2$ gives $25$, which is the value at $p=4$ and is not the minimum. Symmetric constraints do not always give symmetric optima.<\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "quad"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "G1-7", "paper": "G1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[\\sum_{k=1}^{89}\\frac{1}{1+\\tan k^\\circ} .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$44$", "$45$", "$89$", "$\\dfrac{89}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{89}{2}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Pair $k$ with $90-k$. Since $\\tan(90^\\circ-k^\\circ)=\\frac{1}{\\tan k^\\circ}$, with $t=\\tan k^\\circ$, \\[\\frac1{1+t}+\\frac{1}{1+\\frac1t}=\\frac1{1+t}+\\frac{t}{t+1}=1 .\\] Adding the sum to itself in reverse order gives $2S=89$, so $S=\\tfrac{89}{2}$. The middle term $k=45$ pairs with itself and contributes $\\tfrac12$, which is consistent.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "sym"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "G2-6", "paper": "G2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Three arguments are offered for the claim that $\\sqrt3$ is irrational. In each, suppose $\\sqrt3=\\dfrac pq$ with $p$ and $q$ positive integers having no common factor, so that $p^2=3q^2$.<\/p><p><br> <strong>I.<\/strong> $3\\mid p^2$, and $3$ is prime, so $3\\mid p$. Writing $p=3m$ gives $q^2=3m^2$, so $3\\mid q$ as well: a contradiction.<br> <strong>II.<\/strong> If $p$ were even, $3q^2$ would be even, so $q$ would be even: a contradiction. So $p$ is odd, and then so is $q$. But the square of an odd number leaves remainder $1$ on division by $8$, so $p^2\\equiv1$ while $3q^2\\equiv3\\pmod 8$: a contradiction.<br> <strong>III.<\/strong> $\\sqrt3=1.7320508\\ldots$ has a non-terminating decimal expansion, so it is irrational.<\/p><p><br> Which of these arguments are valid?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "II only", "I, II and III", "I and III only", "none of them"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>I is the standard argument and is valid. II is less familiar but also complete: the case $p$ even forces a common factor $2$, and the case $p$ odd is ruled out by squares modulo $8$. III is invalid: $\\tfrac13=0.333\\ldots$ has a non-terminating decimal expansion and is rational. What characterises rational numbers is a decimal that eventually repeats, and the argument does not show that this one never does.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "mod"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "G2-7", "paper": "G2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Let $n$ be a positive integer. Which of the following conditions are equivalent to &ldquo;$n$ is a perfect square&rdquo;? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$n$ has an odd number of positive divisors.<\/li><li>$n$ leaves remainder $0$ or $1$ on division by $4$.<\/li><li>The sum of the positive divisors of $n$ is odd.<\/li><li>Every prime occurs in the prime factorisation of $n$ to an even power.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "IV only", "I, II and IV only", "I, III and IV only", "II and IV only", "I and IV only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>IV is the definition in prime-factor form. I: divisors pair up as $d$ and $\\tfrac nd$, and a divisor is unpaired exactly when $d=\\sqrt n$. II is necessary but not sufficient: $5$ leaves remainder $1$ and is not a square. III fails both ways: $2$ has divisor sum $3$, which is odd, although $2$ is not a square. (The divisor sum is odd exactly for squares and twice squares.) So I and IV.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "ns"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "G2-8", "paper": "G2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>A sequence $a_1,a_2,a_3,\\dots$ is called <em>eventually positive<\/em> if there is a number $N$ such that $a_n>0$ for every $n>N$. How many of the following sequences are eventually positive? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$a_n=n^2-100n$<\/li><li>$a_n=(-1)^n+\\dfrac2n$<\/li><li>$a_n=\\sin n+\\dfrac32$<\/li><li>$a_n=\\dfrac{n-1000}{n^2+1}$<\/li><li>$a_n=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}\\right)+1$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: positive for all $n>100$. II: for odd $n\\ge3$ the terms are $-1+\\frac2n&lt;0$, and odd $n$ occur beyond every $N$, so no. III: $\\sin n\\ge-1$, so every term is at least $\\tfrac12$: yes, with $N=0$. IV: positive for $n>1000$. V: when $n\\equiv2\\pmod4$, $\\cos\\frac{n\\pi}{2}=-1$ and the term is $0$, which is not positive, and such $n$ occur beyond every $N$, so no. Three are eventually positive. Note that V is never negative; &ldquo;eventually positive&rdquo; needs strictly positive terms.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ineq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "G2-9", "paper": "G2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>The polynomial $x^4+ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$ has four distinct positive real roots. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$a&lt;0$ and $c&lt;0$<\/li><li>$b>0$ and $d>0$<\/li><li>$a^2>2b$<\/li><li>$a^2>3b$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II, III and IV", "III and IV only", "I and III only", "I, II and III only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>By Vieta, $-a$ is the sum of the roots, $b$ the sum of products in pairs, $-c$ the sum of products in threes and $d$ the product. With all roots positive, I and II follow. III: $a^2-2b$ is the sum of the squares of the roots, which is positive. IV can fail when the roots are close together: $1,1.01,1.02,1.03$ give $a^2\\approx16.48$ and $3b\\approx18.54$. (The sharp inequality is $3a^2\\ge8b$.) So I, II and III.<\/p>", "tags": ["vie", "poly"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "H1-5", "paper": "H1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>A triangle has sides of length $4$, $5$ and $6$. Find the ratio of its largest angle to its smallest angle.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac32$", "$\\dfrac53$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac52$", "$3$", "the ratio is irrational"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>By the cosine rule, the smallest angle $A$ (opposite $4$) has $\\cos A=\\frac{25+36-16}{60}=\\frac34$, and the largest angle $C$ (opposite $6$) has $\\cos C=\\frac{16+25-36}{40}=\\frac18$. Now $2\\cos^2A-1=\\frac98-1=\\frac18=\\cos C$. Using $\\cos2A=2\\cos^2A-1$, and since $2A$ and $C$ both lie in $(0^\\circ,180^\\circ)$, where cosine is one-to-one, we get $C=2A$. The ratio is exactly $2$, which no amount of approximating the angles would reveal with certainty.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "H1-6", "paper": "H1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Two circles, of radii $3$ and $5$, intersect at the points $P$ and $Q$. At $P$ the tangents to the two circles are perpendicular to each other. Find the length of the common chord $PQ$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{15}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{15\\sqrt{34}}{17}$", "$\\sqrt{34}$", "$\\dfrac{30}{\\sqrt{17}}$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac{15\\sqrt2}{4}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Each tangent at $P$ is perpendicular to the radius there, so perpendicular tangents mean perpendicular radii: triangle $O_1PO_2$ has a right angle at $P$, and $O_1O_2=\\sqrt{9+25}=\\sqrt{34}$. The chord $PQ$ is bisected at right angles by $O_1O_2$, and half of it is the altitude from $P$ in that right triangle: $\\frac{3\\cdot5}{\\sqrt{34}}$. So $PQ=\\frac{30}{\\sqrt{34}}=\\frac{15\\sqrt{34}}{17}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["circ", "coord"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "H1-7", "paper": "H1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>A circle passes through the three points of the parabola $y=x^2$ whose $x$-coordinates are $1$, $2$ and $-4$. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["The circle meets the parabola at a fourth point, with $x$-coordinate $-1$.", "The circle meets the parabola at a fourth point, with $x$-coordinate $3$.", "The circle meets the parabola at a fourth point, with $x$-coordinate $\\tfrac12$.", "The circle meets the parabola at two further points.", "No circle passes through all three points.", "The circle meets the parabola at no further point: it is tangent to the parabola at $(1,1)$."], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Substituting $y=x^2$ into $x^2+y^2+Dx+Ey+F=0$ gives the quartic $x^4+(1+E)x^2+Dx+F=0$, which has no $x^3$ term. So its four roots, counted with multiplicity, sum to $0$. Three of them are $1$, $2$ and $-4$, so the fourth is $1$ again. A repeated root means tangency: the circle touches the parabola at $(1,1)$ and meets it nowhere else. (Solving for $D$, $E$, $F$ confirms the quartic is $(x-1)^2(x-2)(x+4)$.)<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "vie", "circ"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "H2-10", "paper": "H2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A counter starts at $0$ on a number line. Each move either adds $3$ to its position or subtracts $5$ from it. Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>Every integer can be reached.<\/li><li>The least number of moves needed to reach $1$ is $3$.<\/li><li>The integer $2$ can be reached in fewer than $4$ moves.<\/li><li>After exactly $10$ moves the counter cannot be at $0$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and IV only", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>After $a$ additions and $b$ subtractions the position is $3a-5b$, in any order. I: $1=3\\cdot2-5\\cdot1$, and repeating that combination reaches every integer. II: $3a-5b=1$ needs $a\\equiv2\\pmod5$, so $a\\ge2$ and then $b\\ge1$; the minimum is $3$ moves. III: $3a-5b=2$ needs $a\\equiv4\\pmod5$, so $a\\ge4$ and $b\\ge2$, at least $6$ moves. IV: $3a=5b$ with $a+b=10$ would need $8a=50$, impossible. So I, II and IV.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "log"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "H2-11", "paper": "H2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Let $S$ be the set of points $(x,y)$ in the plane satisfying \\[|x|+|y|=|x+y|+1 .\\] Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$S$ is unbounded.<\/li><li>$S$ contains the point $\\left(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac12\\right)$.<\/li><li>Every point of $S$ satisfies $xy&lt;0$.<\/li><li>$S$ contains a point with $|x|+|y|&lt;1$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I, II and III only", "II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and III only", "III only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>If $x$ and $y$ have the same sign, or one is zero, then $|x+y|=|x|+|y|$ and the equation fails. So $xy&lt;0$ throughout: III. With opposite signs, $|x|+|y|-|x+y|=2\\min(|x|,|y|)$, so $S$ is the set where $\\min(|x|,|y|)=\\tfrac12$ with opposite signs: two L-shaped paths, each made of two rays, with corners at $\\left(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac12\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12\\right)$. So I and II hold. IV fails: on $S$, $|x|+|y|\\ge2\\cdot\\tfrac12=1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "coord"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "H2-12", "paper": "H2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Three arguments are offered for the claim that $n^5-n$ is divisible by $5$ for every positive integer $n$.<\/p><p><br> <strong>I.<\/strong> For $n=1$ it is $0$. Expanding, $\\left((n+1)^5-(n+1)\\right)-\\left(n^5-n\\right)=5n^4+10n^3+10n^2+5n$, a multiple of $5$. So each value differs from the previous one by a multiple of $5$, and every value is a multiple of $5$.<br> <strong>II.<\/strong> $n^5-n$ modulo $5$ depends only on $n$ modulo $5$, and for $n\\equiv0,1,2,3,4$ it is $0,0,30,240,1020$ modulo $5$, all $\\equiv0$.<br> <strong>III.<\/strong> $n^5-n=(n-1)n(n+1)(n^2+1)$, which is a product of five consecutive integers and so is divisible by $5$.<\/p><p><br> Which of these are valid proofs?<\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I only", "II only", "I, II and III", "II and III only", "I and III only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>I is complete: a starting value of $0$, and differences that are all multiples of $5$. II is complete: the five residue classes cover every integer, and $2^5-2=30$, $3^5-3=240$ and $4^5-4=1020$ are all multiples of $5$. III is invalid: $n^2+1$ is not an integer adjacent to $n+1$, so the factors are not five consecutive integers. (The factorisation can be rescued by writing $n^2+1=(n^2-4)+5$, but that is a different argument.)<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "mod"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "H2-9", "paper": "H2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>How many of the following statements about integers are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>For every integer $a$ there is an integer $b$ such that $a+b$ is odd and $ab$ is even.<\/li><li>There is an integer $b$ such that $a+b$ is odd for every integer $a$.<\/li><li>For every integer $a$ there is an integer $b$ such that $a^2+b^2=2ab+1$.<\/li><li>There is an integer $a$ such that $ab$ is even for every integer $b$.<\/li><li>For all integers $a$ and $b$, if $a+b$ is odd then $ab$ is even.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: take $b=1-a$, so $a+b=1$ and $ab=a(1-a)$, a product of consecutive integers, which is even. II fails: whatever $b$ is, $a=b+1$ makes $a+b$ odd, but $a=b$ makes it even. III: $a^2+b^2-2ab=(a-b)^2=1$; take $b=a+1$. IV: $a=2$. V: if $a+b$ is odd, one of $a$, $b$ is even. Four are true. II and I differ only in the order of the quantifiers.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "mod"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "I1-3", "paper": "I1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>For which real values of $a$ does the equation \\[|x-a|+|x-2a|=a^2\\] have exactly two real solutions for $x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a\\neq0$", "$|a|\\ge1$", "$a>1$", "$|a|>1$", "$0&lt;|a|&lt;1$", "$a&lt;-1$ or $a>2$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The left side is the sum of the distances from $x$ to $a$ and to $2a$. Its minimum value is $|a|$, the distance between those two points, attained for every $x$ between them, and it increases linearly outside. So there are no solutions if $a^2&lt;|a|$, that is $0&lt;|a|&lt;1$. There are infinitely many, a whole interval, if $a^2=|a|$ with $a\\neq0$, that is $a=\\pm1$. There are exactly two, one on each side, if $a^2>|a|$, that is $|a|>1$. At $a=0$ the equation is $2|x|=0$, with the single solution $x=0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "quad"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "I1-4", "paper": "I1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>For how many integers $a$ does the curve $y=x^4-8x^2+ax$ have exactly three stationary points?<\/p>", "opts": ["$11$", "$23$", "$25$", "$27$", "$13$", "infinitely many"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Stationary points satisfy $4x^3-16x+a=0$, that is $a=16x-4x^3=h(x)$. The graph of $h$ has a local minimum at $x=-\\tfrac{2}{\\sqrt3}$ and a local maximum at $x=\\tfrac{2}{\\sqrt3}$, of value $\\frac{32}{\\sqrt3}-\\frac{32}{3\\sqrt3}=\\frac{64}{3\\sqrt3}\\approx12.3$. Three distinct stationary points need $|a|&lt;\\frac{64}{3\\sqrt3}$, so the integers $-12,\\dots,12$: $25$ values. At the boundary two stationary points merge, but $\\frac{64}{3\\sqrt3}$ is not an integer, so no integer is lost or gained there.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "root"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "I1-5", "paper": "I1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>For which real values of $k$ does the equation \\[x^2e^{-x}=k\\] have exactly three real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0&lt;k&lt;\\dfrac{4}{e^2}$", "$0\\le k\\le\\dfrac{4}{e^2}$", "$0&lt;k&lt;\\dfrac1e$", "$k>\\dfrac{4}{e^2}$", "$0&lt;k&lt;\\dfrac2e$", "$k=\\dfrac{4}{e^2}$ only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $g(x)=x^2e^{-x}$. Then $g'(x)=x(2-x)e^{-x}$: a minimum $g(0)=0$ and a maximum $g(2)=\\frac4{e^2}$. As $x\\to-\\infty$, $g\\to+\\infty$, and as $x\\to+\\infty$, $g\\to0$ from above. So a level $0&lt;k&lt;\\frac4{e^2}$ is met once for $x&lt;0$, once on $(0,2)$ and once for $x>2$, where $g$ decays towards $0$ but never reaches it. That gives three solutions. At $k=\\frac4{e^2}$ there are two; at $k=0$ one; for $k>\\frac4{e^2}$ only the branch $x&lt;0$ remains. The third root, on the long tail, is the one people miss.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "exp", "root"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "I2-3", "paper": "I2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A polynomial $f$ satisfies $f(x)=f(2-x)$ for every real $x$, and $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2f(x)\\,dx=6$. Which of the following are determined by this information alone, with the value shown? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^2xf(x)\\,dx=6$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^2(x-1)^3f(x)\\,dx=0$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^2x^2f(x)\\,dx=8$<\/li><li>$f'(1)=0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "II and IV only", "all four", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The graph is symmetric about $x=1$. I: reflecting, $\\int_0^2xf=\\int_0^2(2-x)f$, so $2\\int_0^2xf=2\\int_0^2f=12$. II: $(x-1)^3$ is odd about $x=1$ and $f$ is even about it, so the integral is $0$. IV: differentiating $f(x)=f(2-x)$ gives $f'(x)=-f'(2-x)$, and $x=1$ gives $f'(1)=0$. III is not determined: $f=3$ gives $8$, but $f(x)=2+3(x-1)^2$, which also has integral $6$, gives $\\tfrac{128}{15}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "sym"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "I2-4", "paper": "I2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A student proves that $|a+b|\\le|a|+|b|$ for all real $a$ and $b$, using cases.<\/p><p><br> (I) If $a\\ge0$ and $b\\ge0$, then $|a+b|=a+b=|a|+|b|$.<br> (II) If $a&lt;0$ and $b&lt;0$, then $|a+b|=-(a+b)=|a|+|b|$.<br> (III) If $a\\ge0>b$, then $a+b&lt;a\\le|a|+|b|$, so $|a+b|\\le|a|+|b|$.<br> (IV) The case $b\\ge0>a$ is the same as (III) with $a$ and $b$ swapped.<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The proof is complete and correct.", "The claim is false when $a$ and $b$ have opposite signs.", "Case (II) is wrong, because $|a+b|=a+b$ there.", "The claim is true, but case (III) only shows $a+b\\le|a|+|b|$; it does not rule out $-(a+b)>|a|+|b|$.", "Case (IV) is invalid because the claim is not symmetric in $a$ and $b$.", "The cases do not cover every pair $(a,b)$."], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Cases I and II are right, the four cases cover everything, and IV is a legitimate symmetry. The gap is in III. When $a\\ge0>b$, the number $a+b$ may be negative, and then $|a+b|=-(a+b)$. The inequality $a+b&lt;a$ bounds $a+b$ from above but says nothing about $-(a+b)$. The repair is one line: $-(a+b)=-a-b\\le-b=|b|\\le|a|+|b|$. So the claim is true, and the proof is incomplete.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "ineq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "I2-5", "paper": "I2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>The sequence $u_1,u_2,\\dots$ is defined by $u_1=1$ and \\[u_{n+1}=\\frac12\\left(u_n+\\frac{4}{u_n}\\right).\\] Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$u_n>2$ for every $n\\ge2$.<\/li><li>$u_{n+1}&lt;u_n$ for every $n\\ge2$.<\/li><li>Every term is rational.<\/li><li>$u_5=2$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I, II and III only", "III only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and III only", "II and III only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>I: by AM-GM, $u_{n+1}\\ge\\sqrt{u_n\\cdot\\frac4{u_n}}=2$, with equality only if $u_n=2$. Since $u_2=\\tfrac52$, the terms never equal $2$, so $u_n>2$ from $n=2$ on. II: $u_{n+1}-u_n=\\frac{4-u_n^2}{2u_n}&lt;0$ when $u_n>2$. III: rational operations on rationals. IV fails for the same reason as I: the terms approach $2$ very quickly ($u_5\\approx2.0000001$) but never reach it.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "ineq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "I2-6", "paper": "I2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>For the cubic $p(x)=x^3+ax^2+bx+c$ with real coefficients, which of the following conditions guarantee that $p$ has three real roots (counted with multiplicity)? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$p(-1)>0$ and $p(1)&lt;0$<\/li><li>$a^2&lt;3b$<\/li><li>$b&lt;0$ and $c=0$<\/li><li>$a=0$ and $b=0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, II and III only", "III only", "I and IV only", "I and III only", "II and IV only"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I: $p\\to-\\infty$ on the left and $p(-1)>0$ give a root below $-1$; $p(-1)>0>p(1)$ gives one in $(-1,1)$; and $p(1)&lt;0$ with $p\\to+\\infty$ gives one above $1$. III: $p=x(x^2+ax+b)$, and the quadratic has discriminant $a^2-4b>0$ when $b&lt;0$. II guarantees the opposite: then $p'=3x^2+2ax+b>0$ everywhere, so there is exactly one real root. IV gives $x^3+c$, which has one real root unless $c=0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "ns"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "I2-7", "paper": "I2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>For which of the following equations is it true that there is exactly one real solution? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$e^x=1+x$<\/li><li>$e^x=2x$<\/li><li>$\\ln x=x-1$<\/li><li>$2^x=x^2$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, II and III only", "III only", "I, III and IV only", "I and III only", "II and IV only"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I: $e^x-1-x$ has derivative $e^x-1$, so its minimum is at $x=0$, with value $0$: the line $y=1+x$ is the tangent at $(0,1)$ and touches exactly once. III is the same picture for the inverse function: $y=x-1$ is the tangent to $\\ln x$ at $(1,0)$, and $\\ln x$ lies below it everywhere else. II has no solution: $e^x-2x$ has minimum value $2-2\\ln2>0$ at $x=\\ln2$. IV has three: $x=2$, $x=4$, and one negative solution between $-1$ and $0$, where $2^x$ is still positive and $x^2$ falls to $0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "exp", "diff"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "B1-1", "paper": "B1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Find the exact value of $\\cos\\dfrac{\\pi}{7}\\cos\\dfrac{2\\pi}{7}\\cos\\dfrac{3\\pi}{7}$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac1{16}$", "$\\dfrac18$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt7}{8}$", "$-\\dfrac18$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Let $P = \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{7}\\cos\\frac{2\\pi}{7}\\cos\\frac{4\\pi}{7}$ and multiply by $\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{7}$. Folding three times with $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta = \\sin 2\\theta$, \\[ \\sin\\tfrac{\\pi}{7}\\,P = \\tfrac12\\sin\\tfrac{2\\pi}{7}\\cos\\tfrac{2\\pi}{7}\\cos\\tfrac{4\\pi}{7} = \\tfrac14\\sin\\tfrac{4\\pi}{7}\\cos\\tfrac{4\\pi}{7} = \\tfrac18\\sin\\tfrac{8\\pi}{7} = -\\tfrac18\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi}{7}, \\] so $P = -\\tfrac18$. Since $\\cos\\frac{3\\pi}{7} = -\\cos\\frac{4\\pi}{7}$, the required product is $\\tfrac18$. Option E is $P$ itself, the answer to the question that was not asked; the sign flip on the last cosine is the whole point.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "sym"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "C1-20", "paper": "C1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>Find \\[ \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\ \\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\frac{n}{(n+k)^2}\\, . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\ln 2-1$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\ln 2$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$", "$1$", "the limit does not exist"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Factor out $\\frac1n$: the sum is $\\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^n\\frac{1}{(1+k/n)^2}$, a Riemann sum for $\\int_0^1\\frac{dx}{(1+x)^2}$. As $n\\to\\infty$ it tends to \\[ \\left[-\\frac{1}{1+x}\\right]_0^1=1-\\frac12=\\frac12 . \\] The recognition is the question: once the sum is seen as rectangles of width $\\frac1n$ under a curve, the limit is an area.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "ser"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "E1-20", "paper": "E1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>The sequence $u_1,u_2,u_3,\\dots$ is defined by $u_{n+1}=u_n^2-2u_n+2$. For which values of $u_1$ does the sequence remain bounded?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0&lt;u_1&lt;2$", "$1\\le u_1\\le2$", "$-1\\le u_1\\le1$", "$0\\le u_1\\le2$", "$u_1\\le2$", "every real $u_1$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Complete the square: $u_{n+1}-1=(u_n-1)^2$. So with $v_n=u_n-1$ the recurrence is $v_{n+1}=v_n^{\\,2}$, giving $v_n=v_1^{\\,2^{n-1}}$. This stays bounded exactly when $|v_1|\\le1$, that is $0\\le u_1\\le2$: then $|v_n|\\le1$ throughout. The endpoints behave differently, since $u_1=0$ and $u_1=2$ both lead to the constant sequence $2$, but both are bounded. For $|v_1|>1$ the terms grow without limit.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "quad"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "B1-15", "paper": "B1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $k$ for which the equation \\[ 4^x + 4^{-x} = k\\left(2^x + 2^{-x}\\right) - 4 \\] has exactly two real solutions in $x$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$k \\ge 2\\sqrt2$", "$k > 2\\sqrt2$", "$k \\ge 3$", "$k > 3$", "$|k| > 3$", "$k > 2$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Let $t = 2^x + 2^{-x}$, so $t \\ge 2$ by AM--GM, with $t = 2$ only at $x = 0$, and $4^x + 4^{-x} = t^2 - 2$. The equation becomes $t^2 - kt + 2 = 0$. Each root $t > 2$ gives two values of $x$ (symmetric under $x \\mapsto -x$), $t = 2$ gives one, and $t &lt; 2$ gives none. The roots multiply to $2$, so at most one of them can be $\\ge 2$. Hence exactly two solutions in $x$ needs exactly one root $t > 2$, i.e.\\ the quadratic $h(t) = t^2 - kt + 2$ satisfies $h(2) &lt; 0$, which is $6 - 2k &lt; 0$, so $k > 3$. At $k = 3$ the roots are $2$ and $1$, giving only $x = 0$. Option B is the discriminant condition and ignores the constraint $t \\ge 2$ entirely.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "root"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "C1-8", "paper": "C1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Find the total length of the set of values of $x$ in $[0,2\\pi]$ for which \\[ \\sin x+\\sin 2x+\\sin 3x>0 . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$", "$\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Pair the outer terms: $\\sin x+\\sin3x=2\\sin2x\\cos x$, so the expression is $\\sin2x\\,(2\\cos x+1)$. On $[0,2\\pi]$, $\\sin2x$ changes sign at $\\frac\\pi2,\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}2$, and $2\\cos x+1$ changes sign at $\\frac{2\\pi}3$ and $\\frac{4\\pi}3$. Checking signs on the six intervals, the product is positive on $(0,\\frac\\pi2)$, $(\\frac{2\\pi}3,\\pi)$ and $(\\frac{4\\pi}3,\\frac{3\\pi}2)$, of total length $\\frac\\pi2+\\frac\\pi3+\\frac\\pi6=\\pi$. If the sum-to-product step is unfamiliar, expand $\\sin3x=3\\sin x-4\\sin^3x$ and $\\sin 2x=2\\sin x\\cos x$ to reach the same factorisation.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "C2-16", "paper": "C2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Let $a$, $b$, $c$ be real numbers and $f(x)=ax^2+bx+c$. Consider the conditions \\[ (1)\\ a>0,\\qquad (2)\\ b^2&lt;4ac,\\qquad (3)\\ c>0,\\qquad (4)\\ f(x)>0\\text{ for every real } x . \\] Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>Conditions (1) and (2) together imply (3).<\/li><li>Condition (4) implies both (1) and (2).<\/li><li>Conditions (2) and (3) together imply (1).<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: $4ac>b^2\\ge0$ and $a>0$ force $c>0$. II fails: $a=b=0$, $c=1$ gives $f\\equiv1>0$, yet (1) fails. The question does not say $f$ is quadratic, and that is the whole trap. III: $4ac>b^2\\ge0$ with $c>0$ forces $a>0$. So I and III.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "poly"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "D2-20", "paper": "D2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>The sequence $u_1,u_2,u_3,\\dots$ is defined by $u_1=1$ and $u_{n+1}=u_n+\\dfrac{2}{u_n}$. Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$u_{n+1}>u_n$ for every $n$.<\/li><li>$u_n^{\\,2}>4n-4$ for every $n\\ge2$.<\/li><li>$u_{100}>20$<\/li><li>$u_{100}>30$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and IV only", "I and III only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Every $u_n$ is positive, so I holds. Squaring the recurrence gives $u_{n+1}^2=u_n^2+4+\\frac{4}{u_n^2}>u_n^2+4$, and adding these from $n=1$ gives II. II alone gives only $u_{100}^2>396$, not enough for III, so keep the error terms: $u_{100}^2=1+4\\cdot99+\\sum_{n=1}^{99}\\frac4{u_n^2}\\ge397+\\frac{4}{u_1^2}=401>400$, and III holds. For IV, each $\\frac4{u_n^2}\\le4$, so $u_{100}^2\\le1+99\\cdot8=793&lt;900$ and IV is false. (In fact $u_{100}\\approx20.1$.)<\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "ineq"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "G1-10", "paper": "G1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>Let $a>0$. Consider the inequality \\[\\left|x^2-2x-3\\right|\\le a(x+1).\\] For which values of $a$ is its complete solution set a single interval, with no isolated points?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a>0$", "$a\\ge2$", "$a>4$", "$a\\ge4$", "$0&lt;a\\le4$", "no value of $a$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>$x^2-2x-3=(x-3)(x+1)$, so the inequality is $|x-3|\\,|x+1|\\le a(x+1)$. The right side is negative for $x&lt;-1$, so no solutions there. $x=-1$ always works, since both sides are $0$. For $x>-1$, divide by $x+1>0$: $|x-3|\\le a$, that is $3-a\\le x\\le3+a$. So the solution set is $\\{-1\\}\\cup\\left[\\max(-1,3-a),\\,3+a\\right]$. If $a&lt;4$, then $3-a>-1$ and $-1$ is an isolated point. If $a\\ge4$ the set is the single interval $[-1,3+a]$. The isolated solution $x=-1$ is the whole question.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "quad"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "G1-8", "paper": "G1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>An open box is made from a rectangular sheet of card measuring $12$ cm by $8$ cm by cutting a square of side $x$ cm from each corner and folding up the sides. Find the maximum possible volume of the box, in cm$^3$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$64$", "$\\dfrac{1792}{27}$", "$\\dfrac{640+224\\sqrt7}{27}$", "$\\dfrac{448+640\\sqrt7}{27}$", "$70$", "$\\dfrac{640+448\\sqrt7}{27}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$V=x(12-2x)(8-2x)=4x^3-40x^2+96x$ for $0&lt;x&lt;4$. $V'=12x^2-80x+96=4(3x^2-20x+24)$, which is zero at $x=\\frac{10\\pm2\\sqrt7}{3}$; only $x=\\frac{10-2\\sqrt7}{3}\\approx1.57$ lies in $(0,4)$, and it gives the maximum. Substituting, with $\\sqrt7=s$: $12-2x=\\frac{16+4s}{3}$ and $8-2x=\\frac{4+4s}{3}$, so \\[V=\\frac{(10-2s)(16+4s)(4+4s)}{27}=\\frac{32(5-s)(4+s)(1+s)}{27}=\\frac{32(20+14s)}{27}=\\frac{640+448\\sqrt7}{27},\\] since $(4+s)(1+s)=11+5s$ and $(5-s)(11+5s)=20+14s$. That is about $67.6$. The arithmetic is the question, and the options are built to catch slips in it.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "G1-9", "paper": "G1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>The polynomial $p(x)$ has degree $4$ and leading coefficient $1$, and $p(1)=p(2)=p(3)=p(4)=5$. Find $\\displaystyle\\int_1^4 p(x)\\,dx$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$15$", "$\\dfrac{141}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{159}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{33}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{147}{10}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$p(x)-5$ has roots $1,2,3,4$ and leading coefficient $1$, so $p(x)=(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)(x-4)+5$. Centre the interval with $u=x-\\tfrac52$: the product becomes $\\left(u^2-\\tfrac94\\right)\\left(u^2-\\tfrac14\\right)=u^4-\\tfrac52u^2+\\tfrac{9}{16}$, an even function. Its integral over $\\left[-\\tfrac32,\\tfrac32\\right]$ is \\[2\\left[\\tfrac{u^5}{5}-\\tfrac56u^3+\\tfrac{9}{16}u\\right]_0^{3/2}=2\\left(\\tfrac{243}{160}-\\tfrac{45}{16}+\\tfrac{27}{32}\\right)=-\\tfrac{9}{10}.\\] Adding the $5\\times3=15$ from the constant gives $\\tfrac{141}{10}$. Option A ignores the product's contribution because it &ldquo;averages to zero&rdquo;; it does not.<\/p>", "tags": ["poly", "arint", "sym"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "G2-10", "paper": "G2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A student sets out to prove that $3^n\\ge n^3$ for every positive integer $n$.<\/p><p><br> (I) For $n=1$: $3\\ge1$, which is true.<br> (II) Suppose $3^k\\ge k^3$ for some positive integer $k$.<br> (III) Then $3^{k+1}=3\\cdot3^k\\ge3k^3$.<br> (IV) And $3k^3\\ge(k+1)^3$.<br> (V) So $3^{k+1}\\ge(k+1)^3$, and the result follows for every $n$ by repeating the step.<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The argument is valid and the claim is true.", "The argument is invalid, and the claim is false for some $n$.", "The claim is true, but line IV fails for $k=1$ and $k=2$, so the argument does not prove it as written.", "The claim is true, but line III is invalid.", "Line IV is false for every $k\\ge3$.", "The argument would be valid if line I checked $n=2$ instead."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Line IV, $3k^3\\ge(k+1)^3$, is equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]3\\,k\\ge k+1$, which holds only for $k\\ge\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]3-1}\\approx2.26$, that is for $k\\ge3$. It fails at $k=1$ ($3&lt;8$) and $k=2$ ($24&lt;27$), so the chain from $n=1$ breaks immediately. Yet the claim is true: $n=1,2,3$ can be checked directly ($3\\ge1$, $9\\ge8$, $27\\ge27$), and the step works from $k=3$ onwards. A correct proof needs those three starting cases. Option F would still leave the step unjustified at $k=2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "G2-11", "paper": "G2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A function $f$ is differentiable for all real $x$. Its derivative satisfies $f'(x)>0$ for $x&lt;1$, $f'(1)=0$, $f'(x)&lt;0$ for $1&lt;x&lt;3$, $f'(3)=0$ and $f'(x)>0$ for $x>3$. It is also known that $f(1)=2$ and $f(3)=-2$. Nothing else is known about $f$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>The equation $f(x)=0$ has a solution between $1$ and $3$.<\/li><li>The equation $f(x)=0$ has exactly three real solutions.<\/li><li>The equation $f(x)=2$ has exactly two real solutions.<\/li><li>$f(0)&lt;2$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and IV only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I only", "I, III and IV only", "II and III only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>I: $f$ falls continuously from $2$ to $-2$ on $[1,3]$. IV: $f$ is increasing on $(-\\infty,1]$, so $f(0)&lt;f(1)=2$. II and III depend on behaviour at the ends that is not given. $f$ might increase towards a limit such as $1$ as $x\\to-\\infty$, and never be $0$ there; and for $x>3$ it might increase towards $0$ or towards $1$, never reaching $2$. So there could be as few as one solution of $f(x)=0$ and as few as one of $f(x)=2$. Only I and IV are forced.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "root"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "G2-12", "paper": "G2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is continuous and increasing on $[0,1]$, with $f(0)=0$, $f(1)=1$ and $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1f(x)\\,dx=\\frac13$. Let $f^{-1}$ be its inverse function. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1f^{-1}(x)\\,dx=\\frac23$<\/li><li>$f(c)&lt;c$ for some $c$ in $(0,1)$.<\/li><li>$f\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)\\le\\tfrac12$<\/li><li>$f(x)\\le x$ for every $x$ in $[0,1]$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and IV only", "I and II only", "I and IV only"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I: the regions under $f$ and under $f^{-1}$ (reflected) fill the unit square, so the two integrals sum to $1$. II: if $f(x)\\ge x$ throughout, the integral would be at least $\\tfrac12$. III and IV can fail. The piecewise-linear function through $(0,0)$, $\\left(\\tfrac25,\\tfrac1{50}\\right)$, $\\left(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac{13}{25}\\right)$, $\\left(\\tfrac9{10},\\tfrac{89}{150}\\right)$ and $(1,1)$ is increasing, and adding up the trapezia gives an integral of exactly $\\tfrac13$, yet $f\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)=\\tfrac{13}{25}>\\tfrac12$. Keeping $f$ tiny on most of $\\left[0,\\tfrac12\\right]$ pays for the jump. So I and II.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "arint"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "H1-10", "paper": "H1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[\\sum_{k=1}^{99}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt k\\,\\sqrt{k+1}\\,\\left(\\sqrt k+\\sqrt{k+1}\\right)} .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{99}{100}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{10}$", "$\\sqrt{99}-1$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Rationalise the bracket: $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt k+\\sqrt{k+1}}=\\sqrt{k+1}-\\sqrt k$. Then each term is \\[\\frac{\\sqrt{k+1}-\\sqrt k}{\\sqrt k\\sqrt{k+1}}=\\frac1{\\sqrt k}-\\frac1{\\sqrt{k+1}},\\] and the sum telescopes to $1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{100}}=\\frac{9}{10}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["tel", "sym"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "H1-11", "paper": "H1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Let $r_1,r_2,r_3,r_4$ be the four roots of $x^4-3x^2+x+1=0$. Find the value of \\[r_1^5+r_2^5+r_3^5+r_4^5 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-9$", "$9$", "$14$", "$-14$", "$-15$", "$15$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Each root satisfies $r^4=3r^2-r-1$, so $r^5=3r^3-r^2-r$. With $s_k=\\sum r_i^k$: from Vieta, $s_1=0$ and the sum of products in pairs is $-3$, so $s_2=0-2(-3)=6$. The sum of products in threes is $-1$, and $s_3=s_1^3-3s_1e_2+3e_3=3(-1)=-3$. Hence \\[s_5=3s_3-s_2-s_1=-9-6-0=-15 .\\] (Along the way $s_4=3s_2-s_1-4=14$, option C.) No root is ever found.<\/p>", "tags": ["vie", "poly"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "H1-8", "paper": "H1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Find the area of the region of the plane enclosed by the curve \\[|x-2|+|y-3|+|x+y-5|=4 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$4\\sqrt3$", "$10$", "$12$", "$16$", "$18$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Shift to $u=x-2$ and $v=y-3$, so that $x+y-5=u+v$ and the curve is $|u|+|v|+|u+v|=4$. If $u$ and $v$ have the same sign, $|u+v|=|u|+|v|$ and the curve is $|u|+|v|=2$. If they have opposite signs, $|u|+|v|+|u+v|=2\\max(|u|,|v|)$, and the curve is $\\max(|u|,|v|)=2$. So the region is a hexagon made of two triangles of area $2$ (in the same-sign quadrants) and two $2\\times2$ squares (in the opposite-sign quadrants): area $2+2+4+4=12$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "coord"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "H1-9", "paper": "H1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>In triangle $ABC$, $BC=5$, $CA=6$ and $AB=7$. The bisector of angle $C$ meets $AB$ at $D$. Find the length $CD$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{12\\sqrt{15}}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{30}}{3}$", "$\\sqrt{15}$", "$\\dfrac{35}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{12\\sqrt5}{5}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The bisector divides $AB$ in the ratio $CA:CB=6:5$, so $BD=\\tfrac{35}{11}$. Apply the cosine rule twice at $B$: $\\cos B=\\frac{25+49-36}{70}=\\frac{19}{35}$, and then \\[CD^2=25+\\left(\\tfrac{35}{11}\\right)^2-2\\cdot5\\cdot\\tfrac{35}{11}\\cdot\\tfrac{19}{35}=25+\\tfrac{1225}{121}-\\tfrac{190}{11}=\\tfrac{3025+1225-2090}{121}=\\tfrac{2160}{121}.\\] So $CD=\\frac{\\sqrt{2160}}{11}=\\frac{12\\sqrt{15}}{11}$. The shortcut $CD^2=ab\\left(1-\\frac{c^2}{(a+b)^2}\\right)=30\\cdot\\frac{72}{121}$ gives the same, and is worth deriving once.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "H2-13", "paper": "H2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>There is a polynomial $p$ of degree $3$ with $p(0)=0$, $p(1)=1$, $p(2)=4$ and $p(3)=10$.<\/li><li>There is a polynomial $p$ of degree at most $2$ with $p(0)=0$, $p(1)=1$, $p(2)=4$ and $p(3)=10$.<\/li><li>There are infinitely many polynomials $p$ of degree $4$ with $p(0)=0$, $p(1)=1$, $p(2)=4$ and $p(3)=10$.<\/li><li>There is a polynomial $p$ with integer coefficients and $p(0)=0$, $p(1)=1$, $p(2)=4$ and $p(3)=10$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, III and IV only", "I and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "III only", "I and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Four values determine a unique polynomial of degree at most $3$; here it is $\\frac{x^3+3x^2+2x}{6}$, of degree exactly $3$: I. II fails because a quadratic through the first three points would be $x^2$, and $3^2\\neq10$. III: add any multiple of $x(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)$. IV fails for a subtler reason: for integer coefficients, $a-b$ always divides $p(a)-p(b)$, since $a^k-b^k$ is divisible by $a-b$. But $3-0=3$ does not divide $10-0=10$.<\/p>", "tags": ["poly", "mod"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "H2-14", "paper": "H2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>Consider the four sets of real numbers \\[A=\\{x:\\ |x|&lt;\\tfrac1n\\text{ for every positive integer }n\\},\\qquad B=\\{x:\\ |x|&lt;\\tfrac1n\\text{ for some positive integer }n\\},\\] \\[C=\\{x:\\ x&lt;n\\text{ for every positive integer }n\\},\\qquad D=\\{x:\\ x>n\\text{ for some positive integer }n\\}.\\] Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$A=\\{0\\}$<\/li><li>$B$ is the interval $-1&lt;x&lt;1$.<\/li><li>$C$ is the set of all real numbers.<\/li><li>$D$ is the set of all real numbers.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only", "I and III only", "I and II only", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I: a non-zero $x$ fails once $\\frac1n&lt;|x|$. II: some $n$ works exactly when $n=1$ works, that is $|x|&lt;1$. III fails: $C=\\{x:x&lt;1\\}$, since the condition for $n=1$ is the strongest. IV fails: $D=\\{x:x>1\\}$, since the condition for $n=1$ is the weakest. So I and II. &ldquo;For every&rdquo; makes the strongest instance decisive, and &ldquo;for some&rdquo; the weakest.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ineq"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I1-10", "paper": "I1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>Let $k>0$ be a constant. For each real number $t$, the tangent to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $(t,t^2)$ meets the curve $y=x^2-k$ at two points, and the tangent and this second curve together enclose a finite region $R_t$. It turns out that the area of $R_t$ is the same for every value of $t$. Given that this area is $36$, find $k$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$3$", "$6$", "$9$", "$27$", "$3\\sqrt[3]{4}$", "$12$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The tangent is $y=2tx-t^2$. It meets $y=x^2-k$ where $x^2-2tx+t^2-k=0$, that is $(x-t)^2=k$, so $x=t\\pm\\sqrt k$: the chord always has horizontal width $2\\sqrt k$, whatever $t$ is. The region between a line and the parabola $y=x^2-k$ over an interval of width $w$ has area $\\frac{w^3}{6}$ (the quadratic difference is $(x-x_1)(x_2-x)$). So the area is $\\frac{(2\\sqrt k)^3}{6}=\\frac43k^{3/2}=36$, giving $k^{3/2}=27$ and $k=9$. Seeing why the area cannot depend on $t$ is the heart of the question.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "arint"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I1-11", "paper": "I1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>For which real values of $k$ does the equation \\[3\\sin x+4\\cos x=k\\] have exactly one solution with $0\\le x\\le\\pi$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-4\\le k&lt;4$, or $k=5$", "$-5\\le k\\le5$", "$-4&lt;k&lt;4$", "$-4\\le k\\le4$", "$k=-5$ or $k=5$", "$4&lt;k&lt;5$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Write $3\\sin x+4\\cos x=5\\sin(x+\\alpha)$, where $\\tan\\alpha=\\frac43$ and $0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\frac\\pi2$, so $\\sin\\alpha=\\frac45$. As $x$ runs from $0$ to $\\pi$, $x+\\alpha$ runs from $\\alpha$ to $\\pi+\\alpha$, so the expression starts at $4$, rises to $5$ at $x=\\frac\\pi2-\\alpha$, and then falls steadily to $-4$ at $x=\\pi$. A level $k$ is met twice when $4\\le k&lt;5$ (once on the rise, which includes $x=0$ when $k=4$, and once on the fall), once at $k=5$, and once for $-4\\le k&lt;4$, on the fall only. So the answer is $-4\\le k&lt;4$ or $k=5$. The endpoint $k=4$ is the trap: $x=0$ is a genuine solution there.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "root"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I1-12", "paper": "I1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Let $m\\ge2$ be an integer. Four different numbers are chosen at random from the integers $1,2,3,\\dots,3m$. Which of the following is the probability that the sum of the four numbers is divisible by $3$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac13-\\dfrac{1}{9m}$", "$\\dfrac13$, whatever the value of $m$", "$\\dfrac13+\\dfrac{2}{9(3m-1)}$", "$\\dfrac{m}{3m-1}$", "$\\dfrac{m+1}{3m+1}$", "it depends on $m$ and has no simple form"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Counting residue patterns works but is long. Instead, consider the map that adds $1$ to each chosen number, sending $3m$ back to $1$. It permutes the four-element subsets, and it changes each chosen number by an amount that is $1$ modulo $3$ (including the wrap-around $3m\\to1$, a change of $1-3m$). So it changes the sum by $4\\equiv1\\pmod 3$. It therefore maps the subsets with sum $\\equiv0$ one-to-one onto those with sum $\\equiv1$, and those onto sum $\\equiv2$. All three classes are equally large, and the probability is exactly $\\tfrac13$ for every $m$. For three numbers the same shift changes the sum by $3\\equiv0$, which is why that case gives $\\tfrac{68}{203}$ for $m=10$ rather than $\\tfrac13$.<\/p>", "tags": ["cnt", "mod", "sym"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I1-13", "paper": "I1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>The curve with parametric equations \\[x=t^2-1,\\qquad y=t^3-t\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb{R})\\] crosses itself at exactly one point, forming a closed loop. Find the area enclosed by the loop.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac4{15}$", "$\\dfrac8{15}$", "$\\dfrac23$", "$\\dfrac{16}{15}$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac45$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Two parameter values give the same $x=t^2-1$ only if they are $t$ and $-t$, and since $y(-t)=-y(t)$, the points coincide only when $y=t(t^2-1)=0$ with $t\\neq0$, so $t=\\pm1$ and the crossing point is $(0,0)$. The loop is traced for $-1\\le t\\le1$, and its area is \\[\\left|\\int_{-1}^{1}y\\,\\frac{dx}{dt}\\,dt\\right|=\\left|\\int_{-1}^1(t^3-t)(2t)\\,dt\\right|=2\\left|\\tfrac25-\\tfrac23\\right|=\\tfrac{8}{15}.\\] At the crossing the tangent gradients are $\\frac{3t^2-1}{2t}=\\pm1$, so the two branches cross at right angles, a pleasant check on any sketch.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "diff"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I1-6", "paper": "I1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>The cubic $f(x)=x^3+ax^2+bx+c$ satisfies $f(0)=0$ and $f(1)=1$, and it is increasing: $f'(x)\\ge0$ for every real $x$. Find the set of all possible values of $f(2)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$f(2)=8$ only", "$0\\le f(2)\\le8$", "$2&lt;f(2)&lt;8$", "$f(2)\\ge2$", "$2\\le f(2)\\le8$", "$4\\le f(2)\\le8$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>$f(0)=0$ gives $c=0$, and $f(1)=1$ gives $b=-a$. Then $f'(x)=3x^2+2ax-a\\ge0$ for all $x$ exactly when the discriminant $4a^2+12a\\le0$, that is $-3\\le a\\le0$. And $f(2)=8+4a+2b=8+2a$, which ranges over $[2,8]$. Both ends are attained: $a=0$ gives $x^3$, and $a=-3$ gives $x^3-3x^2+3x=(x-1)^3+1$, whose stationary point of inflection still counts as increasing.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "poly"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I1-7", "paper": "I1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>For which values of $a$ does the equation $x^3-3ax+2=0$ have three distinct real roots, all greater than $-3$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1&lt;a&lt;\\dfrac{25}{9}$", "$a>1$", "$0&lt;a&lt;\\dfrac{25}{9}$", "$1&lt;a&lt;9$", "$1&lt;a&lt;3$", "no value of $a$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Three real roots need $a>0$, with turning points at $x=\\pm\\sqrt a$. The local maximum value is $f(-\\sqrt a)=2+2a\\sqrt a>0$ always, and the local minimum value is $f(\\sqrt a)=2-2a\\sqrt a$, which is negative exactly when $a>1$. The smallest root exceeds $-3$ exactly when $f(-3)&lt;0$ and $-3$ lies to the left of the local maximum ($\\sqrt a&lt;3$). $f(-3)=9a-25&lt;0$ gives $a&lt;\\tfrac{25}{9}$, which also ensures $a&lt;9$. So $1&lt;a&lt;\\tfrac{25}{9}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "diff"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I1-8", "paper": "I1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>In triangle $ABC$, $AB=x$, $AC=y$ and angle $BAC=120^\\circ$. The bisector of angle $A$ meets $BC$ at $D$, with $AD=2$. Given also that $x+y=12$, find $BC$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$4\\sqrt{6}$", "$2\\sqrt{33}$", "$6\\sqrt3$", "$2\\sqrt{30}$", "$\\sqrt{132}$", "$8$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Split triangle $ABC$ along $AD$ into two triangles with angles $60^\\circ$ at $A$, and add their areas: $\\tfrac12xy\\sin120^\\circ=\\tfrac12\\cdot2x\\sin60^\\circ+\\tfrac12\\cdot2y\\sin60^\\circ$. The sines cancel, leaving $xy=2(x+y)=24$. This is the pleasant fact that a $120^\\circ$ bisector has length $\\frac{xy}{x+y}$. Then the cosine rule gives $BC^2=x^2+y^2+xy=(x+y)^2-xy=144-24=120$, so $BC=2\\sqrt{30}$. Neither $x$ nor $y$ needs to be found.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I1-9", "paper": "I1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>For $a>0$ let \\[I(a)=\\int_0^3\\left|x^2-ax\\right|dx .\\] Find the minimum value of $I(a)$ over all $a>0$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac92$", "$\\dfrac{9}{4}$", "$9-3\\sqrt3$", "$\\dfrac{9\\sqrt2}{2}$", "$3$", "$\\dfrac{18-9\\sqrt2}{2}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>For $a\\ge3$ the integrand is $ax-x^2$ throughout, and $I(a)=\\tfrac92a-9$ is increasing, so it is at least $\\tfrac92$. For $0&lt;a&lt;3$, split at $x=a$: $\\int_0^a(ax-x^2)\\,dx=\\tfrac{a^3}{6}$ and $\\int_a^3(x^2-ax)\\,dx=9-\\tfrac92a+\\tfrac{a^3}{6}$, so $I(a)=\\tfrac{a^3}{3}-\\tfrac92a+9$. This has $I'(a)=a^2-\\tfrac92=0$ at $a=\\tfrac{3}{\\sqrt2}$, giving $I=9-\\tfrac{9}{\\sqrt2}=\\tfrac{18-9\\sqrt2}{2}\\approx2.64$, less than $\\tfrac92$. Option A is the value at $a=3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "diff"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I2-10", "paper": "I2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A triangle has sides $a\\le b\\le c$ that form an arithmetic progression, so $a+c=2b$. Let $B$ and $C$ be the angles opposite $b$ and $c$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$B\\le60^\\circ$<\/li><li>The triangle is not right-angled.<\/li><li>$3a>c$<\/li><li>$C&lt;90^\\circ$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, III and IV only", "I and III only", "III only", "I, II and III only", "II and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: substituting $b=\\tfrac{a+c}{2}$, $\\cos B=\\frac{a^2+c^2-b^2}{2ac}=\\frac{3a^2+3c^2-2ac}{8ac}\\ge\\frac{6ac-2ac}{8ac}=\\frac12$. III: the triangle inequality $a+b>c$ becomes $a+\\tfrac{a+c}{2}>c$, that is $3a>c$. II fails for $3,4,5$. IV fails too: $2,\\ 3.5,\\ 5$ is an arithmetic progression and a valid triangle, but $5^2>2^2+3.5^2$, so $C$ is obtuse. So I and III.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I2-11", "paper": "I2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A bag contains $n$ balls numbered $1,2,\\dots,n$, where $n\\ge2$. Two balls are drawn at random without replacement. For which values of $n$ is the probability that the sum of the two numbers is even exactly $\\tfrac{7}{15}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$n=15$ only", "$n=16$ only", "$n=14$ and $n=15$", "$n=30$ only", "$n=15$ and $n=16$", "no value of $n$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The sum is even when both numbers have the same parity. If $n=2m$, there are $m$ of each parity, and the probability is $\\frac{2\\binom m2}{\\binom{2m}2}=\\frac{m-1}{2m-1}$; setting this to $\\tfrac7{15}$ gives $m=8$, so $n=16$. If $n=2m+1$, there are $m+1$ odd and $m$ even numbers, and the probability is $\\frac{\\binom{m+1}2+\\binom m2}{\\binom{2m+1}2}=\\frac{m}{2m+1}$; this gives $m=7$, so $n=15$. Both work, and only the two-case analysis finds both.<\/p>", "tags": ["cnt", "mod"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I2-12", "paper": "I2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Without evaluating them exactly, decide which of the following integrals are strictly positive. <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi}x\\sin x\\,dx$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}x\\sin x\\,dx$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{1}x^3e^{x^2}\\,dx$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{\\sin x}{1+x}\\,dx$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and IV only", "I and IV only", "IV only", "I, III and IV only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: the integrand is positive on $(0,\\pi)$. II: on $(\\pi,2\\pi)$, $\\sin x$ is negative and the weight $x$ is larger there than on $(0,\\pi)$, so the negative half wins (in fact the value is $-2\\pi$). III: the integrand is odd, so the integral is $0$, not positive. IV: $\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x$ pairs each point of the positive half with one of the negative half, and the weight $\\frac1{1+x}$ is larger on the positive half, so the integral is positive. Here the decreasing weight favours the first hump, where in II the increasing weight favoured the second.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "trg"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I2-8", "paper": "I2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>A bag contains $r$ red balls and $b$ blue balls, where $r,b\\ge1$. Two balls are drawn at random without replacement. Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>The two balls are equally likely to be the same colour as different colours exactly when $(r-b)^2=r+b$.<\/li><li>If $r=b$, the probability that the two balls are different colours is greater than $\\tfrac12$.<\/li><li>It is possible for the probability that both balls are red to equal exactly $\\tfrac12$.<\/li><li>The probability that the two balls are different colours never exceeds $\\tfrac23$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III only", "all four", "I, III and IV only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>With $n=r+b$: $P(\\text{same})=\\frac{r(r-1)+b(b-1)}{n(n-1)}$ and $P(\\text{different})=\\frac{2rb}{n(n-1)}$. I: equating gives $r^2+b^2-r-b=2rb$, that is $(r-b)^2=r+b$. II: $r=b$ gives $\\frac{2r^2}{2r(2r-1)}=\\frac{r}{2r-1}>\\tfrac12$. III: $r=3$, $b=1$ gives $\\frac{3\\cdot2}{4\\cdot3}=\\tfrac12$. IV fails for $r=b=1$, where the balls are certainly different colours.<\/p>", "tags": ["cnt", "quad"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "I2-9", "paper": "I2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>Which of the following counts are correct? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>There are $4$ strictly increasing functions from $\\{1,2,3\\}$ to $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$.<\/li><li>There are $20$ non-decreasing functions from $\\{1,2,3\\}$ to $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$.<\/li><li>There are $36$ functions from $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ onto $\\{1,2,3\\}$ (every value is taken).<\/li><li>There are $12$ one-to-one functions from $\\{1,2,3\\}$ to $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I, II and III only", "I and II only", "I and IV only", "all four", "II and III only", "I only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>I: an increasing function is determined by its set of three values, so $\\binom43=4$. II: a non-decreasing function is a choice of three values with repetition allowed, arranged in order, so $\\binom{4+3-1}{3}=20$. III: of the $3^4=81$ functions, remove those missing a value: $3\\cdot2^4=48$ miss at least one, but the $3$ constant functions have been removed twice, so add them back: $81-48+3=36$. IV fails: there are $4\\cdot3\\cdot2=24$; the figure $12$ comes from wrongly dividing by $2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["cnt"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "C1-17", "paper": "C1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Find the total length of the set of values of $x$ in $[0,2\\pi]$ for which \\[ |\\sin x|+|\\cos x|>\\sqrt{\\tfrac32}\\, . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$", "$\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Both sides are positive, so squaring is reversible: $(|\\sin x|+|\\cos x|)^2=1+2|\\sin x\\cos x|=1+|\\sin2x|$. The inequality becomes $1+|\\sin2x|>\\frac32$, that is $|\\sin2x|>\\frac12$. As $x$ runs over $[0,2\\pi]$, $2x$ runs over $[0,4\\pi]$, two full periods, and in each period $|\\sin\\phi|>\\frac12$ on a set of length $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$. So the set of $2x$ has length $\\frac{8\\pi}{3}$ and the set of $x$ has length $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$. Forgetting to halve at the end gives $\\frac{8\\pi}{3}$; forgetting that there are two periods gives option C.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "B2-6", "paper": "B2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Let $f$ be a continuous function on $[0,2]$ with $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx = 0$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>There is some $c$ with $0 &lt; c &lt; 2$ and $\\displaystyle\\int_0^c f(x)\\,dx = 0$.<\/li><li>There is some $c$ with $0 &lt; c &lt; 2$ and $f(c) = 0$.<\/li><li>There is some $c$ with $0 \\le c \\le 1$ and $\\displaystyle\\int_c^{c+1} f(x)\\,dx = 0$.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Let $F(c) = \\int_0^c f$, so $F(0) = F(2) = 0$. For II, $F$ is differentiable with $F' = f$, and a function that starts and ends at $0$ must have zero derivative somewhere in between (if $F$ is not identically zero it has an interior maximum or minimum, where $F' = 0$; if it is identically zero then $f \\equiv 0$). So II holds. For III, let $g(c) = \\int_c^{c+1} f$ for $0 \\le c \\le 1$. Then $g(0) + g(1) = \\int_0^1 f + \\int_1^2 f = 0$, so $g(0)$ and $g(1)$ are of opposite sign or both zero, and since $g$ is continuous it vanishes somewhere on $[0,1]$. So III holds. I fails: $f(x) = 1 - x$ has $\\int_0^2 f = 0$ but $F(c) = c - \\tfrac12 c^2 = \\tfrac12 c(2-c) > 0$ for $0 &lt; c &lt; 2$. The answer is II and III. The idea in III, adding the two half-length integrals to force a sign change, is worth remembering; it is the reason there is always a unit-length interval on which $f$ has zero average.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "G1-11", "paper": "G1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Let $k>0$. The finite region enclosed between the curve $y=\\left|x^2-k\\right|$ and the line $y=k$ has area $\\dfrac{64}{3}\\left(\\sqrt2-1\\right)$. Find $k$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$2$", "$8$", "$2\\sqrt2$", "$16$", "$\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The curve meets $y=k$ at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm\\sqrt{2k}$. Write $k=s^2$. By symmetry the area is twice the area for $x\\ge0$, which splits at $x=s$: \\[2\\left[\\int_0^s\\left(k-(k-x^2)\\right)dx+\\int_s^{s\\sqrt2}\\left(k-(x^2-k)\\right)dx\\right]=2\\left[\\frac{s^3}{3}+\\left(2s^2\\cdot(\\sqrt2-1)s-\\frac{(2\\sqrt2-1)s^3}{3}\\right)\\right].\\] This simplifies to $\\frac{8}{3}(\\sqrt2-1)s^3$. Setting it equal to $\\frac{64}{3}(\\sqrt2-1)$ gives $s^3=8$, so $s=2$ and $k=4$. The area scales like $k^{3/2}$, which is why doubling a guessed $k$ does not double the area.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "quad"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "G1-12", "paper": "G1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>For each real $k$, let $N(k)$ be the number of real solutions of the equation \\[\\left|x^2-4|x|+3\\right|=k .\\] Find $N(0)+N(1)+N(2)+N(3)+N(4)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$15$", "$17$", "$19$", "$20$", "$21$", "$23$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Put $t=|x|\\ge0$; each positive $t$ gives two values of $x$ and $t=0$ gives one. The graph of $|t^2-4t+3|=|(t-1)(t-3)|$ for $t\\ge0$ starts at $3$, falls to $0$ at $t=1$, rises to a local maximum $1$ at $t=2$, falls to $0$ at $t=3$, then increases. $k=0$: $t=1,3$, so $N=4$. $k=1$: $t=2\\mp\\sqrt2$ and the touching point $t=2$, so $N=6$. $k=2$: one $t$ in $(0,1)$ and one beyond $3$, so $N=4$. $k=3$: $t=0$ (one value of $x$) and $t=4$, so $N=3$. $k=4$: one $t$ beyond $3$, so $N=2$. The total is $19$. The two traps are counting $t=0$ twice at $k=3$ and missing the local maximum at $k=1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "ineq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "G1-13", "paper": "G1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>How many real solutions does the equation \\[2^x+3^x=4^x+1\\] have?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "infinitely many"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>$x=0$ and $x=1$ both work. To see there are no others, compare the two sides as sums of powers of numbers with the same total, $1+4=2+3$. For fixed $x$, the function $t\\mapsto t^x$ is convex when $x>1$ or $x&lt;0$, so the outer pair $1,4$ beats the inner pair $2,3$ and $1^x+4^x>2^x+3^x$; when $0&lt;x&lt;1$ it is concave and the inequality reverses. Either way equality can only occur at $x=0$ or $x=1$. So there are exactly two solutions.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "root"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "G2-13", "paper": "G2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=ax^2+bx+c$, where $a$, $b$ and $c$ are real. Which of the following conditions are <em>sufficient<\/em> for $f(x)>0$ for every $x$ in the interval $[0,1]$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$a>0$ and $b^2&lt;4ac$<\/li><li>$f(0)>0$ and $f(1)>0$<\/li><li>$f(0)>0$, $f\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)>0$ and $f(1)>0$<\/li><li>$c>0$, $a+b+c>0$ and $b\\ge0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, III and IV only", "IV only", "I and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "III and IV only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I makes $f$ positive everywhere. IV: $c=f(0)>0$ and $a+b+c=f(1)>0$. If $a>0$ the vertex $-\\frac b{2a}\\le0$, so $f$ is increasing on $[0,1]$ and its minimum there is $f(0)>0$. If $a\\le0$ the graph is a downward parabola or a line, whose minimum on an interval is at an endpoint. Either way $f>0$ on $[0,1]$. II and III fail: $f(x)=100\\left(x-\\tfrac14\\right)^2-1$ has $f(0)=f\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)=5.25$ and $f(1)=55.25$, but $f\\!\\left(\\tfrac14\\right)=-1$. Sampling points can never rule out a narrow dip.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "quad"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "G2-14", "paper": "G2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>Consider cubic equations $x^3+px+q=0$ with real $p$ and $q$. Which of the following conditions are <em>sufficient<\/em> for the equation to have three distinct real roots? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$p&lt;0$<\/li><li>$p&lt;-3$ and $|q|&lt;2$<\/li><li>$q=0$ and $p&lt;0$<\/li><li>$p&lt;0$ and $q>0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III only", "III only", "II, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Let $g(x)=x^3+px$. For $p&lt;0$ it has stationary values $\\pm2\\left(-\\tfrac p3\\right)^{3/2}$, and the equation $g(x)=-q$ has three distinct roots exactly when $|q|&lt;2\\left(-\\tfrac p3\\right)^{3/2}$. II: $p&lt;-3$ makes that bound exceed $2$, so $|q|&lt;2$ suffices. III: the roots are $0$ and $\\pm\\sqrt{-p}$. I and IV fail for $p=-3$, $q=5$: the bound is $2$, and $x^3-3x+5$ has only one real root.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "root"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "G2-15", "paper": "G2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>A student claims that $x+\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\ge2$ for every $x>0$, and argues as follows: &ldquo;By the AM-GM inequality, $x+\\dfrac1{x^2}\\ge2\\sqrt{x\\cdot\\dfrac1{x^2}}=\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt x}$, and $\\dfrac2{\\sqrt x}\\ge2$.&rdquo; Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The argument is valid and the claim is true.", "The claim is true, but AM-GM does not apply to these two terms.", "The claim is true, but the argument only proves it for $x\\le1$.", "The claim is true for all $x>0$ except $x=1$.", "The claim is false: the argument fails because $\\frac2{\\sqrt x}\\ge2$ only for $x\\le1$, and the least value of $x+\\frac1{x^2}$ is less than $2$.", "The claim is false only for $x>4$."], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The AM-GM step is correct, but its right-hand side is not a constant, and $\\frac2{\\sqrt x}\\ge2$ holds only for $x\\le1$. The claim itself is false. The minimum of $x+\\frac1{x^2}$ is at $1-\\frac2{x^3}=0$, that is $x=\\sqrt[3]2$, where it equals $\\sqrt[3]2+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]4}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]4}\\approx1.89&lt;2$. For instance $x=1.2$ gives about $1.894$. AM-GM gives a useful bound only when the product of the terms is constant: here, split $x$ as $\\frac x2+\\frac x2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "ineq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "H1-12", "paper": "H1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>From the point $(1,-3)$ two tangents are drawn to the parabola $y=x^2$. Find the area of the finite region bounded by the parabola and these two tangents.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{8}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{32}{3}$", "$4$", "$8$", "$\\dfrac{64}{3}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The tangent at $x=t$ is $y=2tx-t^2$, which passes through $(1,-3)$ when $t^2-2t-3=0$, so $t=-1$ or $t=3$. The tangents are $y=-2x-1$ and $y=6x-9$, meeting at $x=1$. The parabola minus each tangent is a perfect square, $(x+1)^2$ and $(x-3)^2$ respectively, so the area is \\[\\int_{-1}^1(x+1)^2\\,dx+\\int_1^3(x-3)^2\\,dx=\\tfrac83+\\tfrac83=\\tfrac{16}{3}.\\] In general the area for the point $(p,q)$ is $\\tfrac23(p^2-q)^{3/2}$; here $p^2-q=4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "diff"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "H1-13", "paper": "H1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[\\sum_{k=1}^{10}\\frac{k}{k^4+k^2+1} .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{55}{111}$", "$\\dfrac{110}{111}$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac{100}{201}$", "$\\dfrac{50}{101}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{112}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Factorise the denominator as a difference of squares: $k^4+k^2+1=(k^2+1)^2-k^2=(k^2-k+1)(k^2+k+1)$. The two factors differ by $2k$, so \\[\\frac{k}{k^4+k^2+1}=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{1}{k^2-k+1}-\\frac{1}{k^2+k+1}\\right),\\] and $k^2+k+1$ is the next value of $k^2-k+1$. The sum telescopes to $\\frac12\\left(1-\\frac{1}{111}\\right)=\\frac{55}{111}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["tel", "poly"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "H1-14", "paper": "H1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>Find the minimum value, over all real $x$ and $y$, of \\[\\sqrt{x^2+1}+\\sqrt{(y-x)^2+4}+\\sqrt{(6-y)^2+9} .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$3+\\sqrt{13}$", "$6\\sqrt2$", "$9$", "$2\\sqrt{17}$", "$\\sqrt{37}+\\sqrt5$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Read each root as the length of a segment: from $(0,0)$ to $(x,1)$, then from $(x,1)$ to $(y,3)$, then from $(y,3)$ to $(6,6)$. The three segments form a path from $(0,0)$ to $(6,6)$, so their total length is at least the straight-line distance $\\sqrt{36+36}=6\\sqrt2$. Equality needs the points collinear on $y=x$, which happens at $x=1$, $y=3$. So the minimum is $6\\sqrt2$. Calculus in two variables would get there too, far more slowly.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "ineq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "H1-15", "paper": "H1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Find the set of all real numbers $x$ for which $x$, $x^2$ and $x^3$ are the side lengths of a non-degenerate triangle.<\/p>", "opts": ["$0&lt;x&lt;\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$", "$\\dfrac12&lt;x&lt;2$", "$1&lt;x&lt;\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}&lt;x&lt;\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}&lt;x&lt;1$", "$x=1$ only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>We need $x>0$, and then only the inequality involving the longest side matters. If $x>1$, the longest side is $x^3$, and we need $x+x^2>x^3$, that is $x^2-x-1&lt;0$, so $x&lt;\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$. If $x&lt;1$, the longest side is $x$, and we need $x^2+x^3>x$, that is $x^2+x-1>0$, so $x>\\frac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}$. At $x=1$ the triangle is equilateral. The answer interval is symmetric under $x\\mapsto\\frac1x$, as it must be: scaling the sides $x,x^2,x^3$ by $x^{-4}$ gives $x^{-3},x^{-2},x^{-1}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "quad"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "H1-16", "paper": "H1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Evaluate the infinite series \\[\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{n^2}{2^n}=\\frac12+\\frac44+\\frac98+\\frac{16}{16}+\\cdots .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac92$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Let $S=\\sum\\frac{n^2}{2^n}$ and $T=\\sum\\frac{n}{2^n}$. Shifting the index, $\\frac S2=\\sum\\frac{(n-1)^2}{2^n}$, so $S-\\frac S2=\\sum\\frac{2n-1}{2^n}=2T-1$. Similarly $T-\\frac T2=\\sum\\frac{1}{2^n}=1$, so $T=2$. Then $\\frac S2=3$ and $S=6$. The subtraction trick removes one power of $n$ at a time.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "H2-15", "paper": "H2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is continuous on $[0,1]$, satisfies $0\\le f(x)\\le1$ for every $x$, and $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1f(x)\\,dx=\\tfrac12$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)^2dx\\le\\tfrac12$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)^2dx\\ge\\tfrac14$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1xf(x)\\,dx\\le\\tfrac38$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1xf(x)\\,dx=\\tfrac14$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "III only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: $0\\le f\\le1$ gives $f^2\\le f$. II: $\\int(f-\\tfrac12)^2\\ge0$ expands to $\\int f^2-\\int f+\\tfrac14\\ge0$, so $\\int f^2\\ge\\tfrac14$. III: $\\int x f$ is largest when the available &ldquo;mass&rdquo; $\\tfrac12$ sits as far right as possible, and it is at most the value for $f=1$ on $\\left[\\tfrac12,1\\right]$, which is $\\tfrac38$. Formally, $\\int\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)\\left(f(x)-\\mathbf 1_{[1/2,1]}\\right)dx\\le0$, because the two factors never share a sign. IV fails: $f(x)=x$ gives $\\int xf=\\tfrac13$.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "ineq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "H2-16", "paper": "H2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>For each positive integer $n$, let $d(n)$ be the highest common factor of $n^2+1$ and $(n+1)^2+1$. Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$d(n)$ is always either $1$ or $5$.<\/li><li>$d(2)=5$.<\/li><li>$d(n)=5$ for infinitely many $n$.<\/li><li>$d(n)=2$ for some $n$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, II and III only", "I and II only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only", "I and III only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Use the Euclidean algorithm. $d$ divides the difference $(n+1)^2+1-(n^2+1)=2n+1$, and hence divides $4(n^2+1)-(2n+1)(2n-1)=5$. So $d\\in\\{1,5\\}$: I holds and IV fails. II: $5$ and $10$ have highest common factor $5$. III: $d(n)=5$ whenever $5\\mid n^2+1$ and $5\\mid 2n+1$, which happens for every $n\\equiv2\\pmod5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "H2-17", "paper": "H2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A continuous function $f$ satisfies $f(x+1)=f(x)+1$ for every real $x$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(n)=n+f(0)$ for every integer $n$.<\/li><li>The function $g(x)=f(x)-x$ is bounded.<\/li><li>$f$ is increasing.<\/li><li>If $f(0)=0$, then $f(x)\\ge x-1$ for every $x$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I only", "I, II and III only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "II only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$g(x+1)=f(x+1)-(x+1)=g(x)$, so $g$ is continuous with period $1$. I follows (apply the relation $n$ times, forwards or backwards). II: $g$ is bounded, being continuous on $[0,1]$ and repeating. III fails: $f(x)=x+\\sin(2\\pi x)$ satisfies the condition but decreases where $1+2\\pi\\cos(2\\pi x)&lt;0$. IV fails: $f(x)=x-5\\sin^2(\\pi x)$ has $f(0)=0$ and $f\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)=\\tfrac12-5&lt;-\\tfrac12$. So I and II.<\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "log"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "I1-14", "paper": "I1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A square of side $1$ is rotated about its centre through an acute angle $\\theta$. The region common to the original square and the rotated square has area $\\tfrac45$. Find the value of $\\tan\\theta+\\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta}$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac54$", "$\\dfrac{25}{12}$", "$\\dfrac85$", "$\\dfrac{12}{5}$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{16}{5}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The overlap is the original square minus four congruent right-angled triangles at its corners. Each has legs $p$ and $q=p\\tan\\theta$, and its hypotenuse lies along a side of the rotated square, so along each side of the original square $p+q+\\sqrt{p^2+q^2}=1$. This gives $p=\\frac{\\cos\\theta}{1+\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta}$ and $q=\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{1+\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta}$. With $s=\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta$, so that $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=s^2-1$, the overlap is $1-2pq=1-\\frac{s^2-1}{(1+s)^2}=\\frac{2}{1+s}$. Setting this to $\\tfrac45$ gives $s=\\tfrac32$, so $\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=\\tfrac58$ and $\\tan\\theta+\\frac1{\\tan\\theta}=\\frac{1}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}=\\tfrac85$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "coord"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "I1-15", "paper": "I1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Numbers $a$ and $b$ are chosen independently and uniformly at random from the interval $-1\\le t\\le1$. What is the probability that the equation \\[x^2+2ax+b=0\\] has two distinct real roots, both strictly between $-1$ and $1$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac1{12}$", "$\\dfrac23$", "$\\dfrac16$", "$\\dfrac18$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Let $g(x)=x^2+2ax+b$. The conditions are $a^2>b$ (distinct real roots), the vertex $-a$ in $(-1,1)$ (automatic), $g(1)=1+2a+b>0$ and $g(-1)=1-2a+b>0$. So $2|a|-1&lt;b&lt;a^2$, and this interval is never empty, since $a^2-2|a|+1=(|a|-1)^2\\ge0$. The favourable area is $\\int_{-1}^1(|a|-1)^2\\,da=2\\int_0^1(1-a)^2\\,da=\\tfrac23$, out of a total area of $4$. The probability is $\\tfrac16$. Option D forgets to divide by the area of the square.<\/p>", "tags": ["cnt", "quad", "arint"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "I1-16", "paper": "I1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The curve $y=x^3+ax^2+bx$, where $a$ and $b$ are real constants, has two distinct stationary points. For which values of $a$ and $b$ does the straight line through these two stationary points pass through the origin?<\/p>", "opts": ["exactly when $a=0$", "exactly when $b=0$", "exactly when $ab=0$", "exactly when $a^2=4b$", "exactly when $a=b$", "for every such $a$ and $b$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Divide $f(x)=x^3+ax^2+bx$ by $f'(x)=3x^2+2ax+b$: \\[f(x)=\\left(\\tfrac x3+\\tfrac a9\\right)f'(x)+\\left(\\tfrac{2b}{3}-\\tfrac{2a^2}{9}\\right)x-\\tfrac{ab}{9}.\\] At each stationary point $f'=0$, so both lie on the line $y=\\left(\\frac{2b}{3}-\\frac{2a^2}{9}\\right)x-\\frac{ab}{9}$, which is therefore the line through them. It passes through the origin exactly when $ab=0$. (The condition for two stationary points is $a^2>3b$, so $a=0$ requires $b&lt;0$, while $b=0$ requires only $a\\neq0$.) Finding the two stationary points explicitly works, but only after heavy surd algebra.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "poly"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "I1-17", "paper": "I1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\sin^3x}{\\sin x+\\cos x}\\,dx .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi-1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi-2}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi+1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}-\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>No direct substitution works. Let $I$ be the integral and $J$ the same integral with $\\cos^3x$ on top. The substitution $x\\mapsto\\frac\\pi2-x$ swaps $\\sin$ and $\\cos$, so $I=J$. Adding, and using $s^3+c^3=(s+c)(s^2-sc+c^2)$: \\[I+J=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\left(1-\\sin x\\cos x\\right)dx=\\frac\\pi2-\\frac12 .\\] So $I=\\frac{\\pi-1}{4}$. Option B comes from forgetting the $-sc$ term.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "trg", "sym"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "I2-13", "paper": "I2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>A function $f$ is differentiable on $[0,1]$, with $f(0)=0$, $f(1)=1$ and $f'(x)\\le1$ for every $x$ in $[0,1]$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(x)=x$ for every $x$ in $[0,1]$.<\/li><li>$f(2)\\le2$<\/li><li>$f'\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)=1$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1f(x)\\,dx=\\tfrac12$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, III and IV only", "I and IV only", "all four", "III and IV only", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The function $g(x)=f(x)-x$ has $g'\\le0$, so $g$ is non-increasing on $[0,1]$. But $g(0)=0=g(1)$, so $g$ is constant, equal to $0$: this is I. III and IV follow from I. II is not forced, because the condition says nothing about $f$ outside $[0,1]$: the function equal to $x$ on $[0,1]$ and to $x+(x-1)^2\\cdot5$ beyond it is differentiable and has $f(2)=7$. The squeeze, a slope of at most $1$ over a rise of exactly $1$, leaves no room at all.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "ineq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "I2-14", "paper": "I2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A continuous function $f$ on $[0,2]$ has $F(t)=\\displaystyle\\int_0^tf(x)\\,dx$. It is known that $F(t)\\ge0$ for every $t$ in $[0,2]$ and $F(2)=0$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(0)\\ge0$<\/li><li>$f(2)\\le0$<\/li><li>$f(c)=0$ for some $c$ in $[0,2]$.<\/li><li>$f(1)=0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["III only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "all four", "II and III only", "I, II and III only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: if $f(0)&lt;0$, then $f$ is negative just to the right of $0$, making $F(t)&lt;0$ for small $t$. II: if $f(2)>0$, then $f$ is positive just to the left of $2$, so $F(t)=-\\int_t^2f&lt;0$ for $t$ close to $2$. III: $\\int_0^2f=0$, so $f$ cannot keep one sign throughout, and by continuity it has a zero. IV fails: $F(t)=t^2(2-t)$ is non-negative on $[0,2]$ and vanishes at both ends, but $f(x)=F'(x)=4x-3x^2$ has $f(1)=1$. The zero guaranteed by III sits wherever $F$ peaks, which here is at $x=\\tfrac43$, not at the midpoint.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "diff"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "I2-15", "paper": "I2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Integers $x&lt;y&lt;z$ satisfy $x+y+z=xyz$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>There are infinitely many such triples.<\/li><li>If $x$, $y$ and $z$ are all positive, then $(x,y,z)=(1,2,3)$.<\/li><li>$xyz\\ge0$<\/li><li>If none of $x$, $y$, $z$ is zero, then $|x|$, $|y|$, $|z|$ are $1$, $2$, $3$ in some order.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II and IV only", "I, II and III only", "I, II and IV only", "I and III only", "all four"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: any triple $(-k,0,k)$ with $k\\ge1$ works, since both sides are $0$. II: with $1\\le x&lt;y&lt;z$, the sum is less than $3z$, so $xy&lt;3$, forcing $x=1$, $y=2$, and then $3+z=2z$ gives $z=3$. IV: if all are non-zero and of one sign, the triple is $(1,2,3)$ or, negating everything, $(-3,-2,-1)$. Mixed signs are impossible: if exactly one is negative, say $x&lt;0&lt;y&lt;z$, the right side is negative, so $|x|>y+z$ and the left side has size $|x|-y-z&lt;|x|$, while the right side has size $|x|\\,yz\\ge2|x|$. Two negatives reduce to this case by changing every sign. III fails: $(-3,-2,-1)$ has product $-6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "log"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "I2-16", "paper": "I2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^4+ax^3+bx^2$, where $a$ and $b$ are real. Which of the following conditions guarantee that $f$ has exactly one stationary point? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$a=0$ and $b>0$<\/li><li>$9a^2&lt;32b$<\/li><li>$b>0$<\/li><li>$a=0$ and $b=0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "II only", "III and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>$f'(x)=x\\left(4x^2+3ax+2b\\right)$, so $x=0$ is always stationary, and the question is what the quadratic adds. II: when $9a^2&lt;32b$ the quadratic has no real roots, so $x=0$ is the only stationary point. I is the special case $a=0$, $b>0$. IV: $f=x^4$, whose only stationary point is $0$. III fails: $a=5$, $b=1$ has $9a^2>32b$, and $f'$ has three roots. (The complete answer is $9a^2&lt;32b$, or $a=b=0$.)<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "ns", "poly"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "I2-17", "paper": "I2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A function $f$ is twice differentiable, with $f&rdquo;(x)>0$ for every real $x$ and $f(0)=0$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(2)>2f(1)$<\/li><li>$f(1)+f(-1)>0$<\/li><li>$f(x)\\ge f'(0)\\,x$ for every real $x$.<\/li><li>$f'(x)>0$ for every $x>0$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, II and III only", "I and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only", "III and IV only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$f&rdquo;>0$ means the graph bends upwards: it lies strictly below its chords and above its tangents. I: $(1,f(1))$ is below the chord from $(0,0)$ to $(2,f(2))$, so $f(1)&lt;\\frac{f(2)}{2}$. II: $(0,0)$ is below the chord from $(-1,f(-1))$ to $(1,f(1))$, so $0&lt;\\frac{f(1)+f(-1)}{2}$. III: the graph lies above its tangent at the origin, $y=f'(0)x$. IV fails: $f(x)=x^2-3x$ has $f&rdquo;=2>0$ and $f(0)=0$, but $f'(1)=-1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "ineq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "C2-20", "paper": "C2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>For each positive integer $n$, $S(n)$ is a statement. It is known that $S(10)$ is true, and that for every positive integer $n$, \\[ \\text{if } S(n) \\text{ is true then } S(n+3) \\text{ is true,}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{if } S(n+2) \\text{ is true then } S(n) \\text{ is true.} \\] Which of the following is the set of positive integers $n$ for which $S(n)$ must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\{10\\}$", "$\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}$", "$\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}\\cup\\{13,16,19,\\dots\\}$", "$\\{1,2,\\dots,10\\}$", "$\\{10,11,12,\\dots\\}$", "all positive integers"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>From $S(10)$, the second rule gives $S(8),S(6),S(4),S(2)$. Now combine the rules: if $S(n)$ holds then $S(n+3)$ holds, and then the second rule gives $S(n+1)$. So each true statement makes the next one true, and once $S(1)$ is reached everything follows. It is: $S(2)\\Rightarrow S(5)$, then $S(5)\\Rightarrow S(3)\\Rightarrow S(1)$. So $S(n)$ is true for every positive integer $n$. Seeing that $+3$ followed by $-2$ is a step of $+1$ is the idea; tracking individual implications by hand, you tend to stop at one of the partial sets in the other options.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "B2-10", "paper": "B2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>$A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ are four points in the plane, no three of which are collinear, with $AB = CD$ and $AD = BC$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$ABCD$ is a parallelogram.<\/li><li>The segments $AC$ and $BD$ bisect each other.<\/li><li>Triangles $ABC$ and $CDA$ are congruent.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Triangles $ABC$ and $CDA$ share the side $AC$ and have $AB = CD$, $BC = DA$, so they are congruent by SSS: III must hold. But congruence leaves two positions for $D$ relative to the fixed triangle $ABC$: the reflection of $B$ through the midpoint of $AC$, which makes $ABCD$ a parallelogram, and the reflection of $B$ in the perpendicular bisector of $AC$, which puts $D$ on the same side of $AC$ as $B$. In the second case $ABCD$ is a crossed quadrilateral (its &ldquo;sides&rdquo; $AB$ and $CD$ cross), $BD$ is parallel to $AC$, and neither I nor II holds. Only III must be true. The trap is that &ldquo;two pairs of equal opposite sides&rdquo; is a parallelogram criterion for convex quadrilaterals, and the question never said convex.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ", "log"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "G1-14", "paper": "G1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>For each real $k$, let $N(k)$ be the number of real solutions of \\[\\Bigl|\\,\\bigl|\\,|x|-3\\,\\bigr|-2\\,\\Bigr|-1\\Bigr|=k .\\] Find $N(0)+N\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)+N(1)+N(2)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$23$", "$25$", "$27$", "$29$", "$31$", "$35$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Work outwards with $t=|x|$, removing one modulus at a time. On $[0,1]$ the expression is $t$; on $[1,3]$ it is $|t-2|$; on $[3,5]$ it is $|t-4|$; and for $t\\ge5$ it is $|t-6|$. So for $t\\ge0$ the graph is a zigzag between $0$ and $1$, with zeros at $t=0,2,4,6$ and peaks at $t=1,3,5$, after which it rises without bound. $k=0$: $x=0,\\pm2,\\pm4,\\pm6$, so $N=7$. $k=\\tfrac12$: two values of $t$ on each of the three teeth plus one beyond $6$, seven positive values, so $N=14$. $k=1$: $t=1,3,5,7$, so $N=8$. $k=2$: $t=8$, so $N=2$. The total is $31$.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "ineq"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "G1-15", "paper": "G1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The circle $C_1$ has centre $(0,0)$ and radius $1$, and the circle $C_2$ has centre $(4,0)$ and radius $2$. There are exactly four lines that are tangent to both circles. Find the sum of the squares of their gradients.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{142}{105}$", "$\\dfrac{284}{105}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{18}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{64}{21}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The two external tangents pass through the point dividing the centres externally in the ratio $1:2$, namely $(-4,0)$; the two internal tangents pass through the point dividing them internally, namely $\\left(\\tfrac43,0\\right)$. (Tangents from a centre of similitude are tangent to both circles at once.) A line $y=m(x+4)$ is at distance $1$ from the origin when $\\frac{4|m|}{\\sqrt{1+m^2}}=1$, so $m^2=\\tfrac1{15}$. A line $y=m\\left(x-\\tfrac43\\right)$ is at distance $1$ when $\\frac{4|m|/3}{\\sqrt{1+m^2}}=1$, so $m^2=\\tfrac97$. Each value of $m^2$ occurs for two lines, so the sum is $2\\left(\\tfrac1{15}+\\tfrac97\\right)=\\tfrac{284}{105}$. Option A counts each line once.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "circ"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "G1-16", "paper": "G1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>In the quadrilateral $ABCD$, $AB=BC=1$ and $\\angle ABC=90^\\circ$. The point $D$ lies on the opposite side of the line $AC$ from $B$, with $\\angle ADC=45^\\circ$. Find the greatest possible length of $BD$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\sqrt2$", "$2$", "$\\sqrt5$", "$1+\\sqrt2$", "$1+\\sqrt3$", "$3$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Let $E$ be the fourth vertex of the square $ABCE$. Then $EA=EC=1$ and $\\angle AEC=90^\\circ$, so by the inscribed angle theorem every point $D$ on the circle with centre $E$ and radius $1$, on the far side of $AC$, sees $AC$ at $45^\\circ$; and the locus of such points is exactly that arc. So $D$ ranges over an arc of the circle centred at $E$, and $BD\\le BE+ED=\\sqrt2+1$, with equality when $D$ is on the ray $BE$ beyond $E$, which lies on the arc. The maximum is $1+\\sqrt2$. The whole question is recognising that circle.<\/p>", "tags": ["circ", "coord"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "G2-16", "paper": "G2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>For which of the following does there exist a function satisfying all the conditions stated? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>A polynomial $p$ with $p(0)=p(1)=0$ and $p'(x)>0$ for every $x$ in $(0,1)$.<\/li><li>A polynomial $p$ with $p(0)=0$, $p(1)=1$ and $p'(x)&lt;0$ for every $x$ in $(0,1)$.<\/li><li>A continuous function $f$ on $[0,1]$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f(1)=1$ that takes the value $2$ somewhere in $[0,1]$.<\/li><li>A differentiable function $f$ with $f(0)=0$, $f(1)=2$ and $|f'(x)|\\le1$ for every real $x$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["none of them", "I and III only", "III only", "III and IV only", "I, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: a function increasing throughout $(0,1)$ cannot return to its starting value, so no. II: a function decreasing throughout $(0,1)$ cannot go from $0$ up to $1$, so no. IV: a slope never exceeding $1$ allows a rise of at most $1$ over an interval of length $1$, not $2$, so no. III: yes. For example $f(x)=9x-8x^2$ has $f(0)=0$ and $f(1)=1$, and its maximum, at $x=\\tfrac9{16}$, is $\\tfrac{81}{32}>2$, so by continuity it takes the value $2$. Only III.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "log"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "G2-17", "paper": "G2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Call a positive integer <em>representable<\/em> if it can be written as $5a+7b$ with $a$ and $b$ non-negative integers. Which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>The largest positive integer that is not representable is $23$.<\/li><li>Exactly $12$ positive integers are not representable.<\/li><li>Every multiple of $3$ greater than $6$ is representable.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I only", "I, II and III", "II only", "I and III only", "II and III only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>List the representable numbers: $5,7,10,12,14,15,17,19,20,21,22,24,25,26,27,28,\\dots$. From $24$ onwards, $24,25,26,27,28$ are five consecutive representable numbers, so adding $5$s covers everything beyond. So I holds. The non-representable numbers are $1,2,3,4,6,8,9,11,13,16,18,23$: twelve, so II holds. III fails: $9$ and $18$ are multiples of $3$ greater than $6$ that are not representable.<\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "log"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "H1-17", "paper": "H1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Find the total area of the finite regions enclosed between the curves \\[y=\\left|x^2-4\\right|\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y=|x|+2 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{13}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{19}{3}$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{29}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{31}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{37}{3}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Both curves are symmetric in the $y$-axis, so work with $x\\ge0$ and double. There, $4-x^2=x+2$ at $x=1$ and $x^2-4=x+2$ at $x=3$. On $[0,1]$ the curve is above the line; on $[1,3]$ the line is above the curve, which dips to $0$ at $x=2$. The areas for $x\\ge0$ are $\\int_0^1(2-x-x^2)\\,dx=\\tfrac76$, $\\int_1^2(x^2+x-2)\\,dx=\\tfrac{11}{6}$ and $\\int_2^3(6+x-x^2)\\,dx=\\tfrac{13}{6}$, totalling $\\tfrac{31}{6}$. Doubling gives $\\tfrac{31}{3}$. The first region straddles the $y$-axis as one piece, which does not affect the total.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "ineq"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "H1-18", "paper": "H1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>The tangent to the curve $y=x^3$ at a point $P$ (not the origin) meets the curve again at $Q$. The tangent at $Q$ meets the curve again at $R$. Find the ratio of the area of the region enclosed between the curve and the segment $PQ$ to the area of the region enclosed between the curve and the segment $QR$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac18$", "$\\dfrac1{16}$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac{1}{32}$", "$\\dfrac1{27}$", "it depends on $P$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>If $P$ has $x$-coordinate $a$, then $x^3-(3a^2x-2a^3)=(x-a)^2(x+2a)$, so $Q$ is at $x=-2a$, and likewise $R$ is at $x=4a$. The region between the curve and the tangent at $x=t$ has area $\\int(x-t)^2(x+2t)\\,dx$ over an interval of length $3|t|$, which scales as $t^4$: it equals $\\tfrac{27}{4}t^4$. So the areas are $\\tfrac{27}{4}a^4$ and $\\tfrac{27}{4}(2a)^4$, in the ratio $\\tfrac1{16}$, whatever $a$ is. Scaling does all the work once the first area is seen to be a fixed multiple of $t^4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "diff"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "H2-18", "paper": "H2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true for every real number $x$? (Angles are in radians.) <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$|\\sin x|+|\\cos x|\\ge1$<\/li><li>$\\tan x+\\dfrac1{\\tan x}\\ge2$, whenever both terms are defined.<\/li><li>$|\\sin x-\\cos x|\\le\\sqrt2$<\/li><li>$\\cos(\\sin x)>\\sin(\\cos x)$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and III only", "I, II and III only", "I, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "III and IV only", "I and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: $(|\\sin x|+|\\cos x|)^2=1+2|\\sin x\\cos x|\\ge1$. III: $(\\sin x-\\cos x)^2=1-2\\sin x\\cos x\\le2$. II fails whenever $\\tan x&lt;0$. IV: cosine is even, so $\\cos(\\sin x)=\\cos|\\sin x|=\\sin\\!\\left(\\tfrac\\pi2-|\\sin x|\\right)$, and $\\tfrac\\pi2-|\\sin x|$ lies in $\\left[\\tfrac\\pi2-1,\\tfrac\\pi2\\right]$. Also $\\cos x$ lies in $[-1,1]$. Both are inside $\\left[-\\tfrac\\pi2,\\tfrac\\pi2\\right]$, where sine is increasing, so it suffices that $\\tfrac\\pi2-|\\sin x|>\\cos x$, that is $|\\sin x|+\\cos x&lt;\\tfrac\\pi2$. This is true because $|\\sin x|+\\cos x\\le\\sqrt2&lt;\\tfrac\\pi2$. So I, III and IV.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "H2-19", "paper": "H2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Positive real numbers $x$, $y$ and $z$ satisfy $xyz=1$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$x+y+z\\ge3$<\/li><li>$xy+yz+zx\\ge3$<\/li><li>$x^2+y^2+z^2\\ge x+y+z$<\/li><li>$\\dfrac1x+\\dfrac1y+\\dfrac1z\\ge x+y+z$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only", "I and IV only", "I, II and III only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: $\\frac{x+y+z}{3}\\ge\\sqrt[3]{xyz}=1$. II: apply the same to $xy$, $yz$, $zx$, whose product is $1$. III: $3(x^2+y^2+z^2)\\ge(x+y+z)^2$, and $(x+y+z)^2\\ge3(x+y+z)$ by I; divide by $3$. IV fails: $x=4$, $y=z=\\tfrac12$ gives $\\tfrac14+2+2=4.25&lt;5$. In fact IV says $xy+yz+zx\\ge x+y+z$, which is not symmetric enough to be forced.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "sym"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "I1-18", "paper": "I1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Let $n\\ge2$ be an integer, and let $N(n)$ be the number of solutions of \\[\\sin(nx)=\\sin x\\qquad\\text{with }0\\le x&lt;2\\pi .\\] Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["$N(n)=2n-2$ when $n$ leaves remainder $1$ on division by $4$, and $N(n)=2n$ otherwise", "$N(n)=2n$ for every $n$", "$N(n)=2n-2$ when $n$ is odd, and $N(n)=2n$ when $n$ is even", "$N(n)=2n-1$ for every $n$", "$N(n)=2n+2$ when $n$ is even, and $N(n)=2n$ when $n$ is odd", "$N(n)=2n-2$ when $n$ is even, and $N(n)=2n$ when $n$ is odd"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$\\sin nx=\\sin x$ exactly when $nx=x+2k\\pi$ or $nx=\\pi-x+2k\\pi$. The first family gives $x=\\frac{2k\\pi}{n-1}$, which is $n-1$ values in $[0,2\\pi)$; the second gives $x=\\frac{(2j+1)\\pi}{n+1}$, which is $n+1$ values. That makes $2n$ before removing overlaps. A common value needs $2k(n+1)=(2j+1)(n-1)$. If $n$ is even, the right side is odd and the left even, so there is no overlap. If $n=4m+3$, the left side is a multiple of $4$ and the right side $(2j+1)(4m+2)$ is only a multiple of $2$, so again none. If $n=4m+1$, the values $x=\\tfrac\\pi2$ and $x=\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ lie in both families, so $N=2n-2$. Checking $n=3$ gives $6$ and $n=5$ gives $8$, which rules out C.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "cnt"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "I2-18", "paper": "I2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>For real constants $p$ and $q$, consider the equation \\[x|x|+px+q=0 .\\] Which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>If $p\\ge0$, the equation has exactly one real solution.<\/li><li>If $p&lt;0$ and $q=0$, the equation has exactly three real solutions.<\/li><li>For some values of $p$ and $q$ the equation has exactly two real solutions.<\/li><li>For some values of $p$ and $q$ the equation has exactly four real solutions.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I, II and III only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "all four", "I only", "I, III and IV only"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$g(x)=x|x|+px$ is odd. I: $x|x|$ is increasing and so is $px$ when $p\\ge0$, so $g$ is increasing and meets each level once. II: $x(|x|+p)=0$ gives $x=0$ or $|x|=-p$, three solutions. III: for $p&lt;0$, $g$ has a local maximum $\\frac{p^2}{4}$ at $x=\\frac p2$ and a local minimum $-\\frac{p^2}{4}$ at $x=-\\frac p2$, so $q=\\mp\\frac{p^2}{4}$ gives a tangency and exactly two solutions. IV fails: each half-line $x\\ge0$ and $x&lt;0$ carries a quadratic, and the shape of $g$ (rising, falling, rising) allows at most three crossings of any horizontal line.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "ineq", "quad"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "I2-19", "paper": "I2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Here <em>increasing<\/em> means that $u&lt;v$ implies $f(u)&lt;f(v)$. Which of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>If $f$ is differentiable everywhere and $f'(x)>0$ for every $x\\neq0$, then $f$ is increasing.<\/li><li>If $f$ is continuous everywhere, differentiable except possibly at $0$, and $f'(x)>0$ for every $x\\neq0$, then $f$ is increasing.<\/li><li>If $f$ is differentiable except at $0$, and $f'(x)>0$ for every $x\\neq0$, then $f$ is increasing.<\/li><li>If $f$ is differentiable and increasing, then $f'(x)>0$ for every $x$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I only", "I, II and III only", "II only", "I, II and IV only", "all four"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>II implies I, since differentiable functions are continuous. For II, take $u&lt;v$. If both lie on the same side of $0$, the mean value theorem on $[u,v]$ gives $f(v)-f(u)=f'(c)(v-u)>0$. If $u&lt;0&lt;v$, apply it on $[u,0]$ and on $[0,v]$, which needs continuity at $0$, and add. III fails without continuity: $f(x)=x$ for $x&lt;0$ and $f(x)=x-1$ for $x\\ge0$ has $f'=1$ away from $0$, but $f(-0.1)>f(0)$. IV fails: $x^3$ is increasing, with $f'(0)=0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "prf", "log"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "B1-3", "paper": "B1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A polynomial $p$ of degree $4$ satisfies $p(k) = 2^k$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4$. Find $p(5)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$16$", "$30$", "$31$", "$32$", "$33$", "$64$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The binomial coefficient $\\binom{x}{j} = \\frac{x(x-1)\\cdots(x-j+1)}{j!}$ is a polynomial in $x$ of degree $j$, and for a non-negative integer $k$ we have $2^k = \\sum_{j=0}^{k}\\binom{k}{j}$, where the terms with $j > k$ vanish. So $p(x) = \\sum_{j=0}^{4}\\binom{x}{j}$ is a quartic with $p(k) = 2^k$ for $k = 0,\\dots,4$, and it is the only one, two quartics agreeing at five points being equal. Then \\[ p(5) = \\sum_{j=0}^{4}\\binom{5}{j} = 2^5 - \\binom55 = 31. \\] The same answer comes from a difference table: the fourth differences of $1,2,4,8,16$ are constant, and extending the table one step gives $31$. The trap is $32$, which assumes a polynomial can imitate $2^x$ one step further than its degree allows.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["poly"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "B2-17", "paper": "B2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A finite set $S$ of points in the plane has the property that every point of $S$ is the midpoint of the segment joining some two other points of $S$. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$S$ must be empty.", "$S$ can be non-empty, and any such $S$ has at least $3$ points.", "$S$ can be non-empty, and any such $S$ has at least $4$ points.", "The four vertices of a parallelogram form such a set $S$.", "There is such a set $S$ with exactly $5$ points."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Suppose $S$ is non-empty. Fix any point $O$ and, since $S$ is finite, choose $P \\in S$ furthest from $O$. If $P$ were the midpoint of $XY$ with $X, Y \\in S$ distinct, then $\\overrightarrow{OP} = \\tfrac12\\bigl(\\overrightarrow{OX} + \\overrightarrow{OY}\\bigr)$, and by the triangle inequality \\[ OP \\le \\tfrac12(OX + OY) \\le \\max(OX, OY), \\] with equality throughout only when $\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OY}$, i.e.\\ $X = Y$. Since $X \\ne Y$, one of $OX$, $OY$ is larger than $OP$, contradicting the choice of $P$. So no non-empty finite set has the property, and $S$ must be empty; the condition holds for the empty set vacuously. Options D and E fail for the same reason as everything else: in any finite set, the point furthest out is never a midpoint. Choosing an extreme element and showing it cannot satisfy the condition is the entire method.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ext"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "G1-17", "paper": "G1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Every tangent to the curve $y=x^3-6x^2+9x$ that passes through the origin is drawn. Find the total area of all the finite regions enclosed between the curve and these tangents, for $x\\ge0$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{27}{4}$", "$108$", "$\\dfrac{405}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{459}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{513}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{243}{2}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The tangent at $x=t$ passes through $O$ when $f(t)=tf'(t)$: $t^3-6t^2+9t=t(3t^2-12t+9)$, that is $2t^3-6t^2=0$, so $t=0$ or $t=3$. At $t=0$ the tangent is $y=9x$; at $t=3$, $f(3)=0$ and $f'(3)=0$, so the tangent is the $x$-axis. The curve minus $9x$ is $x^2(x-6)$, so the curve lies below $y=9x$ on $(0,6)$, enclosing $\\int_0^6(6x^2-x^3)\\,dx=432-324=108$. The curve $x(x-3)^2$ lies above the axis on $(0,3)$, touching it at $3$, enclosing $\\int_0^3x(x-3)^2\\,dx=\\tfrac{27}{4}$. The total is $\\tfrac{459}{4}$. The $x$-axis is easy to miss as a tangent through the origin.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "arint"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "G2-18", "paper": "G2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>A function $f$ from the real numbers to the real numbers satisfies $f\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)=x$ for every real $x$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f$ is one-to-one.<\/li><li>$f$ is increasing.<\/li><li>If $f$ is increasing, then $f(x)=x$ for every $x$.<\/li><li>There is a real number $a$ with $f(a)=a$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "III only", "I, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and III only", "II and III only"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I: if $f(a)=f(b)$ then $a=f(f(a))=f(f(b))=b$. II fails for $f(x)=-x$. III: if $f(x)>x$ for some $x$, applying the increasing $f$ gives $x=f(f(x))>f(x)$, a contradiction, and $f(x)&lt;x$ fails in the same way. IV fails for a discontinuous $f$: let $f(x)=x+1$ when the integer part of $x$ is even and $f(x)=x-1$ when it is odd. This swaps $[0,1)$ with $[1,2)$, $[2,3)$ with $[3,4)$, and so on, so $f(f(x))=x$ but $f(x)\\neq x$ everywhere. For a continuous $f$, IV would hold.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "prf"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "H1-19", "paper": "H1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>For which values of $k$ does the equation \\[\\left|x^2-4x\\right|=x+k\\] have exactly four real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0\\le k\\le\\tfrac94$", "$-4&lt;k&lt;0$", "$0&lt;k&lt;\\tfrac94$", "$0&lt;k&lt;4$", "$k>\\tfrac94$", "$-4&lt;k&lt;\\tfrac94$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Picture the line $y=x+k$, of gradient $1$, sliding upwards across the graph of $y=|x^2-4x|$, which is zero at $x=0$ and $x=4$ and has a hump on $[0,4]$ with peak $4$ at $x=2$. The outer arms $y=x^2-4x$ are steeper than $1$ far out, so the line always crosses the right arm once when $k>-4$ and the left arm once when $k>0$. The line meets the hump $y=4x-x^2$ where $x^2-3x+k=0$: twice when $0&lt;k&lt;\\tfrac94$ (both roots in $[0,4]$), once, tangentially, at $k=\\tfrac94$, and not at all beyond. So there are four solutions exactly for $0&lt;k&lt;\\tfrac94$. At $k=0$ the line passes through the corner $(0,0)$ and there are three, and at $k=\\tfrac94$ there are three.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "root"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "H2-20", "paper": "H2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>A quartic polynomial $f$ has positive leading coefficient and satisfies \\[f(0)=0,\\qquad f'(0)=0,\\qquad f(1)=-1,\\qquad f(2)=0 .\\] Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f$ has exactly one local minimum.<\/li><li>$f(x)&lt;0$ for every $x$ with $0&lt;x&lt;2$.<\/li><li>$f$ has at least two stationary points.<\/li><li>$f(-1)>0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and III only", "II and III only", "III only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "III and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>$x=0$ is a double root and $x=2$ a root, so $f(x)=x^2(x-2)(ax-b)$ with $a>0$, and $f(1)=-1$ gives $b=a-1$. So $f(x)=x^2(x-2)\\bigl(ax-(a-1)\\bigr)$, a one-parameter family. III: $x=0$ is stationary, and $f$ has a minimum somewhere in $(0,2)$ since $f(1)&lt;0=f(0)=f(2)$. The rest can fail. For $a=2$ the fourth root is $\\tfrac12$, so $f>0$ on $\\left(0,\\tfrac12\\right)$, breaking II, and $0$ is itself a local minimum, giving two minima and breaking I. For $a=\\tfrac12$, $f(-1)=0$, breaking IV. Only III is forced.<\/p>", "tags": ["poly", "diff", "root"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "I1-19", "paper": "I1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>As $a$ and $b$ range over all real numbers, find the minimum value of \\[\\int_0^1\\left(x^2-ax-b\\right)^2dx .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{12}$", "$\\dfrac1{45}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{80}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{180}$", "$0$", "$\\dfrac{1}{120}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Expanding, the integral is $J=\\tfrac15+\\tfrac{a^2}{3}+b^2-\\tfrac a2-\\tfrac{2b}{3}+ab$, a quadratic in two variables. For fixed $a$, the best $b$ makes $\\frac{\\partial J}{\\partial b}=2b-\\tfrac23+a=0$, so $b=\\tfrac13-\\tfrac a2$. Substituting leaves a quadratic in $a$ alone, minimised at $a=1$, $b=-\\tfrac16$. Then $J=\\tfrac15+\\tfrac13+\\tfrac1{36}-\\tfrac12+\\tfrac19-\\tfrac16=\\tfrac{36+60+5-90+20-30}{180}=\\tfrac1{180}$. The line $y=x-\\tfrac16$ is the best straight-line approximation to $x^2$ on $[0,1]$ in this sense, and it is not the tangent or the chord, which give larger values.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "quad"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "B1-4", "paper": "B1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Let $a$, $b$ and $c$ be the three real roots of $x^3 - 3x + 1 = 0$. Find the value of \\[ (a^2-2)(b^2-2)(c^2-2). \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-3$", "$-1$", "$0$", "$1$", "$3$", "$9$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Write $f(x) = x^3 - 3x + 1 = (x-a)(x-b)(x-c)$. Then $a^2 - 2 = (a-\\sqrt2)(a+\\sqrt2)$, and \\[ \\prod (a-\\sqrt2) = -f(\\sqrt2), \\qquad \\prod (a+\\sqrt2) = -f(-\\sqrt2), \\] so the required product is $f(\\sqrt2)f(-\\sqrt2) = (1-\\sqrt2)(1+\\sqrt2) = -1$. No root is ever found.<\/p><p>The trigonometric route explains the answer. Putting $x = 2\\cos\\theta$ turns $x^3 - 3x$ into $2\\cos 3\\theta$, so the roots are $2\\cos\\frac{2\\pi}{9}$, $2\\cos\\frac{4\\pi}{9}$, $2\\cos\\frac{8\\pi}{9}$, and the map $x \\mapsto x^2 - 2$ is $2\\cos\\theta \\mapsto 2\\cos 2\\theta$, which permutes the three roots. The product is therefore $abc = -1$ by Vieta.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["vie", "poly"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "C2-18", "paper": "C2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is continuous and increasing on $[0,1]$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx\\le f(1)$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 x f(x)\\,dx\\ge \\frac12\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{1/2} f(x)\\,dx\\le\\int_{1/2}^{1} f(x)\\,dx$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: $f(x)\\le f(1)$ throughout, so the integral is at most $f(1)$. III: substituting $x\\mapsto x+\\frac12$ gives $\\int_{1/2}^1f=\\int_0^{1/2}f(x+\\frac12)\\,dx\\ge\\int_0^{1/2}f(x)\\,dx$. II is the one needing an idea. Since $\\int_0^1(x-\\frac12)\\,dx=0$, \\[ \\int_0^1\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)f(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^1\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)\\left(f(x)-f\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)\\right)dx\\ge0, \\] because both brackets are negative for $x&lt;\\frac12$ and non-negative for $x>\\frac12$. That rearranges to II. All three.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "ineq"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "G1-18", "paper": "G1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>For each real $k$ with $-16&lt;k&lt;16$, the equation $x^3-12x+k=0$ has three distinct real roots $\\alpha&lt;\\beta&lt;\\gamma$. As $k$ varies over this interval, which of the following is the set of all possible values of $\\gamma-\\alpha$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6&lt;\\gamma-\\alpha&lt;8$", "$0&lt;\\gamma-\\alpha\\le4\\sqrt3$", "$6&lt;\\gamma-\\alpha\\le4\\sqrt3$", "$4\\sqrt3\\le\\gamma-\\alpha&lt;8$", "$6\\le\\gamma-\\alpha\\le4\\sqrt3$", "$\\gamma-\\alpha=4\\sqrt3$ for every $k$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Since the roots sum to $0$ and $\\alpha\\beta+\\beta\\gamma+\\gamma\\alpha=-12$, we have $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2=24$. With $\\beta=-(\\alpha+\\gamma)$ and $d=\\gamma-\\alpha$, $s=\\alpha+\\gamma=-\\beta$: $\\alpha^2+\\gamma^2=\\tfrac{s^2+d^2}{2}$, so $\\tfrac{s^2+d^2}{2}+s^2=24$, giving $d^2=48-3\\beta^2$. As $k$ runs over $(-16,16)$, the middle root $\\beta$ runs over $(-2,2)$, since it lies between the stationary points $x=\\pm2$. So $\\beta^2\\in[0,4)$ and $d^2\\in(36,48]$: $6&lt;\\gamma-\\alpha\\le4\\sqrt3$. The maximum occurs at $k=0$, and the value $6$ is approached but not reached, because at $k=\\pm16$ two roots merge.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "poly"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "G1-19", "paper": "G1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Real numbers $x$, $y$ and $z$ satisfy $x+y+z=3$ and $x^2+y^2+z^2=5$. Find the greatest possible value of $xyz$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{9}$", "$\\dfrac49$", "$\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3}{9}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac23$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>From the conditions, $xy+yz+zx=\\frac{9-5}{2}=2$. So $x$, $y$, $z$ are the roots of $t^3-3t^2+2t-c=0$ where $c=xyz$, and we need the largest $c$ for which this cubic has three real roots. That is the largest horizontal line $y=c$ meeting $g(t)=t^3-3t^2+2t=t(t-1)(t-2)$ three times, so $c$ is the local maximum of $g$. $g'(t)=3t^2-6t+2=0$ at $t=1\\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$, and at $t=1-\\frac1{\\sqrt3}$, $g=\\left(1-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}\\right)\\left(-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}\\right)\\left(-1-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}\\right)=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\cdot\\tfrac23=\\tfrac{2\\sqrt3}{9}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "root"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "G2-19", "paper": "G2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>For which of the following combinations does there exist a function $f$, defined and twice differentiable for all real $x$, satisfying all three conditions for every real $x$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(x)>0$, $f'(x)>0$, $f&rdquo;(x)>0$<\/li><li>$f(x)>0$, $f'(x)&lt;0$, $f&rdquo;(x)>0$<\/li><li>$f(x)>0$, $f'(x)&lt;0$, $f&rdquo;(x)&lt;0$<\/li><li>$f(x)>0$, $f'(x)>0$, $f&rdquo;(x)&lt;0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I, II, III and IV", "I, II and IV only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I and IV only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: $f(x)=e^x$. II: $f(x)=e^{-x}$. III is impossible: $f&rdquo;&lt;0$ makes $f'$ decreasing, so for $x>0$, $f'(x)&lt;f'(0)&lt;0$ and $f(x)&lt;f(0)+f'(0)x$, which becomes negative for large $x$. IV is impossible for the mirror-image reason: $f'$ decreasing means $f'(x)>f'(0)>0$ for $x&lt;0$, so $f(x)&lt;f(0)+f'(0)x$, which becomes negative for large negative $x$. A graph that bends downwards must eventually fall below any line it is heading along, in one direction or the other.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "log"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "H1-20", "paper": "H1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>For each positive integer $n$, let $m(n)$ be the minimum value, over all real $x$, of \\[f_n(x)=|x-1|+|x-2|+|x-3|+\\cdots+|x-n| .\\] For how many integers $n$ with $1\\le n\\le50$ is $m(n)$ a perfect square (including $0$)?<\/p>", "opts": ["$7$", "$24$", "$25$", "$26$", "$36$", "$50$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The minimum of a sum of distances is attained at a median of $1,2,\\dots,n$. Pairing the outermost terms, $|x-1|+|x-n|\\ge n-1$, $|x-2|+|x-(n-1)|\\ge n-3$, and so on, with equality at the median. So $m(n)=(n-1)+(n-3)+\\cdots$, which is $\\frac{n^2}{4}$ for even $n$ and $\\frac{n^2-1}{4}$ for odd $n$. For even $n=2m$, $m(n)=m^2$ is always a square: $25$ values. For odd $n=2m+1$, $m(n)=m(m+1)$ lies strictly between $m^2$ and $(m+1)^2$ when $m\\ge1$, so it is a square only when $m=0$, that is $n=1$ with $m(1)=0$. The total is $26$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ineq", "mod"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "I1-20", "paper": "I1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>A point is chosen uniformly at random inside a square of side $1$. What is the probability that it is closer to the nearest side of the square than to the centre of the square?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{8-4\\sqrt2}{3}$", "$\\dfrac34$", "$\\dfrac{4\\sqrt2-2}{3}$", "$\\dfrac23$", "$\\sqrt2-\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>By symmetry, work in the triangle below the centre with vertices $(0,0)$, $(1,0)$ and $\\left(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12\\right)$, where the nearest side is $y=0$. The condition $y&lt;\\sqrt{\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)^2+\\left(y-\\tfrac12\\right)^2}$ simplifies to $y&lt;\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)^2+\\tfrac14$, a parabola. It lies above the edge $y=x$ of the triangle until $x=c=1-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}$. So the favourable area in the left half of the triangle is $\\int_0^cx\\,dx+\\int_c^{1/2}\\left(\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)^2+\\tfrac14\\right)dx$. Evaluating and multiplying by $8$, for two halves times four triangles, gives $\\tfrac{8-4\\sqrt2}{3}\\approx0.781$. Rough reasoning often suggests $\\tfrac34$, which is close but not correct.<\/p>", "tags": ["cnt", "arint", "coord"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "I2-20", "paper": "I2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>For real constants $a$ and $b$, consider the equation \\[\\sqrt{x+a}=x+b .\\] Which condition on $a$ and $b$ is necessary and sufficient for the equation to have exactly two distinct real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a>b-\\tfrac14$", "$b-\\tfrac14&lt;a&lt;b$", "$a\\le b$", "$b-\\tfrac14\\le a\\le b$", "$b-\\tfrac14&lt;a\\le b$", "$a&lt;b+\\tfrac14$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>A solution needs $x+b\\ge0$ and then, squaring, $x+a=(x+b)^2$, that is $x^2+(2b-1)x+b^2-a=0$. (The condition $x+a\\ge0$ follows automatically from the squared equation.) Two distinct solutions need two distinct roots of this quadratic, both at least $-b$. Distinct roots: $(2b-1)^2-4(b^2-a)=1-4b+4a>0$, that is $a>b-\\tfrac14$. The vertex $x=\\tfrac{1-2b}{2}$ always exceeds $-b$, so both roots are at least $-b$ exactly when the quadratic is non-negative at $x=-b$: $b^2-(2b-1)b+b^2-a=b-a\\ge0$. So $b-\\tfrac14&lt;a\\le b$. At $a=b$ the root $x=-b$ is included (the right side is $0$), which is why that end is closed.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "quad", "root"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "B1-9", "paper": "B1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A regular heptagon has side length $1$. Its diagonals take exactly two distinct lengths, $p &lt; q$. Find the value of $\\dfrac1p + \\dfrac1q$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt7}{2}$", "$\\dfrac32$", "$2$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Label the heptagon $ABCDEFG$, with side $1$, short diagonal $p$ (two steps apart, e.g.\\ $AC$) and long diagonal $q$ (three steps apart, e.g.\\ $AD$). Every regular polygon is cyclic, so Ptolemy applies to any four vertices. On the quadrilateral $ABCE$ the sides are $AB = BC = 1$, $CE = p$, $EA = q$ and the diagonals are $AC = p$, $BE = q$, so Ptolemy gives \\[ AC\\cdot BE = AB\\cdot CE + BC\\cdot EA, \\qquad pq = p + q, \\] so $\\frac1p + \\frac1q = 1$ on dividing by $pq$. The lengths themselves are $p = 2\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{7}$ and $q = 1 + p - \\frac1p$, neither of which is needed. Ptolemy on $ABCD$ gives the companion identity $p^2 = 1 + q$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "G2-20", "paper": "G2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^2-2$, and write $f^{(2)}=f\\circ f$ and $f^{(3)}=f\\circ f\\circ f$. Which of the following are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f^{(2)}(x)=x$ has exactly $4$ real solutions.<\/li><li>Every solution of $f(x)=x$ is a solution of $f^{(2)}(x)=x$.<\/li><li>$f^{(3)}(x)=x$ has exactly $8$ real solutions.<\/li><li>$f^{(2)}(x)=x$ has a solution with $|x|>2$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only", "I only", "I and III only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>II is immediate. If $|x|>2$ then $|f(x)|=x^2-2>|x|$, so the iterates grow in size and never return: every solution of any $f^{(n)}(x)=x$ lies in $[-2,2]$, and IV fails. On $[-2,2]$, $f$ runs down from $2$ to $-2$ and back up to $2$, covering $[-2,2]$ twice. So $f^{(n)}$ covers $[-2,2]$ exactly $2^n$ times in $2^n$ monotonic sweeps between $-2$ and $2$, and the line $y=x$, which runs from corner to corner of the square, crosses each sweep exactly once. That gives $4$ solutions for $f^{(2)}$ and $8$ for $f^{(3)}$, and every solution is real. I and III hold.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "poly"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "C1-18", "paper": "C1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $a$ for which \\[ a\\sin x+\\cos 2x\\le 1+a\\qquad\\text{for all real } x . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$0\\le a\\le 4$", "$0\\le a\\le 8$", "$-1\\le a\\le 8$", "$a\\ge -1$", "$a\\ge 0$", "all real $a$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>With $s=\\sin x$ and $\\cos2x=1-2s^2$, the condition is $g(s)=2s^2-as+a\\ge0$ for all $s\\in[-1,1]$. The minimum of $g$ on $[-1,1]$ depends on where the vertex $s=\\frac a4$ lies. If $-4\\le a\\le4$, the minimum is $g(\\frac a4)=a-\\frac{a^2}{8}$, which is non-negative for $0\\le a\\le 8$, so here $0\\le a\\le4$. If $a>4$, the vertex is to the right of the interval and the minimum is $g(1)=2>0$, so every $a>4$ works. If $a&lt;-4$, the minimum is $g(-1)=2+2a&lt;0$. Altogether $a\\ge0$. Option B is the discriminant condition applied without restricting $s$ to $[-1,1]$: it wrongly throws away every $a>8$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 8.7}, {"id": "B2-19", "paper": "B2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>The positive integer $n$ is such that $3n + 1$ and $4n + 1$ are both perfect squares. What is the largest integer that must divide $n$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$8$", "$14$", "$28$", "$56$", "$112$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Write $3n + 1 = a^2$ and $4n + 1 = b^2$. Since $4n + 1$ is odd, $b$ is odd, so $b^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 8$ and $4n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 8$: $n$ is even. Then $3n + 1$ is odd, so $a$ is odd and $a^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 8$, giving $3n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 8$, so $8 \\mid n$. Now modulo $7$: squares are $0, 1, 2, 4$, and $a^2 + b^2 = 7n + 2 \\equiv 2$. The only way two squares sum to $2$ modulo $7$ is $1 + 1$, so $a^2 \\equiv 1$, hence $3n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 7$ and $7 \\mid n$. Therefore $56 \\mid n$. And $n = 56$ itself works: $169 = 13^2$ and $225 = 15^2$, so no larger divisor is forced. The answer is $56$. Option D is what one gets from the mod $7$ argument together with only a partial mod $8$ argument.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 8.8}, {"id": "G1-20", "paper": "G1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^2+c$. Find the complete set of values of $c$ for which the equation $f\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)=x$ has exactly two distinct real solutions.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-\\tfrac34&lt;c&lt;\\tfrac14$", "$-\\tfrac34\\le c&lt;\\tfrac14$", "$-\\tfrac34\\le c\\le\\tfrac14$", "$c&lt;\\tfrac14$", "$-1&lt;c&lt;\\tfrac14$", "$c\\le-\\tfrac34$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Any solution of $f(x)=x$ also solves $f(f(x))=x$, so $x^2-x+c$ divides $f(f(x))-x$, and dividing out gives \\[f(f(x))-x=\\left(x^2-x+c\\right)\\left(x^2+x+c+1\\right).\\] The first factor has two real roots when $c&lt;\\tfrac14$ (one when $c=\\tfrac14$); the second has discriminant $-3-4c$, so real roots only when $c\\le-\\tfrac34$. For $-\\tfrac34&lt;c&lt;\\tfrac14$ there are exactly two solutions. At $c=-\\tfrac34$ the second factor contributes the double root $-\\tfrac12$, but $-\\tfrac12$ is already a root of the first factor ($\\tfrac14+\\tfrac12-\\tfrac34=0$), so there are still only two distinct solutions. At $c=\\tfrac14$ there is only one, and for $c&lt;-\\tfrac34$ there are four. So $-\\tfrac34\\le c&lt;\\tfrac14$: the included endpoint is the point of the question.<\/p>", "tags": ["quad", "root"], "rating": 8.8}, {"id": "B2-18", "paper": "B2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Let $f$ be a continuous function on $[0,1]$ satisfying \\[ \\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx = 0 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\int_0^1 x f(x)\\,dx = 0 . \\] Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(c) = 0$ for at least one $c$ in $(0,1)$.<\/li><li>$f(c) = 0$ for at least two distinct values of $c$ in $(0,1)$.<\/li><li>$f(x) = 0$ for every $x$ in $[0,1]$.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: if $f$ were never zero on $(0,1)$ it would keep one sign there by continuity, and its integral could not be $0$. So $f$ changes sign, and has a zero. II: suppose $f$ changes sign at only one point $c$, so $f$ has one sign on $(0,c)$ and the opposite sign on $(c,1)$. Then $(x - c)f(x)$ has the same sign on both intervals and is not identically zero, so $\\int_0^1 (x-c)f(x)\\,dx \\ne 0$. But that integral equals $\\int_0^1 xf - c\\int_0^1 f = 0 - 0$, a contradiction. So $f$ changes sign at least twice, giving at least two zeros; and if $f \\equiv 0$ it has infinitely many. III fails: $f(x) = x^2 - x + \\tfrac16$ has $\\int_0^1 f = \\tfrac13 - \\tfrac12 + \\tfrac16 = 0$ and $\\int_0^1 xf = \\tfrac14 - \\tfrac13 + \\tfrac1{12} = 0$ without vanishing. The answer is I and II. The multiplier $(x - c)$ is the idea: it is a function of one sign built to be orthogonal to nothing but $f$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint"], "rating": 9.0}, {"id": "J1-1", "paper": "J1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>The cubic equation $x^3+ax^2+bx+c=0$ has three real roots $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$. The three numbers $\\alpha+\\beta$, $\\beta+\\gamma$ and $\\gamma+\\alpha$ are the roots of the equation \\[x^3-2x^2-5x+6=0 .\\]<\/p><p>Find the value of $1+a+b+c$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-12$", "$-7$", "$-6$", "$0$", "$4$", "$6$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Factorise the second cubic first: $x=1$ is a root, and $x^3-2x^2-5x+6=(x-1)(x-3)(x+2)$. So the three pairwise sums are $1$, $3$ and $-2$ in some order. Adding them gives $2(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)=2$, so $\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma=1$, and each root is $1$ minus the sum of the other two: the roots are $0$, $-2$ and $3$. Hence $x^3+ax^2+bx+c=x(x+2)(x-3)$, and $1+a+b+c$ is its value at $x=1$, namely $1\\cdot3\\cdot(-2)=-6$. Option D is what you get by evaluating the <em>second<\/em> cubic at $1$, or by taking $\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma$ to be the sum of the new roots rather than half of it; option B forgets the leading $1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["poly", "sym"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "J1-2", "paper": "J1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>A chord $AB$ of a circle with centre $O$ and radius $r$ divides the circle into two segments. The smaller of these, the <em>minor segment<\/em>, is bounded by the chord $AB$ and the shorter arc $AB$. Its perimeter, the length of that arc plus the length of the chord, is exactly $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}+1\\right)r$. Find the ratio \\[\\frac{\\text{area of the minor segment}}{\\text{area of triangle } OAB} .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi-3}{3}$", "$1-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2\\pi}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\sqrt3\\,\\pi-9}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{2\\sqrt3\\,\\pi-9}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3\\,\\pi-9}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi-3\\sqrt3}{6}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Let $\\theta$ be the angle $AOB$ in radians, with $0&lt;\\theta&lt;\\pi$. The arc has length $r\\theta$ and the chord has length $2r\\sin\\frac\\theta2$, so we need $\\theta+2\\sin\\frac\\theta2=\\frac\\pi3+1$. Both terms on the left increase with $\\theta$ on this range, so there is only one solution, and $\\theta=\\frac\\pi3$ visibly works since $\\sin\\frac\\pi6=\\frac12$. The triangle has area $\\frac12r^2\\sin\\theta=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}r^2$ and the sector has area $\\frac12r^2\\theta=\\frac\\pi6r^2$, so the segment has area $\\left(\\frac\\pi6-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\right)r^2$. The ratio is $\\frac{\\pi/6-\\sqrt3/4}{\\sqrt3/4}=\\frac{2\\pi}{3\\sqrt3}-1=\\frac{2\\sqrt3\\,\\pi-9}{9}\\approx0.21$. Guessing $\\theta$ from the numbers is fine, but the monotonicity is what turns the guess into the answer. Option B compares the segment with the sector instead of the triangle.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "circ"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "J1-3", "paper": "J1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A point $P$ moves in the plane so that its distance from the point $(0,2)$ is always exactly $1$ greater than its distance from the $x$-axis. The path traced by $P$ and the line $y=2$ together enclose a finite region. Find its area.<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$6$", "$8$", "$9$", "$12$", "$3$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The distance from the $x$-axis is $|y|$, so $x^2+(y-2)^2=(|y|+1)^2$. If $y\\ge0$ this gives $x^2-4y+4=2y+1$, that is $y=\\frac{x^2+3}{6}$, which is indeed non-negative. If $y&lt;0$ it gives $x^2=2y-3$, which would need $y\\ge\\frac32$, a contradiction; so there is no second branch, and the path is the single parabola $y=\\frac{x^2+3}6$. It meets $y=2$ at $x=\\pm3$, and the area is \\[\\int_{-3}^{3}\\left(2-\\frac{x^2+3}{6}\\right)dx=\\frac16\\int_{-3}^3(9-x^2)\\,dx=\\frac16\\cdot36=6 .\\] The modulus looks as though it should produce two pieces of curve; checking that the lower piece is empty is the step to take care over.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "arint"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "J1-4", "paper": "J1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Amy and Ben play a game with a fair six-sided die. They take turns, starting with Amy, and on each turn the player rolls the die once. Amy wins as soon as she rolls a $6$. Ben wins as soon as he rolls a $5$ or a $6$. There is one extra rule: a player who rolls a $1$ immediately takes another turn, instead of passing the die to the other player. Find the probability that Amy wins.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac38$", "$\\dfrac25$", "$\\dfrac{5}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{13}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{13}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Let $a$ be the probability that Amy eventually wins when it is Amy's turn, and $b$ the same probability when it is Ben's turn. On her turn Amy wins with probability $\\frac16$, rolls again with probability $\\frac16$, and hands over with probability $\\frac46$, so $a=\\frac16+\\frac16a+\\frac23b$. On Ben's turn Amy loses with probability $\\frac26$, Ben rolls again with probability $\\frac16$, and the die returns to Amy with probability $\\frac36$, so $b=\\frac16b+\\frac12a$, giving $b=\\frac35a$. Then $\\frac56a=\\frac16+\\frac25a$, so $\\frac{13}{30}a=\\frac16$ and $a=\\frac5{13}$. Without the extra rule the same method gives $\\frac38$, option B; option A is the naive ratio of the single-roll chances $\\frac16:\\frac26$.<\/p>", "tags": ["cnt", "ser"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "J1-5", "paper": "J1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $x$ for which \\[\\log_{x^2}(x+2)&lt;\\tfrac12 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-2&lt;x&lt;1$, with $x\\neq-1$ and $x\\neq0$", "$0&lt;x&lt;1$", "$-1&lt;x&lt;1$, with $x\\neq0$", "$-2&lt;x&lt;1$, with $x\\neq0$", "$-2&lt;x&lt;-1$", "$x&lt;1$, with $x\\neq-1$ and $x\\neq0$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The logarithm needs $x+2>0$ and a base $x^2$ that is positive and not $1$: so $x>-2$, $x\\neq0$ and $x\\neq\\pm1$. The tempting step $\\log_{x^2}y=\\frac12\\log_xy$ is only available when $x>0$, and using it throws away every negative solution; that is option B. Work with the base $x^2$ instead. Since $(x^2)^{1/2}=|x|$, the inequality says $x+2&lt;|x|$ when the base exceeds $1$, and $x+2>|x|$ when the base is less than $1$. If $|x|>1$: for $x>1$ it reads $x+2&lt;x$, impossible, while for $-2&lt;x&lt;-1$ it reads $x+2&lt;-x$, which holds throughout. If $0&lt;|x|&lt;1$: $x+2>|x|$ holds for every such $x$. So the solution set is $-2&lt;x&lt;-1$ together with $-1&lt;x&lt;0$ and $0&lt;x&lt;1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["exp", "ineq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "J1-6", "paper": "J1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>The curve $y=x^4-4ax^3+6x^2$, where $a$ is a constant, has a stationary point that is neither a local maximum nor a local minimum. Find the $y$-coordinate of this stationary point.<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$9$", "$18$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>$\\frac{dy}{dx}=4x^3-12ax^2+12x=4x\\left(x^2-3ax+3\\right)$. Near $x=0$ the bracket is close to $3>0$, so the derivative changes from negative to positive and the origin is always a local minimum. A stationary point that is not a turning point needs $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ to vanish without changing sign, which here means the bracket has a repeated root: $9a^2=12$, so $a=\\pm\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}$, and the repeated root is $x=\\frac{3a}{2}=\\pm\\sqrt3$. For $a=\\frac2{\\sqrt3}$, $y=9-4\\cdot\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\cdot3\\sqrt3+18=9-24+18=3$. For $a=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}$ the curve is the reflection of this one in the $y$-axis, so the point is $\\left(-\\sqrt3,3\\right)$. Either way the answer is $3$. Option A is the origin, which is stationary for every $a$ but is always a minimum.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "poly"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "J1-7", "paper": "J1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Find the area of the region of the plane consisting of all the points $(x,y)$ that satisfy \\[\\left|\\,2y-x^2-1\\,\\right|+|x|\\le 3 .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac92$", "$6$", "$9$", "$12$", "$18$", "$\\dfrac{27}{2}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Fix $x$ and look at the vertical cross-section. The condition is $|2y-x^2-1|\\le3-|x|$, which needs $|x|\\le3$ and then says that $y$ lies within $\\frac{3-|x|}{2}$ of $\\frac{x^2+1}{2}$: an interval of length $3-|x|$. The parabola only decides <em>where<\/em> each cross-section sits, not how long it is, so the area is \\[\\int_{-3}^{3}\\left(3-|x|\\right)dx=9 .\\] Sliding vertical strips up or down never changes the area they cover. Option E forgets that the interval for $y$ is half as long as the interval for $2y$.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "ineq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "J1-8", "paper": "J1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>How many non-congruent triangles $ABC$ are there in which $AB=12$, angle $ACB=45^\\circ$, and the length $BC$ is a whole number?<\/p>", "opts": ["$12$", "$16$", "$17$", "$24$", "$28$", "$20$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Write $a=BC$ and $b=CA$. The cosine rule at $C$ gives $144=a^2+b^2-\\sqrt2\\,ab$, a quadratic in $b$: $b^2-\\sqrt2\\,ab+(a^2-144)=0$, with discriminant $2a^2-4(a^2-144)=576-2a^2$. Real triangles need $a\\le12\\sqrt2\\approx16.97$, so $a\\le16$. The two roots have product $a^2-144$ and positive sum. If $a&lt;12$ the product is negative, so exactly one root is positive: one triangle. If $a=12$ the roots are $0$ and $12\\sqrt2$, so again one triangle. If $12&lt;a\\le16$ the product and the discriminant are both positive: two different triangles. The count is $12+2\\times4=20$. This is the ambiguous case of the sine rule, counted: a second triangle exists exactly when $AB&lt;BC&lt;\\frac{AB}{\\sin45^\\circ}$. Option B counts one triangle for each possible $a$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "cnt"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "J1-9", "paper": "J1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A square has side length $2$. A circle is drawn with the same centre as the square. Each side of the square crosses the circle twice, and the part of each side that lies inside the circle subtends an angle of $60^\\circ$ at the centre. Find the area of the region that lies inside both the square and the circle.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{9}+\\sqrt3$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{9}+\\dfrac{4\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{8\\pi}{9}+\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{4\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{9}+\\dfrac{4\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{8\\pi}{9}+\\dfrac{4\\sqrt3}{3}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>A chord subtending $60^\\circ$ at the centre forms an equilateral triangle with the centre, and its distance from the centre is $R\\cos30^\\circ$. That distance is $1$, half the side of the square, so $R=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}$. The corners are at distance $\\sqrt2>R$ from the centre, so the common region is bounded alternately by the four chords and by four arcs. Split it from the centre into four triangles and four sectors. Each triangle has two sides $R$ and apex angle $60^\\circ$, so area $\\frac12R^2\\sin60^\\circ=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{3}$. The four sectors share the remaining $360^\\circ-4\\times60^\\circ=120^\\circ$, so together they have area $\\frac12R^2\\cdot\\frac{2\\pi}{3}=\\frac{4\\pi}{9}$. The total is $\\frac{4\\pi}{9}+\\frac{4\\sqrt3}{3}\\approx3.71$, a little less than the area $4$ of the square. Option F exceeds $4$ and cannot be right.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "circ"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "J1-10", "paper": "J1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>Find the total length of the set of values of $x$ with $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$ for which exactly two of the three numbers \\[\\cos x,\\qquad \\sin 2x,\\qquad \\cos 3x\\]<\/p><p>are positive.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$", "$\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Every sign change happens at a multiple of $\\frac\\pi6$: $\\cos x$ changes sign at $\\frac\\pi2$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}2$, $\\sin2x$ at $\\frac\\pi2$, $\\pi$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, and $\\cos3x$ at $\\frac\\pi6,\\frac\\pi2,\\frac{5\\pi}6,\\frac{7\\pi}6,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ and $\\frac{11\\pi}6$. On the eight intervals between consecutive points the number of positive values is, in order, $3,2,1,0,1,2,1,2$. Exactly two are positive on $\\left(\\frac\\pi6,\\frac\\pi2\\right)$, $\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}6,\\frac{3\\pi}2\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}6,2\\pi\\right)$, of lengths $\\frac\\pi3$, $\\frac\\pi3$ and $\\frac\\pi6$. The total is $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$. Option D counts where <em>at least<\/em> two are positive, which adds the opening interval $\\left(0,\\frac\\pi6\\right)$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "log"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "J1-11", "paper": "J1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A nature reserve plants $100$ saplings in its first year, and in each later year it plants $5\\%$ more saplings than it planted the year before. At the end of every year, after that year's planting, one tenth of all the saplings then in the reserve die. Let $P_n$ be the number of saplings planted in year $n$, and let $T_n$ be the number of living saplings at the end of year $n$, after that year's deaths. Treat all these quantities as exact real numbers, ignoring rounding. As $n$ becomes large, the ratio $T_n/P_n$ approaches a limit. Find this limit.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{63}{10}$", "$6$", "$7$", "$\\dfrac{15}{2}$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{21}{2}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Year $k$'s planting, $P_k=100(1.05)^{k-1}$, loses a tenth at the end of each year from year $k$ to year $n$, so it contributes $(0.9)^{n-k+1}P_k$ to $T_n$. Hence, writing $j=n-k$ and using $\\frac{P_k}{P_n}=(1.05)^{-j}$, \\[\\frac{T_n}{P_n}=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}(0.9)^{n-k+1}\\frac{P_k}{P_n}=0.9\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\frac{0.9}{1.05}\\right)^{j}.\\] This is a geometric series with ratio $\\frac{0.9}{1.05}=\\frac67$, so the limit is $0.9\\cdot\\frac{1}{1-6/7}=6.3$. Option C forgets that each year's planting also suffers that year's deaths; option E ignores the growth in planting.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "J1-12", "paper": "J1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>The polynomial $p$ has degree $3$, and \\[\\int_0^1p(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^2p(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^3p(x)\\,dx=0,\\qquad p(0)=6 .\\]<\/p><p>Find $p(4)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-26$", "$-24$", "$24$", "$-50$", "$50$", "$26$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Work with $P(x)=\\int_0^xp(t)\\,dt$ instead of $p$. It is a polynomial of degree $4$ with $P(0)=0$, and the three conditions say $P(1)=P(2)=P(3)=0$. So $P(x)=k\\,x(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)$ for some constant $k$, and $p=P'$. By the product rule, $P'(x)$ is $k$ times the sum of the four products of three of the factors. At $x=0$ only $(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)$ survives, giving $p(0)=-6k=6$, so $k=-1$. At $x=4$ the four products are $3\\cdot2\\cdot1$, $4\\cdot2\\cdot1$, $4\\cdot3\\cdot1$ and $4\\cdot3\\cdot2$, which sum to $50$, so $p(4)=-50$. Option B is $P(4)$; option E drops the sign of $k$.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "poly"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J1-13", "paper": "J1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>A circular sheet of paper of radius $3$ is cut along two radii into two sectors. Each sector is then rolled up, without overlapping, to form the curved surface of a cone, so that the two straight edges of the sector are joined and become a slant height of the cone. The height of one cone turns out to be exactly twice the height of the other. Find \\[\\frac{\\text{volume of the taller cone}}{\\text{volume of the shorter cone}} .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$\\dfrac{11-3\\sqrt{13}}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{13}-3}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{11-3\\sqrt{13}}{8}$", "$11+3\\sqrt{13}$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Both cones have slant height $3$. The arcs of the two sectors add up to the circumference $6\\pi$, and each arc becomes the circumference of a base, so the base radii satisfy $2\\pi r_1+2\\pi r_2=6\\pi$, that is $r_1+r_2=3$. The heights are $\\sqrt{9-r_1^2}$ and $\\sqrt{9-r_2^2}$. If the first cone is the taller, then $9-r_1^2=4\\left(9-(3-r_1)^2\\right)$, which simplifies to $r_1^2-8r_1+3=0$, so $r_1=4-\\sqrt{13}\\approx0.39$ and $r_2=\\sqrt{13}-1$. The volume ratio is \\[\\frac{r_1^2h_1}{r_2^2h_2}=2\\left(\\frac{4-\\sqrt{13}}{\\sqrt{13}-1}\\right)^2=2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}-3}{4}\\right)^2=\\frac{11-3\\sqrt{13}}{4}\\approx0.046 .\\] The taller cone is the narrow one, and it holds less than a twentieth of the other's volume. Option A takes the heights as the whole story; option E is the reciprocal.<\/p>", "tags": ["quad"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J1-14", "paper": "J1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A farmer has exactly $12$ metres of fencing, which she uses to enclose a pen in the shape of a triangle. One of the angles of the triangle must be $120^\\circ$. Find the greatest possible area of the pen, in square metres.<\/p>", "opts": ["$4\\sqrt3$", "$36\\left(7-4\\sqrt3\\right)$", "$252\\sqrt3-432$", "$126\\sqrt3-216$", "$3\\sqrt3$", "$144\\left(2-\\sqrt3\\right)^2$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let the sides enclosing the $120^\\circ$ angle be $a$ and $b$, with $s=a+b$ and $p=ab$. The third side is $c=12-s$, and the cosine rule gives $c^2=a^2+b^2+ab=s^2-p$. So $(12-s)^2=s^2-p$, that is $p=24s-144$. The area is $\\frac12ab\\sin120^\\circ=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}p$, so we want $p$ as large as possible. But real $a$ and $b$ with sum $s$ need $p\\le\\frac{s^2}{4}$, so $24s-144\\le\\frac{s^2}4$, which means $s^2-96s+576\\ge0$, that is $s\\le48-24\\sqrt3$ (the other range makes $c$ negative). Since $p$ increases with $s$, the best is $s=48-24\\sqrt3$, where $a=b$ and $p=\\frac{s^2}{4}=144\\left(2-\\sqrt3\\right)^2$. The area is $\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\cdot144\\left(7-4\\sqrt3\\right)=252\\sqrt3-432\\approx4.48$. Option A is the equilateral triangle, which is not allowed; option F is the value of $ab$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq", "quad"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J1-15", "paper": "J1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>How many real solutions does the equation \\[2\\,|\\sin x|=\\left|\\,\\frac{|x|}{\\pi}-2\\,\\right|\\]<\/p><p>have? (Here $x$ is measured in radians.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$7$", "$12$", "$13$", "$15$", "$16$", "$14$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Both sides are even functions of $x$, and $x=0$ is not a solution, so count the positive solutions and double. For $x>0$ the right-hand side is $\\left|\\frac x\\pi-2\\right|$: a V with its vertex at $(2\\pi,0)$, equal to $2$ at $x=0$ and $x=4\\pi$, and above $2$ beyond $4\\pi$, where there are no solutions. The left-hand side is a row of arches of height $2$, one on each interval $[k\\pi,(k+1)\\pi]$. On $[0,\\pi]$ the V falls from $2$ to $1$ and crosses the first arch twice. On $[\\pi,2\\pi]$ it falls from $1$ to $0$: one crossing as the arch rises, and then the graphs meet again at $x=2\\pi$, where both are $0$. Just either side of $2\\pi$ the arch is steeper than the V, so this is a single touching point, not a crossing. On $[2\\pi,3\\pi]$ the V rises from $0$ to $1$ and crosses once as the arch comes down, and on $[3\\pi,4\\pi]$, rising from $1$ to $2$, it crosses twice. That makes $2+2+1+2=7$ positive solutions and $14$ in all. Option B misses the touching points at $\\pm2\\pi$; option E counts each of them twice.<\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "root", "ineq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J1-16", "paper": "J1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>A rectangle is drawn with one side on the $x$-axis and its other two vertices on the curve \\[y=3-\\left|x^2-1\\right| ,\\]<\/p><p>in such a way that the whole rectangle lies in the region enclosed by the curve and the $x$-axis. Find the greatest possible area of such a rectangle.<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$\\dfrac{32\\sqrt3}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{3}$", "$2\\sqrt6$", "$4\\sqrt2$", "$3\\sqrt3$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>For $|x|\\le1$ the curve is $y=2+x^2$, and for $1\\le|x|\\le2$ it is $y=4-x^2$: an M shape with peaks of height $3$ at $x=\\pm1$, a dip to $2$ at $x=0$, and feet at $x=\\pm2$. If the top of the rectangle is at height $h$, the whole top edge must stay under the curve. When $h>2$ the region at height $h$ splits into two pieces, one under each peak, each narrower than $\\sqrt2$, so these rectangles have area less than $3\\sqrt2$. When $h\\le2$ the top edge can run from $-\\sqrt{4-h}$ to $\\sqrt{4-h}$, giving area $A=2t(4-t^2)$ with $t=\\sqrt{4-h}\\ge\\sqrt2$. Here $A'(t)=8-6t^2&lt;0$, so the best is $t=\\sqrt2$: area $2\\sqrt2\\cdot2=4\\sqrt2\\approx5.66$. Maximising $2t(4-t^2)$ without the constraint gives $t=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}$ and $\\frac{32\\sqrt3}{9}$, option B, but that rectangle has height $\\frac83$ and pokes through the dip at $x=0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "ineq"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "J1-17", "paper": "J1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>The function $F$ is defined for all real $x$ by \\[F(x)=\\int_0^x (t-1)\\,|t-3|\\;dt .\\]<\/p><p>The graph of $y=F(x)$ and the $x$-axis enclose exactly one finite region. Find its area.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac94$", "$\\dfrac43$", "$\\dfrac34$", "$\\dfrac{27}{4}$", "$\\dfrac92$", "$3$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>For $x\\le3$ the modulus is $3-t$ throughout the range of integration, and this holds for negative $x$ too, so \\[F(x)=\\int_0^x(-t^2+4t-3)\\,dt=-\\tfrac{x^3}3+2x^2-3x=-\\tfrac x3(x-3)^2 .\\] This is positive for $x&lt;0$, negative for $0&lt;x&lt;3$, and zero at $0$ and $3$. For $x>3$, $F(x)=F(3)+\\int_3^x(t-1)(t-3)\\,dt$, and the integrand is positive there, so $F>0$. The region therefore lies below the axis between $0$ and $3$, and its area is \\[\\int_0^3\\frac x3(x-3)^2\\,dx=\\frac13\\left[\\frac{x^4}4-2x^3+\\frac92x^2\\right]_0^3=\\frac13\\cdot\\frac{27}{4}=\\frac94 .\\] The value $F(3)=0$ is the surprise: the curve returns to the axis exactly at the zero of the modulus, crossing it there with a horizontal tangent. Option B is the depth of the region, $-F(1)=\\frac43$.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "arint"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "J1-18", "paper": "J1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Find the area of the region of the plane consisting of all the points $(x,y)$ that satisfy both \\[|x|+|y|\\le3\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y\\ge\\left|x^2-1\\right| .\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{11}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{17\\sqrt{17}-63}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{17\\sqrt{17}-41}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{17\\sqrt{17}-41}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{17\\sqrt{17}-25}{6}$", "$5$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Since $y\\ge|x^2-1|\\ge0$, the first condition is just $y\\le3-|x|$, and by symmetry we can work with $x\\ge0$ and double. For $0\\le x\\le1$ the lower boundary is $1-x^2$, which stays below $3-x$, since the gap $x^2-x+2$ is always positive. For $x\\ge1$ it is $x^2-1$, which reaches $3-x$ where $x^2+x-4=0$, at $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}-1}{2}$. So the area is \\[2\\left[\\int_0^1(2-x+x^2)\\,dx+\\int_1^c(4-x-x^2)\\,dx\\right].\\] The first integral is $\\frac{11}{6}$. For the second, use $c^2=4-c$ and $c^3=5c-4$ to get $\\left[4x-\\frac{x^2}{2}-\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_1^c=\\frac{17c}{6}-\\frac23-\\frac{19}{6}=\\frac{17\\sqrt{17}-63}{12}$. The total is $2\\left(\\frac{11}{6}+\\frac{17\\sqrt{17}-63}{12}\\right)=\\frac{17\\sqrt{17}-41}{6}\\approx4.85$. Reducing powers of $c$ with its own equation keeps the surds under control. Option E drops the modulus and uses $x^2-1$ all the way across.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "ineq"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "J1-19", "paper": "J1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Find the area of the region of the plane that lies inside the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ and above the parabola $y=x^2-2$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+\\sqrt3$", "$\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}+\\sqrt3$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+3\\sqrt3$", "$2\\pi+2\\sqrt3$", "$\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}-3\\sqrt3$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+2\\sqrt3$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Substituting $x^2=y+2$ into the circle gives $y^2+y-2=0$, so $y=1$ or $y=-2$. The value $y=-2$ gives only $x=0$: the parabola touches the circle at its lowest point. The value $y=1$ gives $x=\\pm\\sqrt3$. Between these the parabola lies inside the circle and the region runs from the parabola up to the upper semicircle, while for $\\sqrt3&lt;|x|\\le2$ the parabola is above the circle and there is nothing. So the area is \\[\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{\\sqrt3}\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\,dx-\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{\\sqrt3}(x^2-2)\\,dx .\\] The first integral is an area under an arc of the circle: the sector between the radii to $\\left(\\pm\\sqrt3,1\\right)$, of angle $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ and area $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$, plus two right-angled triangles with legs $\\sqrt3$ and $1$, of total area $\\sqrt3$. The second integral is $2\\left(\\sqrt3-2\\sqrt3\\right)=-2\\sqrt3$. The area is $\\frac{4\\pi}3+\\sqrt3+2\\sqrt3=\\frac{4\\pi}{3}+3\\sqrt3$. No antiderivative of $\\sqrt{4-x^2}$ is needed; reading it as an area of the circle is the whole technique. Option A leaves out the parabola's contribution, and option E is the rest of the disc.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "circ"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "J1-20", "paper": "J1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>For a real constant $k$, consider the equation \\[\\left|\\,x^2-6|x|+5\\,\\right|=x+k .\\]<\/p><p>Find the sum of all the values of $k$ for which this equation has exactly three real solutions.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{29}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{15}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{45}{4}$", "$\\dfrac54$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Let $g(x)=\\left|x^2-6|x|+5\\right|=\\bigl|(|x|-1)(|x|-5)\\bigr|$. Its graph is even: it touches the axis at $x=\\pm1$ and $\\pm5$, has arches of height $4$ over $[1,5]$ and $[-5,-1]$ with peaks at $x=\\pm3$, a peak of $5$ at $x=0$, and grows like $x^2$ outside $[-5,5]$. Since $g$ is above the line far out on both sides, a line of gradient $1$ usually meets the graph an even number of times; an odd count needs a point where the two meet without crossing: a tangency to an arch, or a corner of the graph (the four zeros and the peak at the origin). Tangency to the right arch $y=-x^2+6x-5$ needs $-2x+6=1$, at $\\left(\\frac52,\\frac{15}{4}\\right)$, so $k=\\frac54$; tangency to the left arch happens at $\\left(-\\frac72,\\frac{15}4\\right)$, so $k=\\frac{29}{4}$. The lines through $(1,0)$, $(5,0)$, $(-1,0)$ and $(-5,0)$ have $k=-1,-5,1,5$, and the line through $(0,5)$ also has $k=5$. Now count. $k=\\frac{29}4$: the tangency plus one crossing on each side, $3$. $k=\\frac54$: the tangency plus four crossings, $5$. $k=-1$: $x=1$, $4$ and $6$, so $3$. $k=-5$: only $x=5$. $k=1$: five solutions. $k=5$: four, namely $-5$, $-2$, $0$ and $7$. So exactly three solutions occur for $k=\\frac{29}{4}$ and $k=-1$, with sum $\\frac{25}{4}$. Option A misses the corner case $k=-1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["root", "ineq"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "J2-1", "paper": "J2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>An argument is <em>valid<\/em> if its conclusion must be true whenever all of its premises are true. In each argument below, $n$ stands for an unknown positive integer. Which of the arguments are valid? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>If $n$ is divisible by $6$, then $n$ is divisible by $3$. The number $n$ is not divisible by $3$. Therefore $n$ is not divisible by $6$.<\/li><li>If $n$ is divisible by $6$, then $n$ is divisible by $3$. The number $n$ is divisible by $3$. Therefore $n$ is divisible by $6$.<\/li><li>Either $n$ is even or $n^2$ is odd. The number $n^2$ is even. Therefore $n$ is even.<\/li><li>No perfect square leaves remainder $2$ on division by $4$. The number $n$ leaves remainder $2$ on division by $4$. Therefore $n$ is not a perfect square.<\/li><li>If $n$ is a prime greater than $2$, then $n$ is odd. If $n$ is odd, then $n^2+1$ is even. The number $n^2+1$ is even. Therefore $n$ is a prime greater than $2$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and IV only", "I, III and IV only", "I, II and IV only", "I, III, IV and V only", "III and IV only", "all five"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I denies the conclusion of an implication and so denies its hypothesis: valid. II runs the implication backwards and is invalid, since $n=3$ makes both premises true and the conclusion false. III: the second premise rules out &ldquo;$n^2$ is odd&rdquo;, so the first premise leaves only &ldquo;$n$ is even&rdquo;: valid. IV is valid directly. V has the same flaw as II: the two implications chain to &ldquo;if $n$ is a prime greater than $2$ then $n^2+1$ is even&rdquo;, and knowing the conclusion says nothing about the hypothesis; $n=9$ makes all three premises true. So I, III and IV. Validity is about whether the conclusion follows, not about whether the premises are true: in III the first premise happens to be true for every integer, but that is not what makes the argument valid.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "prf"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "J2-2", "paper": "J2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>At a sixth-form college, each student takes some of the four modules Pure, Mechanics, Statistics and Decision. The following facts are known.<br> (1) A student takes Mechanics only if they take Pure.<br> (2) Nobody takes both Statistics and Mechanics unless they also take Decision.<br> (3) Every student who takes Decision also takes Statistics.<br> (4) Aisha takes Mechanics but not Decision.<br><\/p><p>Here &ldquo;$A$ unless $B$&rdquo; means &ldquo;if $B$ is false, then $A$&rdquo;. Which of the following can be deduced from these facts? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>Aisha takes Pure.<\/li><li>Aisha does not take Statistics.<\/li><li>Every student who takes both Mechanics and Decision also takes both Pure and Statistics.<\/li><li>Every student who takes Decision takes Pure.<\/li><li>No student takes all four modules.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and V only", "II and III only", "I, III and IV only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Translate each fact. (1) says Mechanics implies Pure. (2) says: if a student does not take Decision, then they do not take both Statistics and Mechanics; equivalently, Statistics and Mechanics together force Decision. (3) says Decision implies Statistics. I follows from (1). II: Aisha takes Mechanics and not Decision, so if she took Statistics, (2) would force Decision; hence she does not. III: Mechanics gives Pure by (1), and Decision gives Statistics by (3). IV does not follow: a student taking only Decision and Statistics is consistent with every fact. V does not follow either: a student taking all four modules satisfies (1), (2) and (3), and (4) is only about Aisha. So I, II and III.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "J2-3", "paper": "J2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>In this question a <em>geometric sequence<\/em> is one of the form $a,\\ ar,\\ ar^2,\\dots$ for real numbers $a$ and $r$. How many of the following statements about sequences of real numbers are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>If a sequence is both arithmetic and geometric, then it is constant.<\/li><li>If every term of a geometric sequence is positive, then the logarithms of its terms form an arithmetic sequence.<\/li><li>If $u_1,u_2,u_3,\\dots$ is arithmetic, then $2^{u_1},2^{u_2},2^{u_3},\\dots$ is geometric.<\/li><li>If $u_1,u_2,\\dots$ and $v_1,v_2,\\dots$ are both geometric, then $u_1+v_1,\\ u_2+v_2,\\dots$ is geometric.<\/li><li>If $u_1,u_2,\\dots$ is arithmetic and $u_1^2,u_2^2,\\dots$ is also arithmetic, then $u_1,u_2,\\dots$ is constant.<\/li><li>If the sum of the first $n$ terms is $n^2+n+1$ for every $n\\ge1$, then the sequence is arithmetic.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I: if $a$, $ar$, $ar^2$ are in arithmetic progression then $2ar=a+ar^2$, so $a(r-1)^2=0$: either every term is $0$ or $r=1$, and the sequence is constant either way. II: $\\log\\left(ar^{n-1}\\right)=\\log a+(n-1)\\log r$. III: $2^{a+(n-1)d}=2^a\\left(2^d\\right)^{n-1}$. IV fails: $1,2,4$ plus $1,3,9$ gives $2,5,13$. V: with common difference $d$, $u_{n+1}^2-u_n^2=d(2u_n+d)$, and this increases by $2d^2$ from each $n$ to the next, so it is constant only if $d=0$. VI fails: $u_1=S_1=3$, while $u_n=S_n-S_{n-1}=2n$ for $n\\ge2$, giving $3,4,6,8,\\dots$; the formula $2n$ breaks at $n=1$ because $S_0$ is $0$, not $1$. Four statements are true.<\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "log"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "J2-4", "paper": "J2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A student attempts to prove the following claim.<\/p><p><em>Claim:<\/em> if $f$ is a polynomial with $\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx=0$, then $f(1)=0$.<br> (1) The substitution $u=2-x$ shows that $\\int_0^2 f(2-x)\\,dx=\\int_0^2 f(u)\\,du$.<br> (2) Hence $\\int_0^2\\bigl(f(x)+f(2-x)\\bigr)dx=2\\int_0^2f(x)\\,dx=0$.<br> (3) Let $h(x)=f(x)+f(2-x)$. Then $h(2-x)=h(x)$ for every $x$, so the graph of $h$ is symmetric about the line $x=1$.<br> (4) Since $h$ is continuous and $\\int_0^2h(x)\\,dx=0$, there is some $c$ in $[0,2]$ with $h(c)=0$.<br> (5) By the symmetry in (3), also $h(2-c)=0$; so the zeros of $h$ in $[0,2]$ come in pairs $c$ and $2-c$, except that $x=1$ is paired with itself.<br> (6) A continuous function whose integral over an interval is $0$ has an odd number of zeros in that interval, so $x=1$ must be a zero of $h$.<br> (7) Therefore $2f(1)=h(1)=0$, and $f(1)=0$.<br><\/p><p>Which one of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The first mistake is in line (1), and the claim is false.", "The first mistake is in line (3), and the claim is false.", "The first mistake is in line (4), and the claim is true.", "The first mistake is in line (5), and the claim is false.", "The first mistake is in line (6), and the claim is true.", "The first mistake is in line (6), and the claim is false.", "The argument is valid, and the claim is true."], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Lines (1) to (5) are all correct: the substitution maps $[0,2]$ onto itself, a continuous function with zero integral must vanish somewhere, and the symmetry pairs each zero $c$ with $2-c$. Line (6) is false. Zero integral forces at least one zero, but nothing forces the number of zeros to be odd: $h(x)=(x-1)^2-\\frac13$ has zero integral over $[0,2]$ and exactly two zeros, $1\\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$. The claim is false as well: $f(x)=x^2-\\frac43$ has $\\int_0^2f=\\frac83-\\frac83=0$ but $f(1)=-\\frac13$. What the argument really establishes is that $h$ has a zero, and that zeros away from $x=1$ come in pairs.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "fint", "sym"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "J2-5", "paper": "J2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>$\\mathcal F$ is a collection of functions, each defined for all real numbers. Consider the statement<\/p><p>&ldquo;Every function in $\\mathcal F$ that is increasing is bounded above, unless it is a polynomial.&rdquo;<\/p><p>Here &ldquo;$A$ unless $B$&rdquo; means &ldquo;if $B$ is false, then $A$&rdquo;. Which one of the following is the negation of the statement?<\/p>", "opts": ["There is a function in $\\mathcal F$ that is increasing, is not a polynomial, and is not bounded above.", "There is a function in $\\mathcal F$ that is increasing, is a polynomial, and is not bounded above.", "Every function in $\\mathcal F$ that is increasing and not bounded above is a polynomial.", "There is a function in $\\mathcal F$ that is not increasing, is not a polynomial, and is bounded above.", "Every function in $\\mathcal F$ that is increasing is not bounded above, unless it is a polynomial.", "There is a function in $\\mathcal F$ that is increasing and not bounded above, and every such function is a polynomial.", "No function in $\\mathcal F$ is increasing."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Unpack the statement for a single function $f$ in $\\mathcal F$: if $f$ is increasing, then $f$ is bounded above unless $f$ is a polynomial. That is: if $f$ is increasing and $f$ is not a polynomial, then $f$ is bounded above. A universal implication is false exactly when some case has the hypothesis true and the conclusion false: some $f$ that is increasing and not a polynomial, but not bounded above. That is option A. Option C is not a negation at all: it is the contrapositive of the original, and says the same thing. Option B negates the wrong part of the &ldquo;unless&rdquo;, and option E negates only the conclusion.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "J2-6", "paper": "J2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Consider the following statement about polynomials $f$ and $g$.<\/p><p><em>If $f(x)\\le g(x)$ for every $x$ with $0\\le x\\le1$, then $\\int_0^1\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)^2dx\\le\\int_0^1\\bigl(g(x)\\bigr)^2dx$.<\/em><\/p><p>How many of the following pairs are counterexamples to the statement? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"i\"><li>$f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=1$<\/li><li>$f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=0$<\/li><li>$f(x)=x-1$ and $g(x)=x$<\/li><li>$f(x)=2x-2$ and $g(x)=x$<\/li><li>$f(x)=x^2$ and $g(x)=x$<\/li><li>$f(x)=-x$ and $g(x)=x^2$<\/li><li>$f(x)=x-1$ and $g(x)=1-x$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$", "$7$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>A counterexample needs $f\\le g$ on $[0,1]$ and $\\int f^2>\\int g^2$. Squaring preserves $f\\le g$ only when $f\\ge0$, so any counterexample must use negative values of $f$. All seven pairs satisfy the hypothesis. (i) $\\frac13\\le1$. (ii) $1>0$: counterexample. (iii) $\\frac13\\le\\frac13$. (iv) $\\int_0^1(2x-2)^2\\,dx=\\frac43>\\frac13$: counterexample. (v) $\\frac15\\le\\frac13$. (vi) $\\frac13>\\frac15$: counterexample. (vii) both integrals are $\\frac13$. Three counterexamples. Pairs (iii) and (vii) are the ones to be careful with: $f$ is negative, but $|f|$ is no larger than $|g|$ on average, and equality still satisfies the conclusion.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "fint"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "J2-7", "paper": "J2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Consider the following statement, in which $f$ is a polynomial.<\/p><p><em>If a straight line meets the curve $y=f(x)$ at exactly one point, then the line is the tangent to the curve at that point.<\/em><\/p><p>Which of the following are counterexamples to the statement? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>The curve $y=x^2$ and the line $x=1$.<\/li><li>The curve $y=x^3$ and the line $y=0$.<\/li><li>The curve $y=x^3$ and the line $y=-x$.<\/li><li>The curve $y=x^2$ and the line $y=2x-1$.<\/li><li>The curve $y=x^3-3x$ and the line $y=9x+16$.<\/li><li>The curve $y=x^4+x$ and the line $y=x$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "III only", "I, III and V only", "III and V only", "I and III only", "I, III and VI only", "none of them"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>A counterexample needs a line that meets the curve exactly once and is not the tangent there. I: the vertical line $x=1$ meets $y=x^2$ only at $(1,1)$, but the tangent there has gradient $2$: a counterexample. II: $y=0$ meets $y=x^3$ only at the origin, and is the tangent there. III: $x^3=-x$ only at $x=0$, where the curve has gradient $0$ and the line gradient $-1$: a counterexample. IV: the tangent at $(1,1)$, meeting the curve once. V: $x^3-12x-16=(x-4)(x+2)^2$, so the line is the tangent at $x=-2$ but meets the curve again at $x=4$; the hypothesis fails. VI: $x^4+x=x$ only at $x=0$, where the curve's gradient is $1$, the same as the line's: it is the tangent. So I and III. Meeting exactly once is how tangents to circles behave; for other curves neither property implies the other.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "diff"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "J2-8", "paper": "J2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>For a polynomial $f$, consider the three conditions<br> (1) $f(1)=0$;<br> (2) $\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx=0$;<br> (3) $f(1+t)=-f(1-t)$ for every real $t$.<br><\/p><p>Which one of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["(1) alone is sufficient for (2).", "(2) alone is sufficient for (1).", "(1) and (2) together are necessary and sufficient for (3).", "(1) and (2) together are sufficient but not necessary for (3).", "(3) is sufficient for (1) but not for (2).", "(1) and (2) together are necessary but not sufficient for (3)."], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Putting $t=0$ in (3) gives $f(1)=-f(1)$, so (1) holds. Condition (3) says the graph has half-turn symmetry about $(1,0)$, so the substitution $x\\mapsto2-x$ gives $\\int_1^2f=-\\int_0^1f$, which is (2). So (3) implies both, and (1) and (2) together are necessary for (3). They are not sufficient: $f(x)=(x-1)^4-\\frac35(x-1)^2$ has $f(1)=0$ and $\\int_0^2f=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(u^4-\\frac35u^2\\right)du=\\frac25-\\frac25=0$, yet its graph is symmetric about $x=1$ rather than about the point $(1,0)$. Option A fails for $f(x)=(x-1)^2$, and option B for $f(x)=x^2-\\frac43$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "fint", "sym"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "J2-9", "paper": "J2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>Three arguments are offered for the following claim, in which $g(x)=x^3+x-1$.<\/p><p><em>Claim:<\/em> the equation $g(x)=0$ has exactly one real solution. <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$g(0)=-1&lt;0$ and $g(1)=1>0$, so $g$ has a root between $0$ and $1$. Also $g'(x)=3x^2+1>0$ for every $x$, so $g$ is increasing and cannot have a second root.<\/li><li>The equation says $x^3=1-x$. The graph of $y=x^3$ is increasing and the graph of $y=1-x$ is decreasing, so they meet at most once. They do meet, because at $x=0$ the first graph is below the second and at $x=1$ it is above.<\/li><li>$g(0)&lt;0&lt;g(1)$ gives a root $r$ with $0&lt;r&lt;1$. If there were a second root $s$, then $r^3+r=s^3+s$, so $(r-s)(r^2+rs+s^2+1)=0$, and since $r\\neq s$ this gives $r^2+rs+s^2=-1$. But $r$ and $s$ are both positive, being roots between $0$ and $1$, so $r^2+rs+s^2>0$: a contradiction.<\/li><\/ol><\/p><p>Which of these arguments are complete and correct proofs of the claim?<\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I only", "II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III", "none of them"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>I is complete: the sign change gives a root in $(0,1)$, and a function with positive derivative everywhere is increasing, so it cannot vanish twice. II is complete for the same reasons, phrased with graphs: an increasing function and a decreasing function agree at most once, and the comparison at $0$ and $1$ shows that they agree somewhere. III has a gap. The root $r$ was found in $(0,1)$, but nothing says that a second root $s$ would be there too, so &ldquo;$r$ and $s$ are both positive&rdquo; is unjustified. The step can be repaired, since $r^2+rs+s^2=\\left(r+\\frac s2\\right)^2+\\frac34s^2\\ge0$ for all real $r$ and $s$, but as written the argument assumes something it has not shown.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "root"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "J2-10", "paper": "J2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A student attempts to prove the following claim.<\/p><p><em>Claim:<\/em> $x^4-4x+3\\ge0$ for every real number $x$.<br> (1) If $x\\le0$, then $-4x\\ge0$, so $x^4-4x+3\\ge3>0$.<br> (2) If $0&lt;x\\le\\frac34$, then $3-4x\\ge0$, so $x^4-4x+3>0$.<br> (3) If $x\\ge2$, then $x^3\\ge8$, so $x^4\\ge8x$ and $x^4-4x+3\\ge4x+3>0$.<br> (4) If $\\frac34&lt;x&lt;2$, then $x^4-4x+3$ is a continuous function of $x$ that is positive at $x=\\frac34$ and at $x=2$, so it is positive for every $x$ in between.<br> (5) Hence $x^4-4x+3\\ge0$ for every real number $x$.<br><\/p><p>Which one of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The first mistake is in line (1), and the claim is false.", "The first mistake is in line (2), and the claim is true.", "The first mistake is in line (3), and the claim is true.", "The first mistake is in line (4), and the claim is true.", "The first mistake is in line (4), and the claim is false.", "The first mistake is in line (5), and the claim is true.", "The argument is valid, and the claim is true."], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Lines (1) to (3) are correct; in (3), $x\\ge2$ gives $x^3\\ge8$ and so $x^4\\ge8x$. Line (4) is the first mistake: a continuous function that is positive at both ends of an interval can dip below zero in between, and its values at two points say nothing about the middle. Here the expression actually equals $0$ at $x=1$, so &ldquo;positive for every $x$ in between&rdquo; is false as stated. The claim itself is true: $x^4-4x+3=(x-1)^2\\left(x^2+2x+3\\right)$, and $x^2+2x+3=(x+1)^2+2>0$. The conclusion is right, but the argument has not earned it.<\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "ineq"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "J2-11", "paper": "J2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>The series $a+ar+ar^2+ar^3+\\cdots$, whose $n$th term is $ar^{n-1}$, has real first term $a$ and real common ratio $r$. The condition \\[|r|&lt;1\\]<\/p><p>is <span class=\"blank\">________<\/span>\\ for &ldquo;the series converges and its sum is $2a$&rdquo;.<\/p>", "opts": ["necessary but not sufficient", "sufficient but not necessary", "necessary and sufficient", "neither necessary nor sufficient"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>If $a\\neq0$, the series converges exactly when $|r|&lt;1$, with sum $\\frac{a}{1-r}$, and this equals $2a$ exactly when $r=\\frac12$. So $|r|&lt;1$ is not sufficient: $a=1$, $r=0$ gives sum $1$, not $2$. It is not necessary either, because of the case $a=0$: then every term is $0$, and the series converges to $0=2a$ for every $r$, including $r=5$. So the condition is neither necessary nor sufficient. The instinct that &ldquo;$|r|&lt;1$ is needed for convergence&rdquo; fails only when $a=0$, and one case is all it takes.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "ser"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J2-12", "paper": "J2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>For each real number $k$, let $f_k(x)=x^2-kx$. How many of the following statements are true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"i\"><li>For every real $k$ there is a real $x$ with $f_k(x)&lt;0$.<\/li><li>There is a real $x$ such that $f_k(x)\\le0$ for every real $k$.<\/li><li>For every real $x$ there is a real $k$ with $f_k(x)&lt;0$.<\/li><li>There is a real $k$ such that $f_k(x)\\ge-1$ for every real $x$.<\/li><li>For every real $k$ there is a real $x$ with $f_k(x)=f_k(x+1)$.<\/li><li>There is a real $x$ such that $f_k(x)\\ge f_k(x+1)$ for every real $k$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>(i) is false: for $k=0$, $f_0(x)=x^2$ is never negative. (ii) is true with $x=0$, where every $f_k$ vanishes. (iii) is false for the same reason: at $x=0$ no $k$ makes $f_k(0)&lt;0$. (iv) is true: the least value of $f_k$ is $-\\frac{k^2}{4}$, which is at least $-1$ whenever $|k|\\le2$. (v) is true: $f_k(x+1)-f_k(x)=2x+1-k$, which is zero at $x=\\frac{k-1}{2}$. (vi) is false: $f_k(x)\\ge f_k(x+1)$ means $k\\ge2x+1$, and no single $x$ makes this hold for every $k$. Three statements are true. Notice that (i) and (iii) fail for different reasons, one at a single value of $k$ and the other at a single value of $x$.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "quad"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J2-13", "paper": "J2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Let $a$ and $b$ be positive real numbers, with $a\\neq1$. Which one of the following conditions is necessary and sufficient for $\\log_a b>1$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$b>a$", "$b>a>1$", "$(b-1)(a-1)>0$", "$(b-a)(b-1)>0$", "$b>a$ or $b&lt;1$", "$(b-a)(a-1)>0$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Write $\\log_ab=\\frac{\\ln b}{\\ln a}$. Then $\\log_ab>1$ says $\\frac{\\ln b-\\ln a}{\\ln a}>0$: the numbers $\\ln b-\\ln a$ and $\\ln a$ have the same sign. Since $\\ln$ is increasing, $\\ln b-\\ln a$ has the sign of $b-a$, and $\\ln a$ has the sign of $a-1$. So the condition is exactly $(b-a)(a-1)>0$. In words: $b>a$ when the base exceeds $1$, but $b&lt;a$ when the base lies between $0$ and $1$. Option A ignores the second case, since $a=\\frac12$, $b=\\frac14$ gives $\\log_ab=2$; option C is the condition for $\\log_ab>0$; option E fails for $a=\\frac12$, $b=\\frac34$, where $\\log_ab\\approx0.42$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "exp"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J2-14", "paper": "J2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>Let $f$ be a polynomial. Which of the following conditions are <em>sufficient<\/em> for $\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx>0$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(0)>0$ and $f(1)>0$.<\/li><li>$f$ has degree at most $1$, and $f\\left(\\frac12\\right)>0$.<\/li><li>$f(x)>-1$ for every $x$ in $[0,1]$, and $f(x)>1$ for every $x$ in $\\left[\\frac14,\\frac34\\right]$.<\/li><li>$f$ is increasing on $[0,1]$, and $f\\left(\\frac12\\right)>0$.<\/li><li>$f(x)>0$ for every $x$ in $(0,1)$ except $x=\\frac12$, and $f\\left(\\frac12\\right)=0$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["II only", "II and III only", "II and V only", "II, IV and V only", "III and V only", "I, II, III and V only", "II, III and V only"], "ans": "G", "sol": "<p>II: for a linear $f$ the integral over $[0,1]$ equals $f\\left(\\frac12\\right)$, the average of the end values. III: split $[0,1]$ into the middle half and the two outer quarters. On the middle half $f>1$, contributing more than $\\frac12$; on the outer quarters $f>-1$, contributing more than $-\\frac12$; the total is positive. V: $f$ is continuous and positive on $(0,1)$ apart from one point, so its integral is positive. I fails: $f(x)=1-10x(1-x)$ has $f(0)=f(1)=1$ but integral $1-\\frac{10}{6}&lt;0$. IV fails: $f(x)=2-32(1-x)^5$ is increasing, with $f\\left(\\frac12\\right)=1>0$, but its integral is $2-\\frac{32}{6}&lt;0$. An increasing function can be very negative on a long stretch and only just positive at the midpoint. So II, III and V.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "fint"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J2-15", "paper": "J2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Which of the following conditions on real numbers $x$ and $y$ are equivalent to $|x|+|y|\\le1$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$|x+y|\\le1$ and $|x-y|\\le1$<\/li><li>$|x|\\le1$ and $|y|\\le1$<\/li><li>$x^2+y^2\\le1$<\/li><li>$|x+y|+|x-y|\\le2$<\/li><li>$\\bigl(|x|+|y|\\bigr)^3\\le|x|+|y|$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and V only", "I, IV and V only", "II and IV only", "I, III and V only", "V only"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The key fact is $\\max\\bigl(|x+y|,|x-y|\\bigr)=|x|+|y|$: if $x$ and $y$ have the same sign then $|x+y|=|x|+|y|$, and otherwise $|x-y|=|x|+|y|$, and in each case the other expression is no larger. So I is equivalent to $|x|+|y|\\le1$. V is too: for $t=|x|+|y|\\ge0$, the inequality $t^3\\le t$ holds exactly when $t\\le1$. IV is not: $|x+y|+|x-y|=2\\max(|x|,|y|)$, so IV says $|x|\\le1$ and $|y|\\le1$, which is II, and $x=y=1$ satisfies it. III is not: $x=y=0.6$ satisfies $x^2+y^2\\le1$ but has $|x|+|y|=1.2$. So I and V. Geometrically, I describes the square bounded by the four lines $x\\pm y=\\pm1$, which is the diamond itself.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "ineq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J2-16", "paper": "J2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Let $f$ be a polynomial that is not constant, and let $a$ be a real number. Say that $f$ has a local maximum at $a$ if $f(x)\\le f(a)$ for every $x$ in some interval around $a$, and similarly for a local minimum. Consider the conditions<br> $P$: $f'(a)=0$;<br> $Q$: $f$ has a local maximum or a local minimum at $a$;<br> $R$: $f'(a)=0$ and $f''(a)\\neq0$;<br> $S$: $f'(x)$ has one sign for all $x$ just less than $a$, and the opposite sign for all $x$ just greater than $a$.<br><\/p><p>Which one of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["$P$, $Q$ and $S$ are equivalent, and $R$ implies each of them.", "$R$ and $Q$ are equivalent, and each of them implies $P$.", "$S$ implies $R$, and $R$ implies $P$.", "$P$ implies $Q$, and $Q$ implies $S$.", "$Q$ and $S$ are equivalent and each implies $P$; $R$ implies $Q$; but $P$ does not imply $Q$, and $Q$ does not imply $R$.", "$Q$ implies $S$, but $S$ does not imply $Q$."], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>For a non-constant polynomial, $f'$ has only finitely many zeros, so near $a$ it keeps one sign on each side. If $f'$ changes sign at $a$, then $f$ rises and then falls, or falls and then rises, so there is a local maximum or minimum; if it does not change sign, $f$ is increasing or decreasing through $a$, and there is neither. So $Q$ and $S$ are equivalent. Both imply $P$, since a polynomial that changes sign at $a$ vanishes there. $R$ implies $Q$ by the second derivative test. The implications stop there: $f(x)=x^3$ at $a=0$ satisfies $P$ but not $Q$, and $f(x)=x^4$ at $a=0$ satisfies $Q$ but not $R$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "diff"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J2-17", "paper": "J2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Call a function $f$, defined for every real number, a <em>crossing function<\/em> if for every real number $c$ there are real numbers $a$ and $b$ with $a&lt;b$ and $f(a)&lt;c&lt;f(b)$. How many of the following are crossing functions? \\[x^3-3x,\\qquad 1-x,\\qquad x^2,\\qquad x+\\sin x,\\qquad x\\sin x,\\qquad \\frac{x}{1+|x|}\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>For $x^3-3x$, $x+\\sin x$ and $x\\sin x$, the values go below any given $c$ at some point and above it at a later point. $x^3-3x$ tends to $-\\infty$ on the left and $+\\infty$ on the right. $x+\\sin x$ lies between $x-1$ and $x+1$, so it does the same. $x\\sin x$ is more interesting: it keeps returning to $0$, but at $x=2\\pi n+\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ it equals $-x$, and at $x=2\\pi n+\\frac{\\pi}2$ it equals $x$. So for any $c$ we can take $a$ of the first kind with $f(a)&lt;c$, and then $b>a$ of the second kind with $f(b)>c$. The others fail. $1-x$ is decreasing, so $a&lt;b$ forces $f(a)>f(b)$. $x^2$ fails at $c=-1$, since nothing lies below it. $\\frac{x}{1+|x|}$ stays between $-1$ and $1$, so it fails at $c=2$, even though it is increasing. Three crossing functions.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "J2-18", "paper": "J2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>For a real number $x$, consider the three statements<br> $P$: $x^2&lt;4$ only if $x>1$.<br> $Q$: $x>1$ unless $x&lt;-2$.<br> $R$: $x^2>1$ if $x&lt;0$.<br><\/p><p>Here &ldquo;$A$ only if $B$&rdquo; means &ldquo;if $A$ then $B$&rdquo;, and &ldquo;$A$ unless $B$&rdquo; means &ldquo;if $B$ is false, then $A$&rdquo;. For which values of $x$ are exactly two of the statements $P$, $Q$ and $R$ true?<\/p>", "opts": ["no value of $x$", "$x=-2$ only", "$x\\le-2$", "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$ only", "$-2\\le x&lt;-1$", "$x&lt;-2$ or $1&lt;x&lt;2$", "$-1&lt;x&lt;0$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Translate each statement into a condition on $x$. $P$ is &ldquo;if $x^2&lt;4$ then $x>1$&rdquo;, which is true when $x^2\\ge4$ or $x>1$: that is, $x\\le-2$ or $x>1$. $Q$ is &ldquo;if $x\\ge-2$ then $x>1$&rdquo;, true when $x&lt;-2$ or $x>1$. $R$ is &ldquo;if $x&lt;0$ then $x^2>1$&rdquo;, true when $x\\ge0$ or $x&lt;-1$. Now count region by region. For $x&lt;-2$ all three hold; at $x=-2$ exactly $P$ and $R$ hold; for $-2&lt;x&lt;-1$ only $R$; for $-1\\le x&lt;0$ none; for $0\\le x\\le1$ only $R$; for $x>1$ all three. So exactly two are true only at $x=-2$. The single point comes from the boundary: at $x=-2$ the hypothesis of $P$ fails, since $4&lt;4$ is false, so $P$ is true; while $Q$ needs $x&lt;-2$ or $x>1$ and just misses.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ineq"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "J2-19", "paper": "J2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>For a real number $k$, consider the three statements<br> (1) for every $x$ with $0\\le x\\le1$ there is a $y$ with $0\\le y\\le1$ such that $x^2+y^2=k$;<br> (2) there is an $x$ with $0\\le x\\le1$ such that $x^2+y^2\\le k$ for every $y$ with $0\\le y\\le1$;<br> (3) for every $x$ with $0\\le x\\le1$ there is a $y$ with $0\\le y\\le1$ such that $x^2+y^2\\ge k$.<br><\/p><p>Let $\\mathcal A$, $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ be the sets of real numbers $k$ for which statements (1), (2) and (3), respectively, are true. Which one of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\mathcal A=\\{1\\}$, $\\mathcal B=\\{k:k\\ge1\\}$, $\\mathcal C=\\{k:k\\le1\\}$", "$\\mathcal A=\\{k:1\\le k\\le2\\}$, $\\mathcal B=\\{k:k\\ge1\\}$, $\\mathcal C=\\{k:k\\le1\\}$", "$\\mathcal A=\\{1\\}$, $\\mathcal B=\\{k:k\\ge2\\}$, $\\mathcal C=\\{k:k\\le2\\}$", "$\\mathcal A=\\{k:0\\le k\\le2\\}$, $\\mathcal B=\\{k:k\\ge1\\}$, $\\mathcal C=\\{k:k\\le1\\}$", "$\\mathcal A=\\{1\\}$, $\\mathcal B=\\{k:k\\ge1\\}$, $\\mathcal C=\\{k:k\\le2\\}$", "$\\mathcal A$ is empty, $\\mathcal B=\\{k:k\\ge2\\}$, $\\mathcal C=\\{k:k\\le1\\}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>(1): for every $x$ in $[0,1]$ we need $k-x^2$ to lie in $[0,1]$. Taking $x=1$ forces $k\\ge1$, and taking $x=0$ forces $k\\le1$; conversely $k=1$ works, with $y=\\sqrt{1-x^2}$. So $\\mathcal A=\\{1\\}$. (2): some single $x$ must give $x^2+y^2\\le k$ for all $y$, the worst $y$ being $1$, so we need $x^2+1\\le k$ for some $x$; the best choice is $x=0$, and $\\mathcal B$ is the set of $k\\ge1$. (3): for each $x$ we may choose $y=1$, so we need $x^2+1\\ge k$ for every $x$; the worst case is $x=0$, and $\\mathcal C$ is the set of $k\\le1$. The distractors come from changing the quantifiers: &ldquo;some $x$ and some $y$ with $x^2+y^2=k$&rdquo; gives $0\\le k\\le2$, and &ldquo;every $x$ and every $y$ with $x^2+y^2\\le k$&rdquo; gives $k\\ge2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ineq"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "J2-20", "paper": "J2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>For which of the following sets of conditions does there exist a polynomial $f$ satisfying every condition in the set? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(0)=0$, $f(1)=1$, and $\\int_0^1f(x)\\,dx=0$.<\/li><li>$f(0)=f(1)=0$, $f(x)\\ge0$ for $0\\le x\\le1$, and $\\int_0^1f(x)\\,dx=1$.<\/li><li>$f(0)=0$, $f'(x)\\le1$ for $0\\le x\\le1$, and $\\int_0^1f(x)\\,dx=\\frac12$.<\/li><li>$f(0)=0$, $f(1)=1$, $f'(x)\\ge0$ for $0\\le x\\le1$, and $\\int_0^1f(x)\\,dx=\\frac1{100}$.<\/li><li>$f(0)=0$, $f(1)=1$, $f'(x)\\ge0$ for $0\\le x\\le1$, and $\\int_0^1f(x)\\,dx=1$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I, II and IV only", "II and IV only", "I, II, IV and V only", "I, IV and V only", "all five", "I, II, III and IV only"], "ans": "G", "sol": "<p>I: $f(x)=3x^2-2x$ works, with integral $1-1=0$. II: $f(x)=6x(1-x)$ works. III: $f'\\le1$ and $f(0)=0$ give $f(x)\\le x$ on $[0,1]$, so $\\int_0^1f\\le\\frac12$, with equality only if $f(x)=x$ throughout; and $f(x)=x$ does satisfy all three conditions, with $f'=1\\le1$. So III is possible, but only just. IV: $f(x)=x^{99}$ works, with integral $\\frac1{100}$. V is impossible: $f$ is increasing with $f(1)=1$, so $f\\le1$ on $[0,1]$, and $f$ is close to $0$ near $x=0$, so the integral is less than $1$. So I, II, III and IV. The trap is in III: an inequality that bounds the integral can still be attained.<\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "diff", "log"], "rating": 8.2}];
/* Paper difficulty on the same 4.5 to 9 scale: the average of the mean question rating
   and the mean of the five hardest questions, so a harder final stretch counts for more. */
window.HU_PAPER_RATINGS=(function(){ var by={}, out={};
  window.HU_QUESTIONS.forEach(function(q){ (by[q.paper]=by[q.paper]||[]).push(q.rating); });
  Object.keys(by).forEach(function(p){ var r=by[p].slice().sort(function(a,b){return a-b;}), mean=r.reduce(function(s,x){return s+x;},0)/r.length,
    top=r.slice(-5).reduce(function(s,x){return s+x;},0)/5; out[p]={rating:Math.round((mean+top)/2*10)/10,mean:Math.round(mean*10)/10,top5:Math.round(top*10)/10,max:r[r.length-1]}; });
  return out; })();
